/* ---------- Science (bilingual pairs) ---------- */
const SCIENCE = [
  { id: "science:001", zh: "光在真空中每秒行进约 299,792 公里。你此刻看到的阳光，是 8 分 20 秒前从太阳出发的。", en: "Light travels at about 299,792 kilometres per second in a vacuum. The sunlight you see now left the Sun 8 minutes and 20 seconds ago." },
  { id: "science:002", zh: "月光到达地球只需约 1.3 秒。所以你看到的月亮，是 1.3 秒前的月亮。", en: "Moonlight takes only about 1.3 seconds to reach Earth, so the Moon you see is the Moon of 1.3 seconds ago." },
  { id: "science:003", zh: "人体由大约 37 万亿个细胞组成，而住在你身体里和体表的微生物约有 38 万亿个，和你的细胞数大致相当。", en: "Your body is made of roughly 37 trillion cells, and the microbes living in and on you number about 38 trillion, about the same order of magnitude." },
  { id: "science:004", zh: "一个细胞里的 DNA 展开约有 2 米长。把全身的 DNA 首尾相连，长度相当于地球到太阳往返数十次。", en: "The DNA in a single cell stretches about 2 metres. Joined end to end, all your DNA would reach to the Sun and back dozens of times." },
  { id: "science:005", zh: "人的心脏一生大约跳动 25 亿次，从不停工，也没有备件。", en: "A human heart beats about 2.5 billion times in a lifetime, without a pause and without spare parts." },
  { id: "science:006", zh: "大脑运行只需约 20 瓦功率，和一只昏暗的灯泡差不多，却运行着你的全部思维、记忆和情绪。", en: "The brain runs on about 20 watts, roughly a dim light bulb, yet it powers all your thoughts, memories and feelings." },
  { id: "science:007", zh: "最快的神经信号在身体里以约每秒 120 米的速度传递，慢的只有每秒 1 米左右。所以踩到尖东西时，痛感总是迟到一点点。", en: "The fastest nerve signals travel at about 120 metres per second; the slowest manage barely 1 metre per second. That is why pain from a stubbed toe arrives a fraction late." },
  { id: "science:008", zh: "成年人有 206 块骨头，婴儿出生时却有约 300 块，其中许多会在成长中融合成一块。", en: "Adults have 206 bones, but babies are born with around 300; many fuse together as they grow." },
  { id: "science:009", zh: "皮肤是人体最大的器官，成年人的皮肤总面积约 2 平方米，重约 3.6 公斤。", en: "Skin is the body's largest organ: an adult's skin covers about 2 square metres and weighs roughly 3.6 kilograms." },
  { id: "science:010", zh: "一个红细胞在身体里跑完一整圈循环，平均只需要大约一分钟。", en: "A red blood cell takes only about a minute, on average, to complete a full circuit of your body." },
  { id: "science:011", zh: "人每天眨眼约 15,000 到 20,000 次。加起来，相当于每天有近半小时，你是闭着眼度过的。", en: "You blink roughly 15,000 to 20,000 times a day, which adds up to nearly half an hour spent with your eyes shut." },
  { id: "science:012", zh: "早上刚起床时，你比晚上高约 1 到 2 厘米：脊柱的椎间盘白天被体重压扁，夜里平躺后又慢慢恢复。", en: "You are about 1 to 2 centimetres taller in the morning than at night: the discs in your spine compress by day and recover as you lie flat." },
  { id: "science:013", zh: "蜂鸟每秒可以扇动翅膀约 80 次，心跳每分钟可以超过 1,200 次，是哺乳动物里代谢最疯狂的引擎之一。", en: "A hummingbird can beat its wings about 80 times per second, and its heart can race at over 1,200 beats per minute." },
  { id: "science:014", zh: "章鱼有三颗心脏，血液是蓝色的，因为它用含铜的血蓝蛋白而不是含铁的血红蛋白来运送氧气。", en: "Octopuses have three hearts and blue blood, because they carry oxygen with copper-based haemocyanin instead of iron-based haemoglobin." },
  { id: "science:015", zh: "野生长颈鹿每天的睡眠常常不足 2 小时，而且大多是站着打的几分钟小盹。", en: "Wild giraffes often sleep less than two hours a day, mostly in short naps taken standing up." },
  { id: "science:016", zh: "大象能用脚感知地面传来的低频震动，隔着几公里和同伴“说话”。它们的脚，某种意义上是耳朵。", en: "Elephants can feel low-frequency rumbles through the ground with their feet, 'talking' to each other across kilometres. Their feet are, in a sense, ears." },
  { id: "science:017", zh: "一只蜜蜂忙碌一生，大约只能酿出十二分之一茶匙的蜂蜜。一罐蜂蜜背后，是成千上万只蜜蜂的一生。", en: "A single bee produces only about one-twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in its whole life. Behind one jar stand thousands of lifetimes." },
  { id: "science:018", zh: "鲨鱼在地球上已经存在了约 4.5 亿年，比最早的树木还要早几千万年。树长出来之前，鲨鱼已经游了很久了。", en: "Sharks have existed for about 450 million years, tens of millions of years before the first trees. Sharks were already ancient when forests began." },
  { id: "science:019", zh: "真菌与动物的亲缘关系，比与植物更近。你和一朵蘑菇的共同祖先，比蘑菇和一棵树的共同祖先更晚近。", en: "Fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants. You share a more recent common ancestor with a mushroom than a mushroom shares with a tree." },
  { id: "science:020", zh: "人类约有 60% 的基因，能在香蕉里找到对应版本。生命用的是同一套零件库，只是组装方式不同。", en: "About 60 percent of human genes have a recognisable counterpart in a banana. Life uses one shared parts catalogue, assembled differently." },
  { id: "science:021", zh: "从植物学上说，草莓并不是真正的浆果，香蕉反而是。分类学经常和日常语言唱反调。", en: "Botanically, a strawberry is not a true berry, but a banana is. Taxonomy often disagrees with everyday language." },
  { id: "science:022", zh: "马里亚纳海沟的挑战者深渊深约 10,900 米，比珠穆朗玛峰的高度还要深。把珠峰沉进去，峰顶离海面还差 2 公里多。", en: "The Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench lies about 10,900 metres down, deeper than Everest is tall. Drop Everest in and its peak would still be 2 kilometres underwater." },
  { id: "science:023", zh: "在赤道上，地球自转的线速度约每小时 1,670 公里。你坐着不动，其实一直在以这个速度向东飞驰。", en: "At the equator, Earth's spin carries you eastward at about 1,670 kilometres per hour. Sitting still, you are still flying." },
  { id: "science:024", zh: "地球绕太阳公转的速度约为每秒 30 公里。读完这句话的几秒钟里，你已经在太空里移动了上百公里。", en: "Earth orbits the Sun at about 30 kilometres per second. In the few seconds it took to read this, you travelled hundreds of kilometres through space." },
  { id: "science:025", zh: "太阳大得可以装下大约 130 万个地球。", en: "About 1.3 million Earths would fit inside the Sun." },
  { id: "science:026", zh: "一茶匙中子星物质重约数十亿吨。中子星是恒星死亡后被引力压到极致的残骸。", en: "A teaspoon of neutron star would weigh roughly a billion tonnes. A neutron star is a dead star crushed by gravity to the very limit." },
  { id: "science:027", zh: "银河系中心藏着一个名为人马座 A* 的黑洞，质量约是太阳的 400 万倍。我们整个太阳系，正绕着它缓慢旋转。", en: "At the centre of the Milky Way sits Sagittarius A*, a black hole about 4 million times the mass of the Sun. Our whole solar system slowly orbits it." },
  { id: "science:028", zh: "可观测宇宙中估计至少有约 2,000 亿个星系，每个星系里又有成百上千亿颗恒星。", en: "The observable universe holds an estimated 200 billion galaxies at least, each with hundreds of billions of stars." },
  { id: "science:029", zh: "宇宙的年龄约为 138 亿年。你身体里的大部分氢原子，就是在那最初的时刻形成的。", en: "The universe is about 13.8 billion years old. Most of the hydrogen atoms in your body were forged in its very first moments." },
  { id: "science:030", zh: "闪电的温度可达约 30,000 开尔文，大约是太阳表面温度的 5 倍。一瞬间，空气被加热到爆炸般膨胀，那就是雷声。", en: "Lightning can reach about 30,000 kelvin, roughly five times hotter than the Sun's surface. The explosive expansion of that heated air is thunder." },
  { id: "science:031", zh: "水在 4 摄氏度时密度最大。所以湖水从表面开始结冰，冰浮在上面像一床被子，让水下的生命熬过冬天。", en: "Water is densest at 4 degrees Celsius, so lakes freeze from the top down. The floating ice acts as a blanket, letting life below survive the winter." },
  { id: "science:032", zh: "老教堂玻璃窗下厚上薄，并不是因为玻璃像液体一样流了几百年，而是当年吹制玻璃的工艺本身就不均匀。", en: "Old church windows are thicker at the bottom not because glass flows like a liquid over centuries, but because early glassmaking was uneven." },
  { id: "science:033", zh: "钻石和铅笔芯都是纯碳。同样是碳原子，排列成四面体就是钻石，排列成层片就是石墨。结构即命运。", en: "Diamonds and pencil lead are both pure carbon. Arranged in tetrahedra, carbon becomes diamond; arranged in sheets, graphite. Structure is destiny." },
  { id: "science:034", zh: "人体会发出微弱的可见光，是新陈代谢的副产品，只是强度比肉眼能察觉的极限弱约 1,000 倍。你在发光，只是没人看得见。", en: "The human body glows faintly in visible light, a by-product of metabolism, but about 1,000 times dimmer than the eye can detect. You are shining; no one can see it." },
  { id: "science:035", zh: "即使是同卵双胞胎，指纹也不相同。指纹在子宫里形成时，还受到羊水流动等微小随机因素的影响。", en: "Even identical twins have different fingerprints. They form in the womb under tiny random influences, such as the flow of amniotic fluid." },
  { id: "science:036", zh: "任意两个人的 DNA 约有 99.9% 相同。人与人之间全部的差异，只写在那 0.1% 里。", en: "Any two humans share about 99.9 percent of their DNA. Every difference between us is written in the remaining 0.1 percent." },
  { id: "science:037", zh: "人类与黑猩猩的 DNA 相似度约为 98.8%。分家的时间，大约在 600 万年前。", en: "Humans and chimpanzees share about 98.8 percent of their DNA. The two lineages parted roughly 6 million years ago." },
  { id: "science:038", zh: "你细胞里的线粒体，来自约 20 亿年前被远古细胞吞下的一种细菌。它只通过母亲传给你。", en: "Your mitochondria descend from bacteria swallowed by an ancient cell about 2 billion years ago, and you inherit them only from your mother." },
  { id: "science:039", zh: "已知最古老的一棵单体树，是一棵狐尾松，年龄约 5,000 岁。它发芽的时候，金字塔还没建完。", en: "The oldest known individual tree, a bristlecone pine, is about 5,000 years old. It sprouted before the pyramids were finished." },
  { id: "science:040", zh: "蓝鲸的心脏重约 180 公斤，主动脉粗到可以让一个小孩爬过去。", en: "A blue whale's heart weighs about 180 kilograms, and its aorta is wide enough for a small child to crawl through." },
  { id: "science:041", zh: "考拉的指纹与人类极其相似，在显微镜下都难以分辨，甚至可能干扰犯罪现场的指纹鉴定。", en: "Koala fingerprints are so similar to human ones that they are hard to tell apart under a microscope, and could in principle contaminate a crime scene." },
  { id: "science:042", zh: "在海马的世界里，怀孕生子的是雄性。雌海马把卵放进雄性的育儿袋，接下来的一切由父亲完成。", en: "Among seahorses, it is the male who becomes pregnant. The female places her eggs in his brood pouch, and he does the rest." },
  { id: "science:043", zh: "灯塔水母在受伤或衰老时，可以把自己的细胞退回幼体状态重新来过，因此被称作“不死水母”。", en: "The jellyfish Turritopsis dohrnii can revert its cells to a juvenile stage when injured or old, earning it the name 'immortal jellyfish'." },
  { id: "science:044", zh: "蚂蚁能搬动约自身体重 50 倍的重物。按比例放大到人类，相当于一个人举起一辆小汽车。", en: "An ant can carry about 50 times its own body weight. Scaled to human size, that would be like lifting a small car." },
  { id: "science:045", zh: "北极熊的毛并不是白色的，每根毛都是透明中空的，看起来白，是因为光线在其中反复散射。", en: "Polar bear fur is not white. Each hair is a translucent hollow tube; it looks white because of how light scatters inside it." },
  { id: "science:046", zh: "变色龙变色不是靠换颜料，而是调节皮肤里纳米晶体的间距，改变反射光的颜色。", en: "Chameleons change colour not by swapping pigments but by adjusting the spacing of nanocrystals in their skin, changing the light they reflect." },
  { id: "science:047", zh: "猫头鹰的眼球是管状的，无法在眼窝里转动。作为补偿，它的脖子能转约 270 度。", en: "An owl's eyes are tube-shaped and cannot move in their sockets. To compensate, its neck can rotate about 270 degrees." },
  { id: "science:048", zh: "金鱼的记忆可以保持数月甚至更久，“金鱼只有 7 秒记忆”是个流传很广的谣言。", en: "A goldfish's memory lasts for months. The famous 'seven-second memory' is a myth." },
  { id: "science:049", zh: "睡眠时，大脑的类淋巴系统会加速清除白天积累的代谢废物。熬夜，等于跳过一次大脑的夜间保洁。", en: "During sleep, the brain's glymphatic system speeds up clearing the day's metabolic waste. Skipping sleep means skipping the night cleaning crew." },
  { id: "science:050", zh: "据估计，人一生大约有 6 年时间在做梦。只是绝大多数梦，在醒来几分钟内就被忘了。", en: "By some estimates, we spend roughly six years of our lives dreaming, though most dreams are forgotten within minutes of waking." },
  { id: "science:051", zh: "并不存在“左脑型”或“右脑型”的人。脑成像显示，两个半球始终在协同工作。", en: "There is no such thing as a 'left-brained' or 'right-brained' person. Brain imaging shows both hemispheres working together all the time." },
  { id: "science:052", zh: "“我们只用了 10% 的大脑”是个谣言。一天之内，成像技术能在几乎所有脑区都观测到活动。", en: "The idea that we use only 10 percent of our brains is a myth. Over the course of a day, imaging detects activity in virtually every region." },
  { id: "science:053", zh: "舌头上的“味觉地图”是谣言：舌头的每个区域都能尝到甜、酸、咸、苦和鲜，只是敏感度略有差别。", en: "The tongue map is a myth: every region of the tongue can taste sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami, with only slight differences in sensitivity." },
  { id: "science:054", zh: "拿破仑并不矮。他的身高约 168 厘米，在当时的法国属于平均水平。“小个子”印象来自英制与法制单位的混淆和英国漫画的抹黑。", en: "Napoleon was not short: at about 168 centimetres he was average for a Frenchman of his time. The 'little corporal' myth came from confused units and British caricatures." },
  { id: "science:055", zh: "胡夫金字塔建成时，地球上还有猛犸象活着。最后一批猛犸象在弗兰格尔岛上活到了约公元前 1700 年。", en: "When the Great Pyramid of Giza was built, mammoths still walked the Earth. The last of them survived on Wrangel Island until about 1700 BC." },
  { id: "science:056", zh: "克利奥帕特拉生活的年代，离今天约 2,000 年，离金字塔建成约 2,500 年。也就是说，她离 iPhone 比离金字塔更近。", en: "Cleopatra lived about 2,000 years before us, but some 2,500 years after the pyramids were built. She lived closer in time to the iPhone than to the Great Pyramid." },
  { id: "science:057", zh: "牛津大学比阿兹特克帝国还要古老：牛津在 1096 年就已开始授课，而阿兹特克的首都特诺奇蒂特兰建于 1325 年。", en: "Oxford University is older than the Aztec Empire: teaching at Oxford began in 1096, while the Aztec capital Tenochtitlan was founded in 1325." },
  { id: "science:058", zh: "任天堂创立于 1889 年，那时中国还处在清朝光绪年间。它最早是卖手工绘制的花札纸牌的。", en: "Nintendo was founded in 1889, during China's Qing dynasty. It began by selling hand-painted hanafuda playing cards." },
  { id: "science:059", zh: "土星的平均密度约 0.69 克每立方厘米，比水还小。如果有一个足够大的海洋，土星会浮在水面上。", en: "Saturn's average density is about 0.69 grams per cubic centimetre, less than water. In a large enough ocean, Saturn would float." },
  { id: "science:060", zh: "金星上的一天比一年还长：它自转一周约需 243 个地球日，绕太阳公转一周却只需约 225 个地球日。", en: "A single day on Venus, one full rotation, takes about 243 Earth days, while its year takes only about 225. On Venus, a day outlasts a year." },
  { id: "science:061", zh: "在火星上，日落是蓝色的。火星稀薄的大气散射光线的方式，恰好和地球相反。", en: "On Mars, sunsets glow blue. Its thin atmosphere scatters light in almost the opposite way to Earth's." },
  { id: "science:062", zh: "木星的大红斑是一场比地球还宽的风暴，已经刮了至少几百年。", en: "Jupiter's Great Red Spot is a storm wider than the entire Earth, and it has been raging for centuries." },
  { id: "science:063", zh: "月球正在以每年约 3.8 厘米的速度远离地球。阿波罗宇航员留在月面上的激光反射镜，至今还在帮我们量这个距离。", en: "The Moon is drifting away from Earth at about 3.8 centimetres per year, measured with laser reflectors the Apollo astronauts left on its surface." },
  { id: "science:064", zh: "地球不是一个完美的球：赤道半径比极半径大约 21 公里，因为自转把它“甩”得微微鼓起。", en: "Earth is not a perfect sphere. Its equatorial radius is about 21 kilometres larger than its polar radius, because its spin makes it bulge." },
  { id: "science:065", zh: "地核的温度约为 6,000 摄氏度，几乎和太阳表面一样热。你脚下几千公里深处，藏着一个太阳表面。", en: "Earth's core is about 6,000 degrees Celsius, roughly as hot as the surface of the Sun. A piece of the Sun's surface sits thousands of kilometres beneath your feet." },
  { id: "science:066", zh: "海水中溶解着约 2,000 万吨黄金，只是浓度太低，捞出来的成本远比金子本身贵。", en: "The oceans hold about 20 million tonnes of dissolved gold, but it is so dilute that recovering it would cost far more than the gold is worth." },
  { id: "science:067", zh: "南极冰盖储存了地球上约六成的淡水。如果它全部融化，全球海平面将上升约 58 米。", en: "Antarctica's ice sheet stores roughly 60 percent of the world's fresh water. If it all melted, sea levels would rise by about 58 metres." },
  { id: "science:068", zh: "珠穆朗玛峰每年大约长高 4 毫米，因为印度板块还在持续挤压欧亚板块。世界最高峰，仍在长个子。", en: "Mount Everest grows about 4 millimetres taller each year, as the Indian plate keeps pushing into the Eurasian plate." },
  { id: "science:069", zh: "死海的盐度约 34%，接近普通海水的 10 倍。在那里游泳，人会不由自主地浮在水面上。", en: "The Dead Sea is about 34 percent salt, nearly ten times saltier than the ocean. Swimmers there float whether they want to or not." },
  { id: "science:070", zh: "声音在空气中每秒约走 340 米，而光速约是声速的 90 万倍。所以闪电总是先被看见，雷声随后才到。", en: "Sound travels through air at about 340 metres per second, while light moves roughly 900,000 times faster. That is why you always see the lightning before you hear the thunder." },
  { id: "science:071", zh: "天空是蓝色的，是因为空气分子对阳光的散射很挑颜色：波长短的蓝光被散射得最厉害，红光则大多直穿而过。日落时阳光穿过更厚的空气，蓝光散尽，天边便只剩下红和橙。", en: "The sky is blue because air molecules scatter short blue wavelengths of sunlight far more strongly than red ones, which mostly pass straight through. At sunset, sunlight crosses even more air, the blue is scattered away, and only reds and oranges remain." },
  { id: "science:072", zh: "彩虹是雨滴的杰作：阳光钻进雨滴时被折射、反射、再折射，像穿过一颗小棱镜，被拆成一条色带。其实彩虹是一个完整的圆，只是下半截被地面挡住了——在飞机上有时能看到圆形的彩虹。", en: "A rainbow is made by raindrops: sunlight entering a drop is bent, reflected and bent again, like light through a tiny prism, and split into a band of colours. A rainbow is really a full circle; the ground just hides the lower half, though from an aircraft you can sometimes see the whole ring." },
  { id: "science:073", zh: "潮汐主要来自月球的引力：靠月一侧的海水被拉向月球，背月一侧的海水则因惯性鼓起，地球自转带着海岸轮流穿过这两个水包，于是大多数地方每天涨潮两次。太阳的引潮力只有月球的约一半。", en: "Tides come mainly from the Moon's gravity: the ocean bulges toward the Moon on the near side and, through inertia, on the far side too. As Earth rotates, coasts pass through both bulges, so most places get two high tides a day. The Sun's tidal pull is only about half the Moon's." },
  { id: "science:074", zh: "四季的成因是地轴约 23.5 度的倾斜，而不是地球离太阳的远近。恰恰相反，地球离太阳最近是在一月初——那时正是北半球的隆冬。", en: "The seasons are caused by the roughly 23.5-degree tilt of Earth's axis, not by our distance from the Sun. In fact, Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, in the depths of the northern winter." },
  { id: "science:075", zh: "镜子其实并不左右颠倒，它颠倒的是前后：镜中人举起的“左手”，是你右手沿垂直于镜面的方向翻过去的结果。我们觉得它左右互换，只是因为习惯把镜中人想象成转过身来的自己。", en: "A mirror does not actually swap left and right; it swaps front and back. The 'left hand' your reflection raises is your right hand flipped along the direction facing the mirror. It feels like a left-right swap only because we imagine the reflection as ourselves, turned around." },
  { id: "science:076", zh: "声音撞到墙壁和山崖会反射回来，这就是回声。要把回声和原声分清，障碍物至少要在约 17 米外：声音往返一趟约需 0.1 秒，短于这个间隔，大脑就会把两个声音合成一个。", en: "Sound bouncing off walls and cliffs comes back as an echo. To hear it as separate from the original, the surface must be at least about 17 metres away: the round trip takes roughly a tenth of a second, and anything quicker merges into a single sound in the brain." },
  { id: "science:077", zh: "冬天脱毛衣时的噼啪声，是静电在放电：摩擦让电子从一种材料跑到另一种，积累的电压可达几千伏，最后击穿空气跳出火花。好在电量极小，只吓人一跳，并不真正伤人。", en: "The crackle when you pull off a sweater in winter is static discharge: friction moves electrons from one material to another until the charge reaches thousands of volts and jumps across the air as a spark. The total charge is tiny, so it startles rather than harms." },
  { id: "science:078", zh: "冰能浮在水上，是因为水冻成冰时，水分子排成疏松的六角形网格，体积反而增大约 9%，密度比液态水小。绝大多数物质恰恰相反：它们的固体比液体更密，放进自己的液体里会沉底。", en: "Ice floats because, as water freezes, its molecules lock into an open hexagonal lattice that takes up about 9 percent more room, making ice less dense than liquid water. Most substances do the opposite: their solid form is denser and sinks in its own melt." },
  { id: "science:079", zh: "想估算雷电有多远，看见闪电后开始数秒：声音每秒约走 340 米，数出的秒数除以 3，差不多就是公里数。这个办法不精确，但足以判断那片雷雨云是在靠近还是远离。", en: "To estimate how far away a storm is, count the seconds between the lightning flash and the thunder: sound covers about 340 metres a second, so dividing the count by three gives roughly the distance in kilometres. It is not exact, but it tells you whether the storm is coming or going." },
  { id: "science:080", zh: "今天大气中约五分之一是氧气，这是光合生物用几十亿年一点一点攒下来的。其中海洋浮游植物个头微小，据估计却贡献了全球约一半的光合产氧。", en: "About a fifth of today's atmosphere is oxygen, accumulated grain by grain by photosynthetic life over billions of years. Tiny ocean phytoplankton, by some estimates, produce around half of all the oxygen photosynthesis releases." },
  { id: "science:081", zh: "有些竹子是世界上长得最快的植物：条件适宜时，一天能长高近 1 米。秘诀在于竹笋的每一节都自带生长带，出土后各节同时伸长，像一截截天线节节拔高。", en: "Some bamboos are the fastest-growing plants on Earth, rising nearly a metre in a single day when conditions are right. The trick is that every segment of the shoot carries its own growth zone, so all of them lengthen at once, like a telescope extending." },
  { id: "science:082", zh: "莲子是出了名的长寿种子。在中国辽宁出土的古莲子，年代约在上千年以上，经科学家处理后，有的真的生根、发芽、开花——沉睡千年，醒来仍是夏天。", en: "Lotus seeds are famously long-lived. Ancient lotus seeds unearthed in Liaoning, China, dated to over a thousand years old, have been coaxed by scientists into sprouting, growing and flowering: asleep for a millennium, awake in time for summer." },
  { id: "science:083", zh: "泰坦魔芋开花时散发腐肉般的臭气，吸引逐臭的昆虫替它传粉。它的花序可高达约 3 米，是世界上最大的不分枝花序；但开一次花要积攒数年能量，花期只有一两天。", en: "When the titan arum blooms, it reeks of rotting flesh to lure pollinating insects. Its flower spike can reach about 3 metres, the largest unbranched inflorescence in the world, yet a bloom takes years of stored energy and lasts only a day or two." },
  { id: "science:084", zh: "仙人掌把茎长成了储水罐：表皮有厚蜡层锁水，叶子退化成刺以减少蒸发，连气孔都改在凉爽的夜间才开放。它还把根系在地表下铺得很广，一场雨来，能抢收的水一滴不漏。", en: "A cactus turns its stem into a water tank: a thick waxy skin locks moisture in, the leaves have shrunk to spines to cut evaporation, and even its pores open only at night. Its roots spread wide just under the surface, so a single rainstorm is harvested to the last drop." },
  { id: "science:085", zh: "向日葵只有年轻时才追着太阳转。成熟的花盘大多固定朝向东方：朝东的花盘更暖和，能吸引更多传粉昆虫，结出的种子也更饱满。", en: "Only young sunflowers track the Sun. A mature flower head mostly stays facing east: an east-facing head is warmer, draws more pollinating insects, and produces plumper seeds." },
  { id: "science:086", zh: "挪威斯瓦尔巴的全球种子库埋在北极冻土里，替全人类备份了上百万份种子。而已知成功发芽的最古老种子，是一颗约 2,000 年前的枣椰种子，它在以色列出土后，长成了一棵树。", en: "The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, buried in Arctic permafrost in Norway, backs up over a million seed samples for humanity. The oldest seed known to have germinated is a date palm seed about 2,000 years old, excavated in Israel, which grew into a tree." },
  { id: "science:087", zh: "抗生素只对细菌有效，对病毒毫无作用。而普通感冒大多由病毒引起，吃抗生素治不好感冒，只会误伤肠道里的正常菌群，还可能助长耐药菌。", en: "Antibiotics work only against bacteria and do nothing to viruses. Since most common colds are caused by viruses, antibiotics cannot cure them; they only harm helpful gut bacteria and can encourage resistant strains." },
  { id: "science:088", zh: "疫苗的原理，是让免疫系统先“演习”一遍：把灭活、减毒的病原体，或它的一个片段，提前介绍给身体，免疫系统会留下专门的记忆细胞。真正的病原体日后入侵时，它能抢先一步，在发病之前就把对方清除。", en: "A vaccine works by letting the immune system rehearse: an inactivated or weakened pathogen, or just a fragment of it, is introduced in advance, and the immune system keeps specialised memory cells. If the real pathogen arrives later, the response is fast enough to clear it before illness takes hold." },
  { id: "science:089", zh: "耐药性是被抗生素“筛选”出来的：用药时，偶然带有耐药基因的细菌活了下来，繁衍成群，药就渐渐不管用了。所以抗生素的滥用和误用，等于全人类一起替细菌做陪练。", en: "Drug resistance is selected by antibiotics themselves: the few bacteria that happen to carry resistance genes survive treatment and multiply, until the drug stops working. Every misuse of antibiotics is, in effect, training bacteria on humanity's behalf." },
  { id: "science:090", zh: "病毒没有细胞，也不会自己复制，必须钻进宿主细胞，借用人家的复制机器替自己繁衍。它到底算不算“活物”，科学界至今没有共识：它恰好站在生命与非生命的边界上。", en: "A virus has no cells and cannot copy itself; it must slip into a host cell and borrow that cell's machinery to reproduce. Whether it counts as alive is still unsettled in science: it sits exactly on the border between life and non-life." },
  { id: "science:091", zh: "人体最大的细胞是卵细胞，直径约 0.1 毫米，是极少数能被肉眼直接看见的细胞。而男性的精子全长只有约 0.05 毫米，其中大半还是尾巴。", en: "The largest human cell is the egg cell, about 0.1 millimetres across — one of the very few cells visible to the naked eye. A sperm cell, by contrast, is only about 0.05 millimetres long, and most of that is tail." },
  { id: "science:092", zh: "输血最怕红细胞的抗原对不上。O 型红细胞没有 A、B 抗原，紧急时可少量输给其他血型的人；AB 型的人则对 A、B 抗原都不排斥。不过现代临床以同型输血为原则，“万能供血”只是应急之策。", en: "Blood transfusion fails when the red-cell antigens clash. Type O red cells carry neither A nor B antigens, so in an emergency small amounts can be given to people of other types, while type AB recipients tolerate both. Modern practice, though, is to match types; 'universal donor' is an emergency fallback." },
  { id: "science:093", zh: "发烧不是病，而是免疫系统主动调高的体温：大脑把“设定温度”上调之后，较高的体温能抑制许多病原体的繁殖，也让免疫细胞更活跃。适度发烧，多半是身体在认真打仗。", en: "A fever is not an illness but a setting the immune system deliberately raises: the brain turns up the body's thermostat, and the extra heat slows many pathogens down while immune cells work faster. A moderate fever usually means the body is fighting in earnest." },
  { id: "science:094", zh: "约 6,600 万年前，一颗直径约 10 公里的小行星撞上今天墨西哥的尤卡坦半岛，留下一个宽约 180 公里的陨石坑。这场撞击终结了非鸟恐龙长达一亿多年的时代。", en: "About 66 million years ago, an asteroid roughly 10 kilometres wide slammed into what is now Mexico's Yucatán Peninsula, leaving a crater some 180 kilometres across. The impact ended the reign of the non-avian dinosaurs, which had lasted well over a hundred million years." },
  { id: "science:095", zh: "从演化上说，鸟类就是恐龙：它们是兽脚类恐龙的一支，是那场大灭绝中唯一幸存的恐龙血脉。所以恐龙并没有完全消失——窗外飞过的麻雀，就是活生生的恐龙。", en: "Evolutionarily speaking, birds are dinosaurs: they are a branch of the theropod dinosaurs, the one lineage that survived the great extinction. Dinosaurs never entirely vanished — the sparrow outside your window is one." },
  { id: "science:096", zh: "霸王龙生活在约 6,800 万年前，剑龙生活在约 1.5 亿年前。算一算：霸王龙离我们的时间距离，比它离剑龙的还要近。恐龙时代之长，人类直觉很难装下。", en: "Tyrannosaurus rex lived about 68 million years ago; Stegosaurus about 150 million years ago. Do the arithmetic: T. rex is closer in time to us than it is to Stegosaurus. The dinosaur age lasted longer than intuition can hold." },
  { id: "science:097", zh: "大西洋正在变宽：海底的大洋中脊不断生出新洋壳，把美洲与欧洲、非洲以每年约 2.5 厘米的速度推开——差不多就是你指甲生长的速度。", en: "The Atlantic Ocean is getting wider: new crust forming at the mid-ocean ridge pushes the Americas away from Europe and Africa at about 2.5 centimetres a year — roughly the speed your fingernails grow." },
  { id: "science:098", zh: "今天燃烧的煤，大多来自约 3 亿年前石炭纪的森林。按主流解释，那时树木刚演化出木质素，而能分解它的微生物还没出现，倒下的巨木层层堆积、深埋，才攒成厚厚的煤层。", en: "Most of the coal burned today comes from the forests of the Carboniferous period, about 300 million years ago. By the mainstream account, trees had just evolved lignin, but microbes that could digest it had not appeared, so fallen trunks piled up and were buried, forming thick coal seams." },
  { id: "science:099", zh: "约 24 亿年前，蓝细菌的光合作用让氧气第一次在地球大气中大量积累，史称“大氧化事件”。但对当时占统治地位的厌氧生物来说，氧气是剧毒——这场供氧，是一场大灭绝。", en: "About 2.4 billion years ago, photosynthesis by cyanobacteria filled Earth's atmosphere with oxygen for the first time: the Great Oxidation Event. But to the anaerobic life then in charge, oxygen was a deadly poison — this gift of air was a mass extinction." },
  { id: "science:100", zh: "末次冰期最盛时，约 2 万年前，大量海水冻成了冰盖，全球海平面比今天低约 120 米。今天的白令海峡当时是干陆，人类和动物可以一路步行，从亚洲走到美洲。", en: "At the peak of the last ice age, about 20,000 years ago, so much water was locked in ice sheets that sea level sat about 120 metres lower than today. The Bering Strait was dry land, and humans and animals could walk from Asia into the Americas." },
  { id: "science:101", zh: "化石多半是这样来的：生物死后若很快被泥沙掩埋，软组织腐烂消失，剩下的骨骼和外壳在地下被矿物质一点点渗透、替换，最终变成石头。所以化石不是原来的骨头，而是骨头的一件矿物复制品。", en: "Most fossils form like this: a dead organism is quickly buried in sediment, its soft tissues rot away, and minerals slowly seep into the remaining bone or shell, replacing it until it is stone. A fossil is not the original bone, but a mineral replica of it." },
  { id: "science:102", zh: "一个房间里只要有 23 个人，其中至少两人同一天生日的概率就超过 50%。直觉觉得不可能，是因为我们只数了人数，却忘了配对的数量会随着人数平方般增长。", en: "In a room of just 23 people, the chance that at least two share a birthday already exceeds 50 percent. Intuition protests because we count the people, but forget that the number of pairs grows almost with the square of the headcount." },
  { id: "science:103", zh: "0.999……（9 无限循环下去）恰好等于 1，不是约等于。一个直观的看法：1/3 等于 0.333……，两边同乘 3，左边是 1，右边正是 0.999……。", en: "0.999… (with the 9s repeating forever) is exactly equal to 1, not approximately. One way to see it: 1/3 equals 0.333…, and multiplying both sides by 3 gives 1 on the left and 0.999… on the right." },
  { id: "science:104", zh: "质数有无穷多个，这个结论欧几里得在两千多年前就证明了：假设质数只有有限个，把它们全部乘起来再加 1，新数除以任何已知质数都余 1，只能引出一个新质数——假设就此崩塌。", en: "There are infinitely many prime numbers, and Euclid proved it over two thousand years ago: suppose the primes were finite in number, multiply them all together and add 1, and the result leaves remainder 1 with every known prime, forcing a new prime into existence — and the assumption collapses." },
  { id: "science:105", zh: "π 是无理数：它的小数展开无限长，而且永远不循环，所以没有人能把 π 完整地写出来。计算机已经算出超过一百万亿位小数，依然望不到尽头，也找不到任何重复的规律。", en: "Pi is irrational: its decimal expansion runs forever without ever repeating, so no one can write it down in full. Computers have calculated more than a hundred trillion of its digits, with no end and no pattern in sight." },
  { id: "science:106", zh: "一张纸对折 7 次左右就再也折不动了。但假设能一直折下去：对折 42 次，厚度将超过地球到月球的距离。指数增长的可怕之处，全在后半程。", en: "A sheet of paper gives up after about seven folds. But if you could keep going, 42 folds would make the stack thicker than the distance from Earth to the Moon. The terror of exponential growth is all in the second half." },
  { id: "science:107", zh: "一朵蓬松的积云看着轻飘飘，里面却装着约 50 万公斤的水，相当于约一百头大象悬在空中。它之所以不掉下来，是因为水被打散成无数微小水滴，又被上升气流稳稳托住。", en: "A fluffy cumulus cloud looks weightless, yet it holds about 500,000 kilograms of water — the equivalent of roughly a hundred elephants hanging in the sky. It stays up because the water is dispersed into countless tiny droplets, buoyed by rising air." },
  { id: "science:108", zh: "极光是太阳送来的灯光秀：太阳风里的高能粒子被地球磁场引向两极，撞进高层大气，把氧和氮的原子撞得发光。氧发绿光和红光，氮则添上紫色与粉色。", en: "Auroras are a light show delivered by the Sun: charged particles in the solar wind are funnelled by Earth's magnetic field toward the poles, where they strike the upper atmosphere and make oxygen and nitrogen atoms glow. Oxygen gives green and red; nitrogen adds violet and pink." },
  { id: "science:109", zh: "雪花几乎总是六角形的，原因藏在分子层面：水结冰时，水分子以六角形的网格排列，微观的秩序被放大成了肉眼可见的对称。所以雪花的形状，是冰的晶体结构写给天空的签名。", en: "Snowflakes are almost always six-sided, for a reason written at the molecular level: as water freezes, its molecules arrange themselves in a hexagonal lattice, and that microscopic order grows into symmetry you can see. A snowflake's shape is ice's crystal structure signing its name." },
  { id: "science:110", zh: "厄尔尼诺指的是赤道太平洋中东部的海水每隔几年一次的异常升温。它一旦出现，全球多地的降雨和气温都会被改写——此地的洪水与彼岸的干旱，可能源于同一片变暖的海水。", en: "El Niño is the abnormal warming, every few years, of surface waters in the central and eastern equatorial Pacific. When it arrives, rainfall and temperatures shift across much of the globe: a flood on one shore and a drought on another can spring from the same patch of warm water." },
  { id: "science:111", zh: "温室效应的基本机制并不复杂：阳光加热地面，地面以红外辐射散热，二氧化碳等温室气体会吸收其中一部分，让热量多停留一会儿。完全没有温室效应，地球平均气温将是约零下 18 摄氏度，而不是现在的约 15 摄氏度。", en: "The greenhouse effect is simple at its core: sunlight warms the ground, the ground sheds heat as infrared radiation, and gases such as carbon dioxide absorb part of it, keeping the warmth a little longer. With no greenhouse effect at all, Earth would average about minus 18 degrees Celsius instead of today's roughly 15." },
  { id: "science:112", zh: "露不是从天上落下来的。晴朗无风的夜里，草叶向寒冷的天空辐射热量，温度降到露点以下，紧贴叶面的水汽便直接凝结成水珠。露，是就地长出来的。", en: "Dew does not fall from the sky. On clear, still nights, leaves radiate heat toward the cold sky and cool below the dew point, so water vapour in the air right against them condenses into droplets. Dew grows where it is found." },
  { id: "science:113", zh: "在阳光永远照不到的深海，发光是常态：据估计，那里约四分之三的动物能自己发光。它们用光诱食、迷惑天敌、辨认同类——在一片漆黑里，光就是语言。", en: "In the deep sea, where sunlight never reaches, glowing is the norm: by some estimates, about three-quarters of the animals there make their own light. They use it to lure prey, confuse predators and recognise their own kind — in total darkness, light is language." },
  { id: "science:114", zh: "许多候鸟能感知地球磁场，靠它跨洲导航。研究认为，它们眼中的一种感光蛋白可能把磁场方向“翻译”成视觉信号。再加上星空和地貌，组成一整套导航系统。", en: "Many migratory birds can sense Earth's magnetic field and steer by it across continents. Research suggests a light-sensitive protein in their eyes may translate the field's direction into a visual signal. Together with the stars and the landscape, it makes a complete navigation system." },
  { id: "science:115", zh: "蝙蝠靠回声定位在黑暗中飞行：它发出人耳听不见的超声波，凭回声的延迟和变化判断猎物的位置，精度足以锁定一只飞蛾。其实蝙蝠并不瞎，大多数蝙蝠的视力完全正常，回声定位是额外的感官。", en: "Bats navigate in darkness by echolocation: they emit ultrasonic calls beyond human hearing and judge a target's position from the timing and texture of the echoes, precisely enough to lock onto a moth. Bats are not blind, either — most see perfectly well; echolocation is an extra sense." },
  { id: "science:116", zh: "海獭没有厚脂肪层御寒，靠的是哺乳动物中最密的毛发，每平方厘米多达十几万根。它还会随身带一块石头，藏在腋下的皮肤口袋里，潜水捞到贝类，就浮上水面用石头把壳敲开。", en: "Sea otters have no thick layer of blubber; they stay warm with the densest fur of any mammal, up to well over a hundred thousand hairs per square centimetre. An otter also carries a favourite rock in a pouch of skin under its arm, to crack open shellfish at the surface." },
  { id: "science:117", zh: "辣不是味觉，而是痛觉：辣椒素会激活口腔里感受灼热和疼痛的神经，让大脑误以为你被烫到了。所以解辣靠牛奶比靠水管用：牛奶中的脂肪能把辣椒素溶解带走。", en: "Spiciness is not a taste but a pain signal: capsaicin activates the nerves that sense heat and burning, fooling the brain into thinking your mouth is scalded. That is why milk beats water for relief — the fat in milk dissolves the capsaicin and washes it away." },
  { id: "science:118", zh: "蜂蜜几乎不会腐坏：它含水量极低、酸性较强，还含有微量过氧化氢，细菌很难在里面存活。考古学家在古埃及墓葬中发现的蜂蜜，据报道历经约三千年，仍然可以食用。", en: "Honey almost never spoils: it holds very little water, is fairly acidic and contains traces of hydrogen peroxide, so bacteria struggle to survive in it. Honey found in ancient Egyptian tombs has reportedly remained edible after some three thousand years." },
  { id: "science:119", zh: "微波炉加热靠的是水分子：微波让食物中的水分子高速振动、摩擦生热。但微波只能穿透食物表面约几厘米，再深处要靠热传导慢慢带热，所以大块食物的中心总是热得最慢。", en: "A microwave oven heats through water: its waves make water molecules in food vibrate furiously and generate heat by friction. But microwaves penetrate only a few centimetres, so deeper parts warm by slow conduction — which is why the centre of a large portion always heats last." },
  { id: "science:120", zh: "海拔越高，气压越低，水的沸点也越低。在拉萨，水约 88 摄氏度就沸腾了，米饭容易夹生。高压锅的原理正是反其道而行：把锅内气压顶上去，硬把沸点抬回 100 摄氏度以上。", en: "The higher you go, the lower the air pressure and the lower water's boiling point. In Lhasa, water boils at about 88 degrees Celsius, and rice easily stays half-raw. A pressure cooker works in reverse: it raises the pressure inside to force the boiling point back above 100." },
];

/* ---------- Land & People (bilingual) ---------- */
const LAND = [
  { id: "land:001", zh: "尼罗河长约 6,650 公里，常被称作世界第一长河；亚马孙河稍短，水量却是另一回事——它注入大西洋的水，比排在其后的几条大河加起来还多。最长与最大，原来不是同一条河。", en: "The Nile runs about 6,650 kilometres and is usually called the world's longest river; the Amazon is a little shorter, but its flow is another matter — it pours more water into the Atlantic than the next several great rivers combined. Longest and largest turn out not to be the same river." },
  { id: "land:002", zh: "里海名为海，其实是湖：四面都是陆地，面积约 37 万平方公里，是世界最大的湖泊，沿岸分属五个国家。它只是大得让人误会成了一片海。", en: "The Caspian is called a sea but is really a lake: ringed entirely by land, it covers about 370,000 square kilometres, the largest lake on Earth, shared by five countries. It is simply big enough to be mistaken for a sea." },
  { id: "land:003", zh: "贝加尔湖最深处约 1,640 米，是世界最深的湖。它一个湖就装着全球约五分之一的地表淡水，比北美五大湖的总和还多。", en: "Lake Baikal reaches about 1,640 metres at its deepest and is the world's deepest lake. Alone, it holds roughly a fifth of the planet's surface fresh water — more than all of North America's Great Lakes combined." },
  { id: "land:004", zh: "乞力马扎罗离赤道只有约 300 公里，山顶却终年积雪：海拔 5,895 米的非洲最高峰，把一片冬天搬到了热带。只是这顶雪冠，正一年年变薄。", en: "Kilimanjaro stands only about 300 kilometres from the equator, yet its summit is permanently snow-capped: at 5,895 metres, Africa's highest peak carries a piece of winter into the tropics. The snow crown, though, is thinning year by year." },
  { id: "land:005", zh: "撒哈拉沙漠面积约 920 万平方公里，和整个美国差不多大。但它并非一望无际的沙丘：岩石高原和砾石荒漠占了大半，电视里那种金色沙海只占一小部分。", en: "The Sahara covers about 9.2 million square kilometres, roughly the size of the United States. But it is not an endless sea of dunes: rocky plateaus and gravel plains make up most of it, and the golden sand seas of television are only a small part." },
  { id: "land:006", zh: "法国本土只用一个时区，但算上散布全球的海外领地，它一共横跨 12 个时区，是时区最多的国家。太阳在法兰西的土地上，几乎不会完全落下。", en: "Metropolitan France keeps a single time zone, but counting its overseas territories scattered across the globe, France spans 12 time zones — more than any other country. The sun, over French soil, almost never fully sets." },
  { id: "land:007", zh: "格陵兰是世界最大的岛屿，面积约 216 万平方公里，八成被冰盖覆盖。它是丹麦的自治领地，居民却只有约 5.6 万人——平均每人分得的冰原，比一座城市还大。", en: "Greenland is the world's largest island, about 2.16 million square kilometres, some 80 percent of it buried under an ice sheet. It is an autonomous territory of Denmark with only about 56,000 residents — on average, each person's share of ice is bigger than a city." },
  { id: "land:008", zh: "东非大裂谷绵延约 6,000 公里，是大地正在撕开的一道口子：非洲板块在这里以每年几毫米的速度缓慢分裂。裂谷里躺着一串湖泊与火山，也埋着许多人类祖先的化石。", en: "The Great Rift Valley runs about 6,000 kilometres — a seam where the Earth is slowly tearing open, the African plate splitting apart a few millimetres a year. Its floor holds a chain of lakes and volcanoes, and many fossils of humanity's ancestors." },
  { id: "land:009", zh: "安第斯山脉沿南美洲西海岸绵延约 7,000 公里，穿过七个国家，是陆地上最长的山脉。印加人曾在海拔三四千米的高处，建起他们的帝国和石城。", en: "The Andes stretch about 7,000 kilometres along South America's west coast through seven countries — the longest mountain range on land. The Inca built their empire and their stone cities three to four thousand metres up in it." },
  { id: "land:010", zh: "马达加斯加在约 8,800 万年前就与大陆分了家，从此独自演化。这里约九成的野生动植物在地球上别处找不到：狐猴、猴面包树，都是这座岛自己的作品。", en: "Madagascar parted from the mainland about 88 million years ago and has been evolving on its own ever since. Around 90 percent of its wildlife exists nowhere else on Earth: lemurs and baobabs are this island's own inventions." },
  { id: "land:011", zh: "荷兰约有三分之一的国土低于海平面，最低处比海面还低约 7 米。几个世纪以来，荷兰人靠堤坝、风车和围海造田，硬是从海里夺回大片土地——这个国家有不小的部分，是从水里造出来的。", en: "About a third of the Netherlands lies below sea level, its lowest point some 7 metres under the sea. For centuries the Dutch have won land back with dikes, windmills and polders — a good share of the country was made out of water by its own people." },
  { id: "land:012", zh: "咸海曾是世界第四大湖，面积约 6.8 万平方公里。20 世纪 60 年代起，注入它的两条河被改去灌溉棉田，湖面萎缩了约九成，如今锈蚀的船只搁浅在盐碱化的旧湖底上。", en: "The Aral Sea was once the world's fourth-largest lake, about 68,000 square kilometres. From the 1960s its two feeding rivers were diverted to irrigate cotton, and the lake shrank by some 90 percent; rusting ships now sit stranded on the salt-crusted former lake bed." },
  { id: "land:013", zh: "青藏高原平均海拔 4,000 米以上，存着两极之外最多的冰，被称作“第三极”。长江、黄河、恒河、湄公河——亚洲的大河，许多都从这片高原上出发。", en: "The Tibetan Plateau averages over 4,000 metres above sea level and holds more ice than anywhere outside the poles, earning it the name 'the Third Pole'. The Yangtze, the Yellow River, the Ganges, the Mekong — many of Asia's great rivers begin up here." },
  { id: "land:014", zh: "塔克拉玛干是中国最大的沙漠，面积约 33 万平方公里，沙丘会随风整体迁移。古丝绸之路走到这里，只能沿它的南北两缘绕行——它的名字，相传就是“进去出不来”的意思。", en: "The Taklamakan is China's largest desert, about 330,000 square kilometres of dunes that migrate wholesale with the wind. The old Silk Road split to skirt its northern and southern edges — its name is traditionally read as 'the place you enter and never leave'." },
  { id: "land:015", zh: "多瑙河是流经国家最多的河：从德国黑森林到黑海，全长约 2,850 公里，途经十个国家，还一口气串起维也纳、布拉迪斯拉发、布达佩斯和贝尔格莱德四座首都。", en: "The Danube passes through more countries than any other river: about 2,850 kilometres from Germany's Black Forest to the Black Sea, crossing ten nations and stringing together four capitals — Vienna, Bratislava, Budapest and Belgrade." },
  { id: "land:016", zh: "马六甲海峡夹在马来半岛与苏门答腊岛之间，连接印度洋和太平洋，是全球最繁忙的水道之一。世界贸易中约有四分之一的货物，要从这条窄窄的水道里通过。", en: "The Strait of Malacca, squeezed between the Malay Peninsula and Sumatra, joins the Indian and Pacific oceans and is among the busiest waterways on Earth. Roughly a quarter of the world's traded goods pass through this narrow channel." },
  { id: "land:017", zh: "巴拿马运河长约 82 公里，在美洲最窄处凿通地峡，把大西洋和太平洋接了起来。走运河的船，省去了绕行南美洲最南端合恩角的上万公里航程。", en: "The Panama Canal, about 82 kilometres long, cuts through the Americas at their narrowest and joins the Atlantic to the Pacific. Ships that use it skip the detour of more than ten thousand kilometres around Cape Horn at South America's tip." },
  { id: "land:018", zh: "苏伊士运河长约 193 公里，在埃及的沙漠中把地中海和红海连通。它没有船闸——两端海面几乎等高，船队就排着队，平平地从一片海开进另一片海。", en: "The Suez Canal runs about 193 kilometres through Egypt's desert, linking the Mediterranean with the Red Sea. It has no locks at all — the two seas sit at nearly the same level, so convoys simply sail flat from one sea into the other." },
  { id: "land:019", zh: "挪威的峡湾是冰川留下的签名：冰河时代，厚重的冰层像锉刀一样刨开山体；冰退之后，海水灌进深谷。其中松恩峡湾长约 205 公里，是挪威最长、水最深的峡湾。", en: "Norway's fjords are the signature of glaciers: during the ice ages, immense ice sheets gouged valleys like a file; when the ice withdrew, the sea flooded in. Sognefjord, about 205 kilometres long, is Norway's longest and deepest fjord." },
  { id: "land:020", zh: "冰岛正骑在大西洋中脊上，被北美板块和欧亚板块以每年约 2 厘米的速度缓缓拉开。所以它遍地火山与温泉——一个国家，就坐在地球的裂缝上生活。", en: "Iceland straddles the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, pulled apart by the North American and Eurasian plates at about 2 centimetres a year. Hence its volcanoes and hot springs everywhere — a whole country living on a crack in the Earth." },
  { id: "land:021", zh: "夏威夷是地球上最孤远的群岛，离最近的大陆海岸约 3,900 公里。它是海底火山一个接一个冒出海面排成的队，队尾最年轻的岛屿，至今还在长大。", en: "Hawaii is the most remote island chain on Earth, about 3,900 kilometres from the nearest continental shore. It is a line of undersea volcanoes that rose one after another above the waves — and the youngest island, at the end of the line, is still growing." },
  { id: "land:022", zh: "复活节岛离南美大陆约 3,500 公里，是世界上最偏远的有人岛屿之一。岛上的拉帕努伊人在这里竖起近 900 尊石像，最大的高约 10 米，几百年来一直面朝内陆站着。", en: "Easter Island lies about 3,500 kilometres off South America and is among the most remote inhabited islands in the world. Its Rapa Nui people raised nearly 900 stone statues here, the largest about 10 metres tall, standing for centuries facing inland." },
  { id: "land:023", zh: "亚马孙雨林覆盖约 550 万平方公里，六成在巴西。它常被称为“地球之肺”，其实它产生的氧大多被自己用掉；它真正的伟业是造雨——蒸腾的水汽在空中汇成“飞河”，滋润着大半个南美洲。", en: "The Amazon rainforest covers about 5.5 million square kilometres, 60 percent of it in Brazil. It is often called the lungs of the Earth, though most of the oxygen it makes is used by the forest itself; its true feat is making rain — transpired vapour gathers into 'flying rivers' that water much of South America." },
  { id: "land:024", zh: "潘帕斯草原是阿根廷的粮仓：约 75 万平方公里的肥沃草甸，养着成群的牛和一望无际的小麦。骑马放牧的高乔人，就是在这片草原上成为传奇的。", en: "The Pampas are Argentina's granary: some 750,000 square kilometres of fertile grassland carrying herds of cattle and wheat to the horizon. The gauchos, the horsemen of these plains, became legends here." },
  { id: "land:025", zh: "恒河长约 2,525 公里，是印度教徒心中的圣河。它的流域养育了数亿人，是地球上人口最稠密的河流走廊之一；临入海时，它与布拉马普特拉河共同冲积出世界最大的三角洲。", en: "The Ganges runs about 2,525 kilometres and is sacred in Hinduism. Its basin sustains hundreds of millions of people — one of the most densely peopled river corridors on Earth — and near the sea it joins the Brahmaputra to build the world's largest delta." },
  { id: "land:026", zh: "湄公河发源于青藏高原，在中国境内叫澜沧江，出境后流经缅甸、老挝、泰国、柬埔寨和越南，全长约 4,500 公里。它下游的洞里萨湖很特别：雨季河水倒灌，湖面能涨大数倍。", en: "The Mekong rises on the Tibetan Plateau, where in China it is called the Lancang; it then flows through Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam for about 4,500 kilometres. Its lower lake, Tonlé Sap, is remarkable: in the rainy season the river flows backwards into it, and the lake swells several times over." },
  { id: "land:027", zh: "莱茵河从瑞士的阿尔卑斯山流向鹿特丹外的北海，全长约 1,230 公里，是欧洲内河航运的大动脉。两岸排着城堡、葡萄园和古镇，一条河走成了欧洲的缩影。", en: "The Rhine flows about 1,230 kilometres from the Swiss Alps to the North Sea near Rotterdam, and is the great artery of European inland shipping. Castles, vineyards and old towns line its banks — a single river that reads like Europe in miniature." },
  { id: "land:028", zh: "密西西比河长约 3,730 公里，从明尼苏达的一座小湖一路向南，在新奥尔良附近注入墨西哥湾。它的流域覆盖了美国约四成的土地，马克·吐温的许多故事就发生在这条河上。", en: "The Mississippi runs about 3,730 kilometres from a small Minnesota lake south to the Gulf of Mexico near New Orleans. Its basin drains some 40 percent of the United States, and many of Mark Twain's stories are set on this river." },
  { id: "land:029", zh: "死海的岸边是地球陆地的最低点，低于海平面约 430 米，而且还在以每年约一米的速度继续下沉。站在死海边，你站在所有陆地的最底处。", en: "The shore of the Dead Sea is the lowest land on Earth, about 430 metres below sea level, and still sinking by roughly a metre a year. Stand on that shore and you are standing at the bottom of all the world's land." },
  { id: "land:030", zh: "长江全长约 6,300 公里，是亚洲最长、世界第三长的河，也是全程只流经一个国家的最长河流。从青藏高原的冰川到东海，它的流域养育着中国约三分之一的人口。", en: "The Yangtze runs about 6,300 kilometres — Asia's longest river, the world's third longest, and the longest of all to flow entirely within one country. From glaciers on the Tibetan Plateau to the East China Sea, its basin sustains roughly a third of China's people." },
  { id: "land:031", zh: "墨西哥的亡灵节在每年 11 月初。家人摆起祭坛，放上逝者的照片、万寿菊和他们生前爱吃的食物，还去墓地野餐、弹唱——在墨西哥人看来，死亡不是告别，是一年一度的回家。", en: "Mexico's Day of the Dead falls in early November. Families build altars with photographs of the departed, marigolds and the foods they loved, and picnic and sing in the cemeteries — for Mexicans, death is not a farewell but an annual homecoming." },
  { id: "land:032", zh: "印度的洒红节在春天到来时庆祝：人们上街互相抛洒彩色粉末和水，纪念春天和善对恶的胜利。这一天，长幼尊卑暂时作废——到最后，所有人都被染成同一种颜色：彩色。", en: "India's Holi greets the spring: crowds throw coloured powder and water over one another, celebrating the season and the victory of good over evil. For one day, rank and age are suspended — by the end, everyone is dyed the same colour: all of them." },
  { id: "land:033", zh: "排灯节是印度最重要的节日之一，秋季连庆五天。家家点起陶土小油灯，排满窗台、台阶和屋顶，迎接光明战胜黑暗，也迎接富足。灯火最密的地方，就是祝福最密的地方。", en: "Diwali, among India's greatest festivals, is celebrated for five days in autumn. Households set out rows of small clay oil lamps on windowsills, steps and roofs, welcoming light over darkness and prosperity into the home. Where the lamps are thickest, so are the blessings." },
  { id: "land:034", zh: "泰国的宋干节是泰历新年，在 4 月中旬。人们先去寺庙浴佛、往长辈手心洒水祝福，后来演变成满城泼水——水在这里不是胡闹，是洗去旧年、迎接新生的祝福。", en: "Thailand's Songkran, in mid-April, is the Thai New Year. People first pour water over Buddha statues in temples and into elders' palms as a blessing; it has grown into citywide water play — the water is no prank, but a blessing that washes off the old year." },
  { id: "land:035", zh: "蒙古的那达慕在每年 7 月的草原上举行，比三样：摔跤、赛马、射箭，合称“男儿三艺”。这套竞赛脱胎于成吉思汗时代的练兵，从草原帝国的军营，一直比到了今天的节日。", en: "Mongolia's Naadam is held every July on the grasslands, contesting three skills — wrestling, horse racing and archery, the 'three games of men'. The contests grew out of military training in the age of Genghis Khan, and are still run today as a festival." },
  { id: "land:036", zh: "芬兰约有 550 万人，却有约 300 万间桑拿。对芬兰人来说，桑拿不是消遣，是生活本身：旧时孩子在桑拿房里出生，要紧的话也在蒸汽里谈——先脱下衣服，再放下防备。", en: "Finland has about 5.5 million people and some 3 million saunas. For Finns the sauna is not a pastime but a way of life: in the old days children were born in saunas, and serious talk still happens in the steam — first you take off your clothes, then your guard." },
  { id: "land:037", zh: "埃塞俄比亚用着自己的一套历法：一年有 13 个月，年份比公历慢了约七八年，新年落在 9 月。走进这个国家，像走进了世界日历的另一页。", en: "Ethiopia keeps its own calendar: thirteen months in a year, running some seven or eight years behind the Gregorian one, with New Year in September. To enter the country is to step onto another page of the world's calendar." },
  { id: "land:038", zh: "慕尼黑啤酒节其实主要在 9 月：它始于 1810 年的一场王室婚礼庆典，后来年年都办，办成了传统。如今每年有约六百万人涌进节日的帐篷，喝掉数百万升啤酒。", en: "Munich's Oktoberfest actually takes place mostly in September: it began in 1810 as the celebration of a royal wedding and simply never stopped. Today about six million people a year crowd into its tents and drink millions of litres of beer." },
  { id: "land:039", zh: "巴西狂欢节在里约热内卢达到顶点。桑巴学校要花上一整年缝制服装、排练舞步，只为在桑巴大道上走完那几十分钟——对参赛的人来说，那不是表演，是一年一度的决战。", en: "Brazil's Carnival reaches its peak in Rio de Janeiro. Samba schools spend a whole year sewing costumes and rehearsing steps for a parade of a few dozen minutes down the Sambadrome — for those taking part, it is not a show but the decisive contest of the year." },
  { id: "land:040", zh: "威尼斯狂欢节的主角是面具。历史上，面具让贵族与平民在街上暂时认不出彼此，等级被藏在面具后面，人人可以一起狂欢；节期一过，面具摘下，日子各归其位。", en: "The star of Venice's Carnival is the mask. In centuries past, masks let nobles and commoners pass unrecognised in the street, rank hidden for a while so everyone could revel together; when the festival ends, the masks come off and life returns to its stations." },
  { id: "land:041", zh: "日本的成人式在每年 1 月，为刚成年的年轻人举行。女孩子穿振袖和服——一生中袖子最长的礼服——回到家乡参加典礼，和老同学合影：一群人一起长大成人，是这件事的仪式感所在。", en: "Japan's Coming of Age Day is held every January for those who have just reached adulthood. Young women wear furisode kimono — the long-sleeved formal dress of their unmarried years — and return to their hometowns for the ceremony and photographs with old classmates: growing up, done together, is the point of the ritual." },
  { id: "land:042", zh: "冰岛有个“圣诞书洪”：平安夜互赠新书，然后就着热巧克力，读上一整晚。这个国家只有约 37 万人，却是全球人均出版图书最多的国家之一——书是最受欢迎的圣诞礼物，没有之一。", en: "Iceland has its 'Christmas book flood': on Christmas Eve people give each other new books, then read late into the night with hot chocolate. The country has only about 370,000 people yet publishes more books per head than almost anywhere — books are the Christmas present, without rival." },
  { id: "land:043", zh: "瑞典的仲夏节在夏至前后，是瑞典人最看重的节日之一。人们竖起缠满花叶的柱子，戴上花环，围着它唱歌跳舞——在漫长的北欧冬天之后，这是对阳光的一次集体致谢。", en: "Sweden's Midsummer, around the solstice, is among the country's most cherished holidays. People raise a pole wound with flowers and leaves, wear wreaths, and sing and dance around it — after the long northern winter, a collective thank-you to the sun." },
  { id: "land:044", zh: "荷兰国王日在 4 月底，全国上下穿成橙色——那是荷兰王室的姓氏之色。这一天，任何人都可以在街上摆摊卖旧物，运河上挤满了船，整座阿姆斯特丹像一间巨大的露天旧货市场。", en: "On the Netherlands' King's Day in late April, the whole country dresses in orange — the colour of the royal house's name. Anyone may set up a street stall to sell second-hand goods, the canals fill with boats, and Amsterdam becomes one vast open-air flea market." },
  { id: "land:045", zh: "圣帕特里克节在 3 月 17 日，爱尔兰人穿绿衣、佩三叶草，纪念他们的守护圣人。如今最盛大的游行不在爱尔兰，而在纽约——移民把故乡的节日带在身上，带到了全世界。", en: "St Patrick's Day, on 17 March, sees the Irish in green with shamrocks, honouring their patron saint. Today the grandest parades are not in Ireland but in New York — emigrants carried their home festival with them, and it now belongs to the world." },
  { id: "land:046", zh: "斋月里，穆斯林从日出到日落不饮不食，把心思放在克制、祈祷和施舍上。一个月后的开斋节，家家摆开盛宴、走亲访友——先苦行，后团圆，是这个节日的节奏。", en: "During Ramadan, Muslims fast from sunrise to sunset, turning their minds to restraint, prayer and charity. A month later, Eid al-Fitr opens with feasts and visits to family — first the discipline, then the reunion: that is the rhythm of the festival." },
  { id: "land:047", zh: "犹太人的逾越节，要吃一顿讲故事的晚餐：无酵饼纪念祖先逃离埃及时来不及等面团发酵，苦菜纪念为奴的岁月。席间由最小的孩子发问“今晚为何与别的夜晚不同”——节日就这样一代代讲下去。", en: "At Passover, Jewish families eat a dinner that tells a story: unleavened bread recalls ancestors fleeing Egypt with no time for dough to rise, bitter herbs recall slavery. The youngest child asks why this night differs from all others — and so the festival is retold, generation to generation." },
  { id: "land:048", zh: "韩国的秋夕在农历八月十五，是最大的传统节日之一：全家回乡祭祖、吃松饼、赏满月。节日期间，高速公路会堵成长龙——那是几千万人同时在往家赶。", en: "Korea's Chuseok, on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month, is among its greatest traditional festivals: families return home to honour ancestors, eat songpyeon rice cakes and admire the full moon. The motorways jam solid — tens of millions of people, all heading home at once." },
  { id: "land:049", zh: "越南的中秋节是孩子们的节日：孩子们提着星星形状的灯笼上街，看舞狮、吃月饼。大人们忙着摆供桌敬月亮，孩子才是这一天真正的主角。", en: "Vietnam's Mid-Autumn Festival belongs to children: they carry star-shaped lanterns through the streets, watch lion dances and eat mooncakes. The adults are busy setting out offerings to the moon, but the children are the day's true protagonists." },
  { id: "land:050", zh: "秘鲁的太阳祭在每年 6 月 24 日于古都库斯科举行，源自印加人在冬至祭祀太阳神因蒂的仪式。今天的庆典是 20 世纪复兴的版本，成千上万人涌进古城，看“太阳”重新升起。", en: "Peru's Inti Raymi is held every 24 June in the old capital of Cusco, reviving the Inca winter-solstice rite honouring the sun god Inti. Today's ceremony is a twentieth-century revival, and thousands pour into the ancient city to watch the 'sun' rise again." },
  { id: "land:051", zh: "哈卡是新西兰毛利人的传统战舞：顿足、怒目、吐舌，配上齐声的呼喊，既能震慑对手，也能欢迎贵客。橄榄球队“全黑队”在赛前的那一跳，让全世界都认识了它。", en: "The haka is the traditional posture dance of New Zealand's Māori: stamping feet, wide eyes, tongues out, voices raised together — it can daunt an opponent or honour a guest. The All Blacks rugby team's pre-match haka has carried it to the whole world." },
  { id: "land:052", zh: "格鲁吉亚人用埋在地下的大陶罐酿酒，已经约八千年：葡萄连皮带籽封进陶罐，在土里慢慢发酵。这是人类最古老的酿酒方法之一，今天格鲁吉亚农家的院子地下，可能就埋着一罐正在变的酒。", en: "Georgians have made wine in great clay vessels buried underground for some eight thousand years: grapes go in with skins and seeds and ferment slowly in the earth. It is among humanity's oldest winemaking methods, and beneath a Georgian farmyard today there may be a qvevri quietly at work." },
  { id: "land:053", zh: "埃塞俄比亚是咖啡的故乡，待客最隆重的是咖啡仪式：生豆当场烘烤、研磨、煮沸，前后三巡，要花一个多小时。在那里，咖啡不是用来提神的，是用来把时间慢慢过的。", en: "Ethiopia is the homeland of coffee, and its warmest welcome is the coffee ceremony: green beans are roasted, ground and brewed on the spot, in three rounds that take over an hour. There, coffee is not for waking up — it is for slowing time down." },
  { id: "land:054", zh: "日本人在除夕夜吃“年越荞麦面”：面条细长，象征长寿；又容易咬断，象征把一年的辛苦干脆地切断。新年还没到，先把旧年吃掉。", en: "On New Year's Eve, Japanese families eat toshikoshi soba, 'year-crossing noodles': the long noodles stand for long life, and their easy bite for cutting the year's hardships cleanly away. Before the new year arrives, the old one is eaten." },
  { id: "land:055", zh: "英式下午茶是饿出来的：19 世纪的英国晚餐要等到晚上八点，贝德福德公爵夫人下午饿了，就让人备上茶和点心垫一垫。一顿临时的加餐，慢慢吃成了英国的象征。", en: "English afternoon tea was born of hunger: in nineteenth-century Britain dinner waited until eight in the evening, and the Duchess of Bedford, peckish in the afternoon, called for tea and small bites to tide her over. An improvised snack slowly became a national institution." },
  { id: "land:056", zh: "探戈诞生在拉普拉塔河两岸的港口区，是移民和劳工在异乡揉出的舞步：起初被上流社会看不起，后来征服了巴黎的舞厅，才衣锦还乡，成了阿根廷的名片。", en: "Tango was born in the port districts on both banks of the Río de la Plata, a dance kneaded together by immigrants and labourers far from home: looked down on at first by polite society, it conquered the ballrooms of Paris before coming home in glory as Argentina's calling card." },
  { id: "land:057", zh: "土耳其的旋转舞源自苏菲派的修行：舞者身穿白袍，在科尼亚的传统里旋转不止，一只手掌向天，一只手掌向地。这不是表演，是用身体做的祈祷——转，是为了接近神圣。", en: "Turkey's whirling dance comes from Sufi practice: dancers in white robes turn without ceasing, in the tradition of Konya, one palm to the sky and one to the earth. It is not a performance but a prayer made with the body — turning, in order to draw near the divine." },
  { id: "land:058", zh: "西班牙布尼奥尔镇的番茄大战在每年 8 月的最后一个星期三：上百吨过熟的番茄被运进小镇，上万人互砸一个小时。规矩只有一条要紧：开砸之前，先把番茄捏软——尽兴，但不许伤人。", en: "La Tomatina, in the Spanish town of Buñol, falls on the last Wednesday of August: over a hundred tonnes of overripe tomatoes are trucked in, and thousands pelt one another for an hour. One rule matters above all — squash each tomato before you throw it: total abandon, but nobody gets hurt." },
  { id: "land:059", zh: "苏格兰人过除夕有个“第一脚”的讲究：午夜之后第一个跨进家门的人，最好随身带着煤、面包和威士忌——分别象征温暖、丰足和欢乐。第一个客人带什么进门，像是一年的预兆。", en: "Scotland's Hogmanay keeps the custom of 'first-footing': the first person to cross your threshold after midnight should carry coal, bread and whisky — standing for warmth, plenty and good cheer. What the first guest brings in is taken as an omen for the year." },
  { id: "land:060", zh: "加拿大的枫糖季在冬末春初：夜里上冻、白天回暖，枫树的汁液才开始流动。仅魁北克一省，就产出全球约七成的枫糖浆——加拿大人把春天熬成了甜的。", en: "Canada's maple season comes at the end of winter: only when nights freeze and days thaw does the sap begin to run. Quebec alone produces about 70 percent of the world's maple syrup — Canadians boil the spring down into sweetness." },
];

/* ---------- Mathematics (bilingual) ---------- */
const MATH = [
  { id: "math:001", zh: "祖冲之在约 1500 年前算出圆周率在 3.1415926 与 3.1415927 之间，还给出分数 355/113 作为近似——这个纪录在世界范围内保持了近千年。他用的工具，只有算筹和耐心。", en: "Some 1,500 years ago, Zu Chongzhi pinned pi between 3.1415926 and 3.1415927, and offered the fraction 355/113 as an approximation — a world record that stood for nearly a thousand years. His only tools were counting rods and patience." },
  { id: "math:002", zh: "常数 e 约等于 2.71828，最早藏在一道利息题里：本金存一年，年利 100%，若利息按月、按日、按每一秒复利，最终金额不会无限膨胀，而是收敛到 e 倍。增长本身，长着一张数学的脸。", en: "The constant e is about 2.71828, and it first hid inside a question about interest: deposit money at 100 percent for a year, compound it monthly, daily, every second — the total does not grow forever but settles at e times the principal. Growth itself wears a mathematical face." },
  { id: "math:003", zh: "黄金比约等于 1.618，常被说成藏在帕特农神庙和向日葵里。但仔细核查会发现，许多“黄金比例”是先画好框再去套数字，量出的误差大得足够塞进别的比例。它是个有趣的数，只是没有传说中那么无处不在。", en: "The golden ratio is about 1.618, often said to hide in the Parthenon and in sunflowers. Careful checking shows many such 'golden ratios' are frames drawn first and numbers fitted afterwards, with errors large enough to fit other ratios too. It is a fascinating number — just not as omnipresent as the legend claims." },
  { id: "math:004", zh: "√2 是第一个被发现的无理数：边长为 1 的正方形，对角线长得无法用任何分数表示。相传毕达哥拉斯学派的希帕索斯泄露了这个秘密，被同门投入大海——故事真假难考，无理数却从此留在了人间。", en: "√2 was the first irrational number ever found: the diagonal of a unit square has a length no fraction can express. Legend says Hippasus of the Pythagorean school was thrown into the sea for leaking the secret — whether the tale is true is hard to say, but irrational numbers stayed in the world for good." },
  { id: "math:005", zh: "虚数单位 i 定义为 √−1。16 世纪它被当作无用的把戏，卡尔达诺称它“既精致又没用”。可没有它，今天的交流电分析、信号处理和量子力学都无从下笔——被嘲笑的数，后来撑起了半个世界。", en: "The imaginary unit i is defined as √−1. In the 16th century it was dismissed as a useless trick; Cardano called it 'as subtle as it is useless'. Yet without it, modern AC circuit analysis, signal processing and quantum mechanics could not even begin — the mocked number went on to hold up half the world." },
  { id: "math:006", zh: "0 不只是“什么都没有”：作为占位符，它让 205 和 25 成为两个数。这套空位记数法在印度成熟起来。没有这个表示空缺的符号，就没有位值制，多位数乘法都无从下手——现代数学的大门，是一个圈画出来的。", en: "Zero is not just 'nothing': as a placeholder it makes 205 and 25 two different numbers. This positional notation matured in India. Without a symbol for an empty place there is no place-value system, and multiplying large numbers has nowhere to start — the doorway of modern mathematics is drawn as a circle." },
  { id: "math:007", zh: "希尔伯特旅馆有无穷多间房，而且已经住满。这时再来一位客人，店主让每位住客搬到隔壁房间，1 号房就空了出来。无穷的世界里，“满了”和“还能住”可以同时为真。", en: "Hilbert's hotel has infinitely many rooms, and every one is occupied. When one more guest arrives, the manager moves each guest to the next room, and room 1 stands empty. In the world of infinity, 'full' and 'room for more' can both be true at once." },
  { id: "math:008", zh: "负数在中国《九章算术》里早已自如使用，还分红黑两色算筹区分正负。但欧洲数学家迟迟不肯承认：直到 17 世纪，还有人称负数是“荒谬的数”。一个数是否有资格存在，有时要等上千年。", en: "Negative numbers were handled freely in China's Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art, with red and black counting rods distinguishing positive from negative. European mathematicians, though, resisted for centuries — as late as the 1600s some still called negatives 'absurd numbers'. Whether a number is allowed to exist can take a thousand years to settle." },
  { id: "math:009", zh: "6 是完美数：它的真因子 1、2、3 加起来正好是它自己。220 与 284 则是一对亲和数：各自的因子和恰好等于对方。古人把亲和数刻在护身符上，当作友谊的证明——数学里的温情，两千多年前就有了。", en: "Six is a perfect number: its proper divisors 1, 2 and 3 add up to itself. And 220 and 284 form an amicable pair — each one's divisors sum to the other. The ancients carved amicable numbers into amulets as tokens of friendship: mathematics had tenderness more than two thousand years ago." },
  { id: "math:010", zh: "任取一个四位数（数字不全相同），把数字从大到小排、再从小到大排，两数相减；重复这个操作，最多七步，一定会落到 6174，而 6174 减下去还是它自己。这个“黑洞常数”由印度数学家卡普雷卡尔发现，至今没有更深的解释。", en: "Take any four-digit number (not all digits the same), arrange its digits descending and ascending, and subtract; repeat, and within at most seven steps you always land on 6174 — and 6174 maps to itself. This 'black hole constant' was found by the Indian mathematician Kaprekar, and no deeper explanation has ever turned up." },
  { id: "math:011", zh: "勾股定理出现过很多次：中国《周髀算经》记下“勾三股四弦五”，巴比伦泥板上刻着更早的勾股数组，印度、希腊也各有证明。它大概是被人类独立发现次数最多的定理——直角属于每一个文明。", en: "The Pythagorean theorem was discovered again and again: China's Zhoubi Suanjing records the 3-4-5 triangle, Babylonian tablets hold still older triples, and India and Greece each had their proofs. It may be the most independently rediscovered theorem in history — the right angle belongs to every civilisation." },
  { id: "math:012", zh: "1637 年，费马在书页边写下：他对某个命题有一个美妙的证明，可惜空白太小写不下。此后 358 年，一代代数学家前赴后继，直到 1995 年怀尔斯才补上完整证明，长达一百多页。那句“写不下”，是数学史上最长的一句玩笑。", en: "In 1637 Fermat scribbled in a margin that he had a marvellous proof of a certain claim, but the margin was too small to hold it. For 358 years mathematicians laboured, until Wiles completed the proof in 1995 — over a hundred pages long. That 'too small to hold it' became the longest-running joke in mathematics." },
  { id: "math:013", zh: "1742 年，哥德巴赫提出：每个大于 2 的偶数，都能写成两个质数之和。4=2+2，100=3+97，从未失手，计算机已验算到极大的数——但至今无人能给出证明。它是数学里最容易讲懂、又最难拿下的猜想之一。", en: "In 1742 Goldbach proposed that every even number greater than 2 is the sum of two primes. 4 = 2+2, 100 = 3+97 — it has never failed, and computers have checked it to enormous numbers, yet to this day no one can prove it. It is among the easiest conjectures to explain and the hardest to claim." },
  { id: "math:014", zh: "任何一张地图，只用四种颜色，就能让相邻的国家不同色。1976 年它被证明，方法却让许多人不舒服：计算机把近两千种情形逐一验完。这是第一个主要靠计算机完成的著名证明——从那以后，“证明”这个词的边界变了。", en: "Any map can be coloured with just four colours so that neighbouring countries never match. It was proved in 1976, in a way that made many uneasy: a computer checked nearly two thousand cases one by one. It was the first famous proof completed mainly by machine — and the word 'proof' has had a wider border ever since." },
  { id: "math:015", zh: "e 的 iπ 次方加 1 等于 0。一个等式里，住着数学最要紧的五个常数：0、1、e、π 和虚数单位 i，它们本是陌生人，却在这里严丝合缝。费曼称它是“数学中最卓越的公式”。", en: "e raised to the power of iπ, plus 1, equals 0. Inside one equation live the five most important constants in mathematics — 0, 1, e, π and the imaginary unit i — strangers that fit together without a seam. Feynman called it 'the most remarkable formula in mathematics'." },
  { id: "math:016", zh: "黎曼猜想关乎质数的分布规律，1859 年提出，至今未被证明。它是克雷研究所悬赏一百万美元的七个“千禧难题”之一：上千条数学结论以它成立为前提，它若倒下，一座大厦要跟着摇晃。", en: "The Riemann hypothesis concerns the pattern of the primes; proposed in 1859, it remains unproved. It is one of the Clay Institute's seven Millennium Problems, each carrying a million-dollar prize: over a thousand results assume it is true, and if it fell, a whole edifice would shake." },
  { id: "math:017", zh: "3 与 5、11 与 13、17 与 19——相差 2 的质数对叫孪生素数。它们是否有无穷多对，至今未证。2013 年，张益唐证明：间隔小于七千万的质数对有无穷多——把“无穷远”的距离，第一次拉到了有限的数字上。", en: "3 and 5, 11 and 13, 17 and 19 — prime pairs two apart are called twin primes. Whether there are infinitely many remains unproved. In 2013 Zhang Yitang showed there are infinitely many prime pairs less than seventy million apart — pulling an 'infinite' distance down to a finite number for the first time." },
  { id: "math:018", zh: "1736 年，哥尼斯堡人爱问：散步时能否一次走遍全城七座桥，每座只过一次？欧拉把陆地缩成点、桥缩成线，一举证明：不可能。问题消失了，一门新学问——图论，却从这一天诞生。", en: "In 1736 the citizens of Königsberg liked to ask: can a walk cross all seven bridges of the city exactly once each? Euler shrank land masses to points and bridges to lines, and proved it impossible. The puzzle vanished — and a new subject, graph theory, was born that day." },
  { id: "math:019", zh: "把纸带扭半圈再粘合，就得到莫比乌斯带：它只有一个面。一只蚂蚁沿着它爬，不跨过任何边界，就能走遍“两面”回到原点。1858 年它被独立提出两次——简单的东西，有时最难想到。", en: "Twist a strip of paper half a turn and join the ends, and you get a Möbius strip: it has only one side. An ant walking along it, never crossing an edge, can visit 'both sides' and return to the start. It was described independently twice in 1858 — the simplest ideas are sometimes the hardest to have." },
  { id: "math:020", zh: "英国的海岸线有多长？答案取决于你的尺子：尺子越短，量出的弯弯绕绕越多，总长就越长，甚至可以无限增加。曼德博由此提出分形：有些形状，放大多少倍都还有新的细节——长度，原来不是天生的。", en: "How long is Britain's coastline? It depends on your ruler: the shorter the ruler, the more wiggles it catches, and the longer the total — potentially without limit. From this Mandelbrot developed fractals: shapes that reveal new detail at every magnification. Length, it turns out, is not simply given." },
  { id: "math:021", zh: "三扇门后，一辆车、两只羊。你选中一扇，主持人打开另一扇露出羊，再问你换不换。直觉说换不换一样，答案却是：换，胜率从三分之一变成三分之二。当年这个答案刊出，连一些数学教授都写信说它错了。", en: "Behind three doors: one car, two goats. You pick a door; the host opens another to reveal a goat and asks if you want to switch. Intuition says it makes no difference, but switching raises your chance from one third to two thirds. When this answer was published, even some mathematics professors wrote in to say it was wrong." },
  { id: "math:022", zh: "硬币连出五次正面，下一次是不是更该出反面？不是。硬币没有记忆，每一次都是五五开。赌徒谬误最危险的地方，是它听起来像常识：我们总忍不住替随机编出一个“该轮到”的故事。", en: "A coin lands heads five times in a row — is tails now 'due'? No. The coin has no memory; every toss is fifty-fifty. The gambler's fallacy is dangerous precisely because it sounds like common sense: we cannot resist inventing a story in which randomness takes turns." },
  { id: "math:023", zh: "芝诺说阿喀琉斯永远追不上乌龟：他跑到乌龟的出发点时，乌龟又往前挪了一点，如此无穷无尽。破解它的钥匙是：无穷多个越来越短的时间段，加起来可以是有限长——箭，终究会飞到靶心。", en: "Zeno argued that Achilles can never overtake the tortoise: by the time he reaches the tortoise's starting point, it has crept a little further, and so on forever. The key that unlocks it: infinitely many ever-shorter stretches of time can add up to a finite span — the arrow does reach the target after all." },
  { id: "math:024", zh: "村里的理发师宣布：他只给“不给自己理发的人”理发。那么他该不该给自己理发？给也不是，不给也不是。罗素用这个故事说明：有些集合不能包含自己——一个听起来无害的定义，能把逻辑逼到墙角。", en: "The village barber announces he shaves only those who do not shave themselves. So should he shave himself? Either way leads to contradiction. Russell used the story to show that some sets cannot contain themselves — an innocent-sounding definition can back logic into a corner." },
  { id: "math:025", zh: "“这句话是假的。”——如果它真，它就是假；如果它假，它就成真。两千多年前克里特人埃庇米尼得斯的版本是：“所有克里特人都是骗子”，而他本人就是克里特人。一个句子，能把自己绕成死结。", en: "'This sentence is false.' If it is true, then it is false; if it is false, then it is true. Over two thousand years ago, Epimenides of Crete gave his version: 'All Cretans are liars' — spoken by a Cretan. A single sentence can tie itself into a knot." },
  { id: "math:026", zh: "抛硬币，第一次出正面得 2 元，第二次才出得 4 元，第三次 8 元……期望值算下来是无穷大。可几乎没有人愿意为玩一次掏出大价钱。数学说它值无限，钱包说不——这个悖论逼出了“效用”的概念：钱的多少和钱的分量，不是一回事。", en: "Toss a coin: heads on the first throw pays 2, on the second pays 4, on the third 8, and so on — the expected value works out to infinity. Yet almost nobody would pay much to play once. Mathematics says it is worth infinitely much; the wallet disagrees. The paradox forced out the idea of 'utility': the amount of money and the weight of money are not the same thing." },
  { id: "math:027", zh: "辛普森悖论：一所大学总体录取率显示女生更高，逐个院系看却处处是男生更高——原来女生多报了录取率低的热门院系。分组与合并，结论可以整个反转。看数据之前，先看数据是怎么分堆的。", en: "Simpson's paradox: a university's overall admission rate favours women, yet department by department men fare better — because women applied in greater numbers to the most competitive departments. Group the data one way or another, and the conclusion can flip entirely. Before reading numbers, look at how they were sorted." },
  { id: "math:028", zh: "买彩票中头奖的概率，常常比一个人一生中被闪电击中的概率还低。把所有奖级按概率加权平均，一张彩票的期望回报通常只有票面价的一半左右。彩票卖的从来不是投资，而是那几天里，一个便宜的白日梦。", en: "The odds of a lottery jackpot are often lower than the odds of being struck by lightning in a lifetime. Weight every prize by its probability, and a ticket's expected return is typically only about half its price. What a lottery sells was never an investment — it is a few days of inexpensive daydreaming." },
  { id: "math:029", zh: "相传少年高斯的老师想让全班安静一节课，布置下 1 加到 100。高斯几乎立刻交卷：他把首尾两两相加，1+100、2+99……五十对 101，答案 5050。故事未必字字属实，但那个配对的方法，确实是天才的样子。", en: "Legend has it that young Gauss's teacher, wanting a quiet lesson, set the class to add the numbers from 1 to 100. Gauss handed in his answer almost at once: pairing first with last — 1+100, 2+99 — gives fifty pairs of 101, totalling 5050. The story may not be true in every detail, but that pairing trick is exactly what genius looks like." },
  { id: "math:030", zh: "哈代去医院看望拉马努金，说自己坐的出租车牌号 1729 “很无趣”。病床上的人立刻回答：不，它是最小的、能用两种方式写成两个立方数之和的数。1729 = 1³ + 12³，也等于 9³ + 10³。对数字的亲近，能到这个地步。", en: "Hardy visited Ramanujan in hospital and remarked that his taxi's number, 1729, was rather dull. From his bed Ramanujan replied at once: no — it is the smallest number expressible as a sum of two cubes in two different ways: 1729 = 1³ + 12³ = 9³ + 10³. Intimacy with numbers can reach this far." },
  { id: "math:031", zh: "传说叙拉古国王怀疑金匠在王冠里掺了银，阿基米德泡澡时看见水溢出，跳起来大喊“我发现了”。故事多半是后人附会，但用排水量测体积的思路是真的——灵感的那场澡，他大概确实泡过。", en: "Legend says the king of Syracuse suspected his goldsmith of mixing silver into a crown, and that Archimedes, watching bathwater overflow, leapt out crying 'Eureka!'. The tale is likely embellished, but the idea — measuring volume by displaced water — is real; the inspirational bath, he probably did take." },
  { id: "math:032", zh: "三国时期的刘徽用“割圆术”求圆周率：把圆内接多边形的边数一再翻倍，96 边、192 边……“割之弥细，所失弥少”。他算到 3.1416。祖冲之正是沿着他这条路，再往前走。", en: "In the third century, Liu Hui computed pi by exhaustion: doubling the sides of an inscribed polygon — 96, 192 — 'the finer the cutting, the smaller the loss'. He reached 3.1416. Zu Chongzhi later walked further down the very road Liu Hui had opened." },
  { id: "math:033", zh: "南宋秦九韶在《数书九章》里系统解决了同余问题：物不知数，“三三数之剩二，五五数之剩三，七七数之剩二”，问物几何。他的“大衍求一术”给出完整算法，比欧洲同类成果早了几百年——中国剩余定理，从一道算术题开始。", en: "In his Mathematical Treatise in Nine Sections, the Song-dynasty mathematician Qin Jiushao systematically solved congruence problems — 'counted by threes, two remain; by fives, three; by sevens, two: how many things are there?' His algorithm, the Dayan method, preceded comparable European work by centuries. The Chinese remainder theorem began as an arithmetic riddle." },
  { id: "math:034", zh: "钢琴上相邻两个键的频率比，恒为 2 的十二次方根。算出它的人是明代王子朱载堉：1580 年代，他用算盘把这个无理数算到小数点后二十多位，还造出按此定律的乐器。音乐要能自由转调，先得有人把这个数算出来。", en: "On a piano, the frequency ratio between neighbouring keys is always the twelfth root of two. The man who first computed it was the Ming prince Zhu Zaiyu: in the 1580s he calculated the irrational number to more than twenty decimal places on an abacus, and built instruments tuned by it. For music to change keys freely, someone first had to do this sum." },
  { id: "math:035", zh: "希帕蒂娅是亚历山大城最后的学者之一，讲授数学与天文，注释过丢番图和阿波罗尼奥斯的著作。约 415 年，她在宗教冲突中被暴徒杀害。随着她死去，古典学术的一盏灯熄了——她是古代最著名的女数学家。", en: "Hypatia was among the last scholars of Alexandria, teaching mathematics and astronomy and writing commentaries on Diophantus and Apollonius. Around 415 she was murdered by a mob amid religious conflict. With her death, a lamp of classical learning went out — she is the most famous woman mathematician of the ancient world." },
  { id: "math:036", zh: "爱米·诺特改写了现代代数，还证明了物理学里对称性与守恒律之间的深刻联系——诺特定理。她长期无薪任教，因为大学不肯给女性正式教席。爱因斯坦曾称赞她是自女性接受高等教育以来最杰出的数学天才。天赋从不缺席，缺席的常常是席位。", en: "Emmy Noether reshaped modern algebra and proved the deep link between symmetry and conservation laws in physics — Noether's theorem. For years she lectured unpaid, because the university would not grant a woman a proper chair. Einstein praised her as the most significant mathematical genius since women began receiving higher education. Talent was never absent; the chair was." },
  { id: "math:037", zh: "1936 年，图灵在论文里设想了一台假想的机器：一盘无限长的纸带、一个读写头。计算机还没有影子，他先画出了“计算”本身的模样，还证明有些问题任何机器都解不了。后来的每一台电脑，都住在他这张图纸里。", en: "In 1936 Turing imagined a hypothetical machine in a paper: an infinitely long tape and a read-write head. Before any computer existed, he had drawn the shape of computation itself — and proved some problems no machine can ever solve. Every computer since lives inside that blueprint." },
  { id: "math:038", zh: "埃尔德什一生没有固定住所，提着箱子在数学家之间流浪，敲开门就说：“我的大脑敞开了。”他与五百多人合著过论文，数学界用“埃尔德什数”衡量一个人与他的远近。他说，数学家是把咖啡变成定理的机器。", en: "Paul Erdős owned no home; he drifted between mathematicians with a suitcase, knocking on doors with 'My brain is open.' He co-authored papers with more than five hundred people, and the field measures distance from him by one's 'Erdős number'. A mathematician, he said, is a machine for turning coffee into theorems." },
  { id: "math:039", zh: "玛雅人独立发明了 0，还用二十进制记数：数到 19 之后进位，历法计算精密得惊人。在美洲的丛林里，数学走了一条与旧大陆完全不同的路——好的想法，不止一个出生地。", en: "The Maya independently invented zero, and counted in base twenty, carrying over after 19; their calendar calculations were astonishingly precise. In the jungles of the Americas, mathematics took a road entirely separate from the Old World's — good ideas have more than one birthplace." },
  { id: "math:040", zh: "我们今天写的数字源起印度，经阿拉伯世界西传。1202 年，斐波那契在《计算之书》里向欧洲力荐这套记数法，可佛罗伦萨 1299 年还下令禁止银行使用“0”，怕人篡改。新记数法赢下旧世界，用了几百年。", en: "The digits we write today began in India and travelled west through the Arab world. In 1202 Fibonacci championed them to Europe in his Liber Abaci — yet Florence still banned banks from using '0' in 1299, fearing forgery. The new notation took centuries to win over the old world." },
];
/* ---------- Masterpieces (bilingual) ---------- */
const ART = [
  { id: "art:001", zh: "《星月夜》是梵高在法国圣雷米的疗养院里画的：1889 年，他透过病房朝东的窗，看见清晨的星空和翻卷的云。画里那颗最亮的星是金星——他画的不是幻觉，是望得太久之后的真实。", en: "The Starry Night was painted by Van Gogh in an asylum at Saint-Rémy in 1889, from the east-facing window of his room: morning stars and rolling clouds. The brightest 'star' in it is Venus — he painted not a hallucination, but what reality looks like after you have stared at it long enough." },
  { id: "art:002", zh: "《蒙娜丽莎》是达·芬奇晚年最放不下的作品：他带着它从意大利到法国，反复修改了十多年，直到去世。如今它藏在卢浮宫，每年在特制防弹玻璃后迎接数百万目光——一幅小小的木板油画，成了全世界最著名的脸。", en: "The Mona Lisa was the work Leonardo could never let go: he carried it from Italy to France and kept refining it for over a decade, until his death. Now in the Louvre, it receives millions of gazes a year from behind special bulletproof glass — a small oil painting on poplar wood that became the most famous face on Earth." },
  { id: "art:003", zh: "《千里江山图》长近 12 米，画它的王希孟只有 18 岁。他是宋徽宗亲自指点过的画院学生，约半年画完这卷青绿山水，此后史书里再无他的消息，只留下少年早逝的猜测。整幅江山，是一个少年留给世界的全部。", en: "A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains runs nearly 12 metres long, and Wang Ximeng, who painted it, was only 18. A student personally guided by Emperor Huizong, he finished the blue-green scroll in about half a year — then vanishes from the records, leaving only the guess that he died young. An entire realm of rivers and mountains is all a teenager left the world." },
  { id: "art:004", zh: "《清明上河图》里画了八百多个人物：挑担的、赶驴的、桥上看热闹的，虹桥下正有大船险险过桥。张择端画的不是风景，是北宋汴京一整天的市井生活。热闹之外，桥边的惊险也埋着盛世的隐忧。", en: "Along the River During the Qingming Festival holds more than eight hundred figures: porters, donkey drivers, crowds on the bridge — and below Rainbow Bridge a great boat is narrowly squeezing through. Zhang Zeduan painted not scenery but a full day of street life in the Northern Song capital. Beneath the bustle, the peril at the bridge hints at worries inside the prosperity." },
  { id: "art:005", zh: "东晋永和九年，王羲之在兰亭酒后写下《兰亭序》，涂改之处都舍不得重抄。相传唐太宗爱极此帖，命人临摹多本分赐臣下，真迹则随他陪葬昭陵——“天下第一行书”的真身，从此只活在临本里。", en: "In 353, at the Orchid Pavilion, Wang Xizhi wrote the Preface to the Orchid Pavilion Collection after wine, unwilling to recopy even its corrections. Legend has it that Emperor Taizong of Tang loved it so much he had copies made for his ministers and took the original with him into his tomb at Zhaoling — the true body of 'the finest running script under heaven' has lived only in copies ever since." },
  { id: "art:006", zh: "黄公望的《富春山居图》在明代遭火劫，被烧成两段：前段《剩山图》藏在浙江省博物馆，后段《无用师卷》藏在台北故宫博物院。2011 年，两段在台北合璧展出——一幅画隔着海峡，分开了三百多年。", en: "Huang Gongwang's Dwelling in the Fuchun Mountains was burned in two during the Ming dynasty: the front section, Remaining Mountain, is kept in Hangzhou's Zhejiang Provincial Museum, and the longer Master Wuyong Scroll in Taipei's National Palace Museum. In 2011 the two halves were exhibited together in Taipei — one painting, separated across a strait for more than three hundred years." },
  { id: "art:007", zh: "莫奈晚年双眼患白内障，世界在他眼里蒙上黄褐色。他在吉维尼的花园里画了约 250 幅睡莲，越画越大、越画越模糊——那不是风格的选择，是他真实看见的水面。后来他做了手术，颜色才重新变蓝。", en: "In his last years Monet's eyes were clouded by cataracts, and the world turned yellow-brown. In his garden at Giverny he painted some 250 water-lily canvases, growing larger and hazier — not a stylistic choice, but the water's surface as he truly saw it. Only after surgery did blue return to his colours." },
  { id: "art:008", zh: "《呐喊》不止一幅：蒙克在 1893 到 1910 年间画了四个版本，粉彩与油画都有。画中人捂住的其实是耳朵：他不是在喊，而是被自然的“巨大呐喊”吓住——蒙克在日记里写，那天晚霞如血，他感到一阵战栗穿过全身。", en: "The Scream is not one picture: Munch made four versions between 1893 and 1910, in pastel and paint. The figure is in fact covering its ears — not screaming, but stunned by nature's 'great unending scream'. In his diary Munch wrote that the sunset was like blood, and a tremor ran through him." },
  { id: "art:009", zh: "《戴珍珠耳环的少女》不是肖像画，而是一幅“特罗尼”：荷兰画家专画的表情习作，模特是谁并不重要。维米尔笔下少女回头的一瞬、唇边将语未语，让她成了“荷兰的蒙娜丽莎”——那颗耳环的光，只用了两笔白色。", en: "Girl with a Pearl Earring is not a portrait but a 'tronie', the Dutch genre of expressive character studies, where the sitter's identity does not matter. Vermeer's girl, caught mid-turn with lips parted, became 'the Dutch Mona Lisa' — and the pearl's gleam is just two strokes of white." },
  { id: "art:010", zh: "《最后的晚餐》画在米兰一座修道院食堂的墙上。达·芬奇不用当时成熟的湿壁画法，偏要在干墙上试验新配方，结果颜料几十年就开始剥落。五百年来它一直在被修复——这幅名作，从画完那天起就在慢慢消失。", en: "The Last Supper covers the wall of a refectory in Milan. Leonardo rejected the proven fresco method and experimented with a new mixture on dry plaster — within decades the paint began to flake. It has been under restoration, on and off, for five hundred years: the masterpiece began slowly disappearing almost as soon as it was finished." },
  { id: "art:011", zh: "人们常以为米开朗基罗是躺着画完西斯廷天顶画的，其实他站在特制脚手架上仰头作画，四年下来脖子僵得只能把信举过头顶看。他本自认是雕塑家，被教皇“赶鸭子上架”，却画出了文艺复兴的天花板——字面意义上的。", en: "Michelangelo is often imagined lying on his back to paint the Sistine ceiling; in fact he stood on a specially built scaffold, head tilted up, for four years, until his neck stiffened so much he had to hold letters above his head to read. He considered himself a sculptor, pressed into the job by the Pope — and produced the Renaissance's ceiling, in the literal sense." },
  { id: "art:012", zh: "1900 年，道士王圆箓在莫高窟发现藏经洞：一个小小的洞窟里，塞满五万多件从晋到宋的写经、文书和绢画。它们封存了近千年，打开之后却在几年内散往英、法、俄、日——敦煌的宝藏，成了中国学术最痛的一课。", en: "In 1900 the Taoist priest Wang Yuanlu found the Library Cave at Mogao: one small grotto crammed with over fifty thousand manuscripts, documents and paintings from the Jin to the Song. Sealed for nearly a thousand years, within a few years of opening they were scattered to Britain, France, Russia and Japan — Dunhuang's treasure became Chinese scholarship's most painful lesson." },
  { id: "art:013", zh: "梵高痴迷日本浮世绘，在巴黎买了几百张，还把歌川广重等人的画认认真真临摹成油画，边框上添了汉字。他在给弟弟的信里说，日本艺术令他快乐而振奋——后来《星月夜》里翻卷的线条，有浮世绘的影子。", en: "Van Gogh was enthralled by Japanese ukiyo-e: in Paris he bought hundreds of prints and faithfully copied works by Hiroshige and others into oil, adding Chinese characters to the borders. Japanese art, he wrote to his brother, made him happy and cheerful — and the swirling lines of The Starry Night carry its shadow." },
  { id: "art:014", zh: "1937 年，纳粹轰炸西班牙小镇格尔尼卡。毕加索在巴黎用三十五天画完同名巨作，只用黑白灰：没有血，没有飞机，只有一匹嘶鸣的马和举灯的女人。此后他拒绝让原作回到独裁统治下的西班牙，直到 1981 年才归国。", en: "In 1937 Nazi planes bombed the Spanish town of Guernica. In Paris, Picasso finished his huge canvas of the same name in thirty-five days, in black, white and grey alone: no blood, no aircraft — only a screaming horse and a woman holding a lamp. He refused to let the original return to Spain under dictatorship; it went home only in 1981." },
  { id: "art:015", zh: "“印象派”这个名字，本是报纸上的嘲讽：1874 年，评论家看到莫奈的《印象·日出》，挖苦这群画家只画“印象”。画家们索性把骂名接了过来，当作旗号——艺术史上最有名的流派，是被骂出来的。", en: "The name 'Impressionism' began as a newspaper insult: in 1874 a critic, sneering at Monet's Impression, Sunrise, mocked these painters for painting mere 'impressions'. The painters simply adopted the slur as their banner — the most famous movement in art history was named by a jeer." },
  { id: "art:016", zh: "相传南唐后主李煜怀疑大臣韩熙载装疯避世，派画家顾闳中夜里潜入他家，偷看后凭记忆画下《夜宴图》：听乐、观舞、休息、再听乐、散席，一场夜宴的五个段落全在画里。皇帝用画当监控，画家用记忆当相机。", en: "Legend says Li Yu, last ruler of the Southern Tang, suspected his minister Han Xizai of feigning dissipation to avoid court, and sent the painter Gu Hongzhong to spy on a night banquet and paint it from memory. The Night Banquet unfolds in five scenes — music, dance, rest, more music, farewells. An emperor used painting as surveillance; the painter used memory as a camera." },
  { id: "art:017", zh: "齐白石画虾，从真虾养起：他在画案旁养虾多年，盯着看虾须怎么动、虾身怎么透光。他笔下的虾身体半透明，须子却根根有力——六十岁以后，他的画风才真正大变：衰年变法，越老越辣。", en: "Qi Baishi painted shrimp by keeping live ones beside his desk for years, watching how their feelers moved and how light passed through their bodies. His painted shrimp are translucent yet every whisker is full of force — and his style transformed only after sixty: a late-life reinvention, growing bolder with age." },
  { id: "art:018", zh: "敦煌壁画里最贵的颜色是青金石蓝：这种石头古代主要产自今天的阿富汗，沿着丝绸之路运进洞窟，比黄金还贵。画工们却把它大面积刷在佛的衣服上——在敦煌，虔诚是可以按克计价的。", en: "The costliest colour in the Dunhuang murals is lapis lazuli blue: in ancient times the stone came mainly from what is now Afghanistan, carried along the Silk Road into the caves, dearer than gold. Yet painters spread it lavishly over the Buddhas' robes — at Dunhuang, devotion could be priced by the gram." },
  { id: "art:019", zh: "顾恺之的《洛神赋图》画的是曹植笔下的人神之恋：洛神在水面上来去，衣带飘举。原作早已不存，我们今天看到的是宋代临摹本——中国绘画的许多源头，都是靠一代代临摹者的手活下来的。", en: "Gu Kaizhi's Nymph of the Luo River illustrates Cao Zhi's tale of love between a man and a goddess: she drifts over the water, ribbons flying. The original is long lost; what we see today is a Song-dynasty copy. Many springs of Chinese painting survive only through the hands of copyists, generation after generation." },
  { id: "art:020", zh: "徐悲鸿画马，改掉了古人画马的程式：他研究马的骨骼与肌肉，笔下的马四蹄腾空、鬃尾飞扬，瘦而有力。他把西方的解剖与素描，装进中国的水墨里——奔马从此成了那个时代中国人自况的形象。", en: "Xu Beihong reinvented the painted horse: he studied equine bone and muscle, and his horses gallop with flying manes — lean, taut, powerful. He put Western anatomy and drawing inside Chinese ink. The running horse became an emblem by which that era of China pictured itself." },
  { id: "art:021", zh: "1824 年《第九交响曲》首演，贝多芬亲自站在台上指挥，却已几乎完全失聪。演出结束，掌声如雷，他还背对观众站着，直到一位歌手把他轻轻转过身来——他这才看见满场的欢呼。那一幕，音乐史记了两百年。", en: "At the 1824 premiere of the Ninth Symphony, Beethoven stood on stage conducting, almost completely deaf. When the applause thundered, he still faced the orchestra, until a singer gently turned him around — only then did he see the cheering hall. Music history has remembered that moment for two hundred years." },
  { id: "art:022", zh: "巴赫的《平均律键盘曲集》被称为钢琴文献的“旧约圣经”：他用两卷、四十八首前奏曲与赋格，走遍所有大小调，证明新调律系统下每个调都能用。一套练习曲，同时也是一次数学与听觉的宣言。", en: "Bach's Well-Tempered Clavier is called the 'Old Testament' of piano literature: in two books, forty-eight preludes and fugues run through every major and minor key, proving the new tuning system made them all usable. A set of exercises that is also a manifesto of mathematics and the ear." },
  { id: "art:023", zh: "1978 年出土的曾侯乙编钟共 65 件，最奇的是“一钟双音”：敲击同一个钟的不同位置，能发出两个乐音。两千四百年前的铸造师，已经把声学写进了青铜。整套编钟音域跨五个半八度，至今还能演奏。", en: "The bianzhong bells of Marquis Yi of Zeng, unearthed in 1978, number 65 — and the marvel is 'one bell, two tones': striking different spots on a single bell sounds two distinct notes. Bronze-founders of 2,400 years ago had written acoustics into metal. The set spans five and a half octaves and can still be played today." },
  { id: "art:024", zh: "传说伯牙鼓琴，钟子期总能听出他心里想的是高山还是流水。子期死后，伯牙摔琴绝弦，终身不再弹——“知音”这个词，就是这样来的。故事未必是史实，但中国人对理解的全部期待，都装进了这一个词。", en: "Legend tells that when Bo Ya played the qin, Zhong Ziqi could always hear whether his mind was on high mountains or flowing water. After Ziqi died, Bo Ya smashed his instrument and never played again — the origin of the Chinese word zhiyin, 'one who knows the tone'. The tale may not be history, but everything China hopes for in being understood is packed into that one word." },
  { id: "art:025", zh: "斯特拉迪瓦里一生做了约一千件弦乐器，存世约六百件，顶级名琴拍出过上千万美元。三百年过去，人们用尽现代仪器分析他的木材与清漆，仍说不清那声音的全部秘密——有些工艺，失传就是失传。", en: "Stradivari made about a thousand stringed instruments in his life; some six hundred survive, and the finest have fetched over ten million dollars at auction. Three centuries on, modern instruments have analysed his wood and varnish exhaustively, yet the sound's full secret remains unexplained — some crafts, once lost, are lost." },
  { id: "art:026", zh: "柴可夫斯基的《1812 序曲》是为纪念俄国击退拿破仑而作，乐谱上真的写了大炮：高潮处要用真炮轰鸣。如今音乐厅里多用录音或礼炮代替，但户外演出时，真炮上场仍是保留节目。", en: "Tchaikovsky's 1812 Overture, written to mark Russia's repulse of Napoleon, literally scores cannon fire: at the climax, real cannons are called for. Concert halls today substitute recordings or salutes, but at outdoor performances the genuine guns remain the main event." },
  { id: "art:027", zh: "帕格尼尼的琴技高得不像人：他能在断了三根弦后，只用一根弦演完整场。观众怀疑他与魔鬼交易，他的经纪人顺势炒作，他本人乐得神秘——天才加上谣言，是最早的明星人设之一。", en: "Paganini's technique seemed inhuman: he could finish a concert on a single string after the other three snapped. Audiences suspected a pact with the devil; his manager happily fed the rumour, and Paganini enjoyed the mystery. Genius plus gossip — one of the earliest celebrity personas." },
  { id: "art:028", zh: "肖邦 39 岁病逝于巴黎，葬在拉雪兹公墓，但他的心脏按遗愿被姐姐带回华沙，封存在圣十字教堂的柱子里。他生前怕被活埋，嘱咐死后取出心脏——身体留在异乡，心要回波兰。", en: "Chopin died in Paris at 39 and was buried at Père Lachaise, but by his wish his sister carried his heart back to Warsaw, where it rests sealed inside a pillar of the Holy Cross Church. He had feared being buried alive and asked that his heart be removed after death — his body stayed abroad; his heart went home to Poland." },
  { id: "art:029", zh: "标准钢琴有 88 个键：52 个白键、36 个黑键，音域七个多八度。这个数目是 19 世纪末定型的——再宽，人手够不着；再窄，大作曲家的音不够用。88，是乐器与人的手谈判出的结果。", en: "A standard piano has 88 keys — 52 white, 36 black — spanning a little over seven octaves. The number settled in the late nineteenth century: wider, and hands could not reach; narrower, and the great composers ran out of notes. Eighty-eight is the truce negotiated between the instrument and the human hand." },
  { id: "art:030", zh: "莫扎特临终前在写《安魂曲》：一位神秘人上门订制，他至死不知对方是谁。乐曲没写完，他 35 岁去世，由学生续成。至于“萨列里毒杀莫扎特”，是后世戏剧的演绎，史学界基本不认——嫉妒的故事，比真相传得远。", en: "Mozart spent his last days writing his Requiem, commissioned by a mysterious stranger he never identified. He died at 35 with the work unfinished; a pupil completed it. As for 'Salieri poisoned Mozart' — that is theatre, not history, and scholars broadly reject it. The jealousy story simply travels farther than the truth." },
  { id: "art:031", zh: "琵琶的名字来自弹法：向前弹叫“批”，向后挑叫“把”，合起来就是琵琶。它从西域沿丝绸之路来到中国，在敦煌壁画里反弹琵琶的飞天，抱着的正是它的祖辈——一件乐器，走完了一条商路才变成国乐。", en: "The pipa is named after its own strokes: pi, plucking forward, and pa, flicking back. It travelled from Central Asia into China along the Silk Road; the apsaras in Dunhuang's murals, playing the pipa behind their backs, hold its ancestors. One instrument crossed a trade route and became a national voice." },
  { id: "art:032", zh: "爵士乐的灵魂是即兴：乐手们在既定的和弦框架里轮流“说话”，谁也不知道下一句会落在哪里。阿姆斯特朗说过，爵士若还要问便永远不会懂——有些音乐，是在演奏的当下才被写出来的。", en: "The soul of jazz is improvisation: within an agreed chord framework, the players take turns 'speaking', and no one knows where the next sentence will land. Armstrong is said to have remarked that if you have to ask what jazz is, you'll never know — some music is composed in the very moment it is played." },
  { id: "art:033", zh: "成名之前，披头士在汉堡的酒吧里驻唱了两年多：每晚演七八个小时，练出了日后横扫世界的台风。列侬说，乐队是在利物浦出生的，在汉堡长大的。台上的一分钟，台下先熬过上万个小时。", en: "Before fame, the Beatles spent over two years playing residencies in Hamburg bars — seven or eight hours a night, forging the stagecraft that would conquer the world. Lennon said the band was born in Liverpool but grew up in Hamburg. One minute on stage was bought with ten thousand hours below it." },
  { id: "art:034", zh: "毕达哥拉斯发现，琴弦长度成简单整数比时，声音最和谐：2:1 是八度，3:2 是五度。他由此相信宇宙也按数字的和声运转，提出“天体音乐”。数学与音乐的第一次相遇，是在琴弦上。", en: "Pythagoras found that strings whose lengths form simple whole-number ratios sound most consonant: 2:1 gives the octave, 3:2 the fifth. He came to believe the cosmos itself runs on numerical harmony, and proposed a 'music of the spheres'. Mathematics and music first met on a vibrating string." },
  { id: "art:035", zh: "《二泉映月》是盲艺人阿炳在街头拉了一辈子的曲子。1950 年，音乐家专程为他录音，他已病重，只留下六首曲子，几个月后去世。要不是那次抢救式的录音，这首二胡名曲，就随他一起消失了。", en: "Erquan Yingyue — Moon Reflected on the Second Spring — was a tune the blind street musician Abing played all his life. In 1950 musicians recorded him as a rescue effort; already gravely ill, he left only six pieces and died months later. Without that recording, this erhu masterpiece would have vanished with him." },
  { id: "art:036", zh: "帕赫贝尔的《卡农》只用一条和弦进行循环往复，写成后沉寂了近三百年，20 世纪才被翻出来，如今成了婚礼的标配。一段低音线条重复到底，三把小提琴在上面织出无穷变化——极简的种子，开花最晚。", en: "Pachelbel's Canon is built on a single chord progression looping without end; after it was written it slept for nearly three centuries before the twentieth century revived it into a wedding standard. One bass line repeats while three violins weave endless variation above it — the most minimal of seeds, and the latest to bloom." },
  { id: "art:037", zh: "巴塞罗那的圣家堂 1882 年动工，高迪接手后改了全部设计，他知道自己看不到完工：“我的客户（上帝）不着急。”一百四十多年过去，它仍在建，是世界上建造时间最长的教堂之一——慢，本身成了它的风格。", en: "Barcelona's Sagrada Família broke ground in 1882; Gaudí took over and redesigned everything, knowing he would never see it finished: 'My client — God — is in no hurry.' More than 140 years on it is still rising, among the longest-building churches in the world. Slowness itself became its style." },
  { id: "art:038", zh: "悉尼歌剧院的“贝壳”，灵感来自建筑师伍重剥开的一瓣橘子。工程严重超支，他中途愤然退出，至死没再回澳大利亚看一眼成品。帆影落在海港上成了国家象征，作者却终身缺席——作品与作者，有时就这样分手。", en: "The Sydney Opera House's 'shells' were inspired by a segment of orange its architect, Jørn Utzon, had peeled. The project ran far over budget; Utzon resigned midway in anger and never returned to Australia to see it finished. Its sails became a national symbol while their author stayed away for life — sometimes a work and its maker part like that." },
  { id: "art:039", zh: "埃菲尔铁塔原是为 1889 年世博会搭的临时建筑，执照只有二十年。到期时它没被拆，因为塔顶装了无线电天线，军方说有用。一座被巴黎艺术家们联名骂作“丑陋铁架”的塔，靠着一根天线活了下来，如今是法国的代名词。", en: "The Eiffel Tower was a temporary structure for the 1889 World's Fair, licensed for only twenty years. When the term expired it survived because a radio antenna on top had made it useful to the military. A tower Parisian artists had jointly denounced as an ugly iron frame was saved by an aerial — and is now synonymous with France." },
  { id: "art:040", zh: "比萨斜塔从第三层就开始歪：地基一边是软土，塔身一点点沉下去。工匠将错就错，把上层往回扳，塔便弯成了香蕉形。传说伽利略在塔上做过落球实验，多半不实——但斜塔确实是靠“斜而不倒”活成了奇迹。", en: "The Leaning Tower of Pisa began to tilt by its third storey: soft soil under one side of the foundation let it sink, course by course. The builders compensated by curving the upper levels back, and the tower bent like a banana. Galileo's falling-spheres experiment from its top is most likely legend — but the tower truly became a wonder by leaning without falling." },
  { id: "art:041", zh: "泰姬陵是莫卧儿皇帝沙贾汗为亡妻建的陵墓：两万工匠、二十二年，白色大理石上嵌满宝石花纹。陵墓两侧还各有一座几乎对称的建筑配着——一座是清真寺，另一座只为对称而建，几乎从不使用。思念，有时讲究到这个地步。", en: "The Taj Mahal is the tomb the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan built for his beloved wife: twenty thousand craftsmen, twenty-two years, white marble inlaid with gemstone flowers. It is flanked by two near-identical buildings — one a mosque, the other built purely for symmetry and almost never used. Longing can be this exacting." },
  { id: "art:042", zh: "吴哥窟是世界上最大的宗教建筑群，供奉的先是印度教的毗湿奴，后来转为佛教。它的五座塔象征须弥山的五峰，护城河象征大海——一整座宇宙，被人用石头在丛林里复刻了出来，又被丛林藏了几百年。", en: "Angkor Wat is the largest religious monument in the world, dedicated first to the Hindu god Vishnu and later turned to Buddhism. Its five towers stand for the five peaks of Mount Meru and its moat for the cosmic ocean — an entire universe rebuilt in stone in the jungle, then hidden by that jungle for centuries." },
  { id: "art:043", zh: "赵州桥由隋代匠人李春设计，已在洨河上站了约一千四百年。它首创“敞肩拱”：大拱两肩各开两个小拱，泄洪又减重。桥身历经多次大地震和洪水，至今还在通行——最长寿的设计，往往最简洁。", en: "The Zhaozhou Bridge, designed by the Sui-dynasty craftsman Li Chun, has stood over the Xiao River for some 1,400 years. It pioneered the open-spandrel arch: two small arches in each shoulder of the great arch, easing floods and shedding weight. Through earthquakes and deluges it still carries traffic — the longest-lived designs are often the simplest." },
  { id: "art:044", zh: "应县木塔高 67 米多，全部用木头建成，相传通身不用一根铁钉，靠的是榫卯咬合。近一千年里它挨过地震、炮击和雷击，塔身微微倾斜却不倒。木头比石头柔软，有时候，柔软的反而站得久。", en: "The Yingxian Wooden Pagoda rises over 67 metres, built entirely of timber — legend says without a single iron nail, held together by mortise-and-tenon joints. In nearly a thousand years it has survived earthquakes, shelling and lightning, leaning slightly but never falling. Wood is softer than stone; sometimes the softer thing stands longer." },
  { id: "art:045", zh: "福建土楼是客家人聚族而居的堡垒：一座圆楼里住着几十户同姓人家，外墙是夯土，厚可两米，只向内开窗。冷战时期，卫星照片里的土楼曾被误认作导弹发射井——一座为防御而生的民居，长得太像武器。", en: "The Fujian tulou are fortresses the Hakka built to live in as a clan: one round earthen building houses dozens of households of the same surname, its rammed-earth walls up to two metres thick, windows facing only inward. During the Cold War, satellite photos of tulou were reportedly mistaken for missile silos — homes built for defence that looked too much like weapons." },
  { id: "art:046", zh: "布达拉宫依红山而建，宫体顺着山势层层收上去，墙基最厚处达数米。它始建于唐代松赞干布时期，现存主体是五世达赖在 17 世纪重建的。十三层楼、数千房间，整座山就是它的地基——建筑与山，在这里分不出彼此。", en: "The Potala Palace climbs Red Hill course by course with the slope, its walls several metres thick at the base. First raised under Songtsen Gampo in the Tang era, its present body is the 17th-century rebuilding of the Fifth Dalai Lama. Thirteen storeys and thousands of rooms, with the mountain itself as foundation — here, building and hill cannot be told apart." },
  { id: "art:047", zh: "罗马万神殿的穹顶是无钢筋混凝土浇筑的，跨度约 43 米，两千年过去仍是同类中的巨无霸。穹顶正中的圆洞是它唯一的光源，雨水就从洞里落在地面上——古罗马人把天气，也设计进了神庙。", en: "The Pantheon's dome in Rome is poured unreinforced concrete spanning about 43 metres; two thousand years on, it remains a giant of its kind. The open oculus at its crown is the temple's only source of light, and rain falls straight through it onto the floor — the Romans designed even the weather into their temple." },
  { id: "art:048", zh: "圣索菲亚大教堂 537 年建成时，它的穹顶像浮在空中：阳光从穹顶根部的一圈窗户涌进来，圆顶仿佛悬空。近一千五百年里，它做过大教堂、清真寺、博物馆，如今又是清真寺——一座建筑，先后装下了几个帝国的心脏。", en: "When Hagia Sophia was completed in 537, its dome seemed to float: light pours in through a ring of windows at the dome's base, leaving it apparently suspended. Across nearly fifteen centuries it has served as cathedral, mosque, museum and mosque again — one building that has held the hearts of several empires in turn." },
  { id: "art:049", zh: "故宫的太和殿坐落在三层汉白玉台基上，是明清两代举行大典的地方。民间爱说故宫有九千九百九十九间半房，其实按间数统计约八千多间——“半间”的传说，是百姓替皇帝留的分寸：不敢比天多。", en: "The Forbidden City's Hall of Supreme Harmony stands on a triple terrace of white marble, where Ming and Qing emperors held their grandest ceremonies. Folklore credits the palace with 9,999 and a half rooms; the actual count is around eight thousand and more. The legendary 'half room' is the people's tact: the emperor must not outnumber heaven." },
  { id: "art:050", zh: "流水别墅直接建在一条瀑布上：赖特把房子压进溪谷，阳台像岩石一样层层探出，坐在客厅里能听见水声，却看不见瀑布。委托人起初想“看着瀑布住”，赖特说：要和瀑布一起生活。建成后，它被称作美国最伟大的住宅。", en: "Fallingwater stands directly on top of a waterfall: Wright pressed the house into the ravine, its terraces jutting out like rock ledges, so that from the living room you hear the water but cannot see it. The client had wanted to live looking at the falls; Wright said: live with them. It is now called the greatest American house." },
  { id: "art:051", zh: "蓬皮杜中心把“五脏六腑”全翻在外面：扶梯、管道、通风管都挂在外墙上，还按功能涂成不同颜色，蓝是空气、绿是水、黄是电。1977 年它开业时，巴黎人骂它像炼油厂——后来，骂声变成了昵称。", en: "The Centre Pompidou wears its insides outside: escalators, pipes and ducts hang on the façade, colour-coded by function — blue for air, green for water, yellow for electricity. When it opened in 1977, Parisians jeered that it looked like an oil refinery. In time, the jeer softened into a nickname." },
  { id: "art:052", zh: "帕特农神庙看起来笔直，其实几乎没有一条直线：柱子微微内倾，基座中央隆起，边角的柱子特意加粗。这些“视觉矫正”是为了让眼睛觉得它直——希腊人两千五百年前就懂得：完美，要为错觉让路。", en: "The Parthenon looks perfectly straight, yet it contains almost no straight lines: its columns lean slightly inward, the platform swells at the centre, and the corner columns are made thicker. These 'optical refinements' exist so the eye will judge it straight — the Greeks understood, 2,500 years ago, that perfection must make room for illusion." },
  { id: "art:053", zh: "兵马俑出土时是彩色的：颜料在接触空气后很快氧化剥落，我们才以为它们天生土黄。八千件陶俑面孔各异，据说没有两张完全相同。为一位皇帝守陵的军队，每个人都有一张自己的脸。", en: "The Terracotta Warriors were painted when buried: their pigments oxidised and flaked away on contact with air, which is why we imagine them earth-toned by nature. The eight thousand figures have distinct faces — it is said no two are exactly alike. An army guarding one emperor's tomb, and every soldier has his own face." },
  { id: "art:054", zh: "米开朗基罗开始雕《大卫》时只有 26 岁。那块大理石已被两位前辈放弃，在仓库里躺了几十年。他偏要接手：三年后，少年大卫站在佛罗伦萨——后来人们说，那块“废料”里一直睡着一个人。", en: "Michelangelo was only 26 when he began the David. The marble block had been abandoned by two sculptors before him and had lain in storage for decades. He insisted on taking it on; three years later, the young David stood in Florence. People later said a figure had been sleeping inside that 'scrap' all along." },
  { id: "art:055", zh: "《断臂的维纳斯》1820 年在米洛斯岛出土时，就已经没有双臂了，此后所有补臂的尝试都显得多余。人们渐渐接受：残缺不是她的缺陷，而是她的一部分——美，有时要靠留白完成。", en: "When the Venus de Milo was unearthed on Melos in 1820, her arms were already gone, and every attempt to restore them has felt superfluous. People gradually accepted that the loss is not her defect but part of her — sometimes beauty is completed by what is missing." },
  { id: "art:056", zh: "三星堆的青铜纵目面具，双眼凸出成柱，最长的一件眼柱伸出十几厘米，耳朵大得夸张。三千年前的古蜀人为什么这样造神，没有文字留下答案。它越是沉默，越让人想一直看下去。", en: "The bronze mask with protruding eyes from Sanxingdui stares with pupils on stalks — the longest projecting well over ten centimetres — and ears of exaggerated size. Why the ancient Shu, three thousand years ago, shaped a god this way, no text survives to tell. The more silent it is, the longer we keep looking." },
  { id: "art:057", zh: "东汉铜奔马三足腾空，一足踏在飞燕背上——燕子还没反应过来，马已经过去了。工匠把千钧之力，平衡在一个马蹄的落点上。它后来成为中国旅游业的标志：一只蹄子，撑起整个速度。", en: "The Eastern Han bronze galloping horse flies with three hooves in the air and the fourth resting on a swallow's back — the bird has not yet noticed before the horse is past. The craftsman balanced all that power on a single hoof's landing point. It later became the emblem of China's tourism industry: one hoof, holding up all that speed." },
  { id: "art:058", zh: "青花瓷的蓝来自钴料：元代景德镇的工匠发现，从西域来的钴在白瓷上烧出稳定的蓝。从此青花走遍世界，欧洲王室以藏青花为荣——中国瓷器的英文 china，小写的瓷器和大写的中国，是同一个词。", en: "Blue-and-white porcelain takes its blue from cobalt: Yuan-dynasty potters at Jingdezhen found that cobalt from the western regions fired into a steady blue on white porcelain. From there it travelled the world, and European royalty prized it — in English, china the ware and China the country are the same word." },
  { id: "art:059", zh: "金缕玉衣是汉代帝王的葬服：两千多片玉片用金丝编缀成人形。古人相信玉能防腐保身，穿着它下葬，就能不朽。玉衣没能留住肉身，却留住了那个时代手艺的顶点——两千多年后，它仍完好如初。", en: "The jade burial suit, sewn with gold thread, was the funeral dress of Han emperors: over two thousand jade plaques linked into human form. The ancients believed jade preserved the body from decay, and that to be buried in it was to be incorruptible. The suit failed to keep the flesh — but it kept the summit of its age's craftsmanship, intact after two thousand years." },
  { id: "art:060", zh: "《思想者》本是《地狱之门》顶部的一个小人物：罗丹原想让他代表诗人但丁，俯瞰门里的众生。后来它被放大成独立雕塑，成了全人类思考的通用姿势——一个配角，长成了主角。", en: "The Thinker began as a small figure atop The Gates of Hell: Rodin meant him to be Dante, gazing down at the souls below. Enlarged into a free-standing sculpture, he became the universal posture of human thought — a supporting character who grew into the lead." },
];

/* ---------- Once Upon a Time (bilingual) ---------- */
const TALES = [
  { id: "tales:001", zh: "盘古是中国神话里第一个巨人：他睡在像鸡蛋一样的混沌里，醒来后把天地劈开，又用身体顶住它们一万八千年。死后，他的呼吸成了风，眼睛成了日月，血液成了江河。这个开天辟地的故事其实定型得很晚——现存最早的记载出自三国时期，远晚于许多其他神话。", en: "Pangu is the first giant of Chinese myth: he slept inside a chaos shaped like an egg, woke, and split heaven from earth, then held them apart with his body for eighteen thousand years. When he died, his breath became wind, his eyes the sun and moon, his blood the rivers. Curiously, this creation story took shape late — its earliest surviving record dates only to the Three Kingdoms period, later than many other myths." },
  { id: "tales:002", zh: "女娲用黄土仿照自己的样子捏出人来，一个一个捏太慢，她便把绳子蘸上泥浆甩动，泥点落地也成了人。后来的传说索性说：亲手捏的是贵人，绳子甩出来的是平民——古人连人间的等级，都要给它找一个神话的源头。", en: "Nuwa moulded the first humans from yellow earth in her own image. Tiring of shaping them one by one, she dipped a rope in mud and flicked it, and the flying droplets became people too. Later tellings even claimed the hand-made ones became nobles and the rope-flung ones commoners — the ancients wanted a mythic origin even for the ranks of society." },
  { id: "tales:003", zh: "水神共工败给火神祝融，一头撞断了撑天的不周山：天向西北倾斜，地向东南塌陷。女娲炼五色石补好苍天，又斩下巨龟的四只脚当柱子重新撑起四极。这个故事还顺便解释了中国地势：为什么天在西北高、江河都向东南流。", en: "Defeated by the fire god Zhurong, the water god Gonggong smashed his head into Mount Buzhou, a pillar of the sky: heaven tilted toward the northwest and the earth sank in the southeast. Nuwa smelted five-coloured stones to patch the sky and cut off a giant turtle's legs as new pillars. The myth doubles as geography — it explains why China's land stands high in the northwest and its rivers all run southeast." },
  { id: "tales:004", zh: "夸父想追上太阳，与它赛跑。他一路追到太阳落山的地方，口渴得喝干了黄河与渭河，还要去喝北方的大泽，没走到就渴死了。他扔下的手杖，化作一片桃林——中国神话里最壮烈的失败，结局是一片给后来人乘凉的树荫。", en: "Kuafu raced the sun across the sky. Reaching the place where the sun sets, parched, he drank the Yellow River and the Wei dry, then set off for the great northern lake — and died of thirst before he got there. The staff he dropped became a forest of peach trees: the grandest failure in Chinese myth ends as shade for those who came after." },
  { id: "tales:005", zh: "天上本有十个太阳，是天帝帝俊的十个儿子。它们一度同时升上天空，大地焦裂、庄稼枯死。尧派神射手羿射下九个，只留一个照明。羿的妻子，就是后来奔月的嫦娥——两个故事，原本是一对夫妻的悲剧。", en: "There were once ten suns in the sky, the ten sons of the god Dijun. When they rose all at once, the earth cracked and the crops withered. The sage-king Yao sent the archer Yi, who shot down nine and left one to give light. Yi's wife was Chang'e, who later flew to the moon — the two famous myths were originally one couple's tragedy." },
  { id: "tales:006", zh: "嫦娥是羿的妻子。羿从西王母那里求来不死药，交给她保管。药最后被嫦娥吃下，她身子变轻，飞向月亮，住进广寒宫。至于她为什么吃药，版本并不一致：有的说小人逢蒙来抢，她情急吞下；有的说她独自偷吃——两千年里，人们一直在改写她的动机。", en: "Chang'e was the wife of the archer Yi. He had obtained an elixir of immortality from the Queen Mother of the West and left it in her keeping. In the end Chang'e swallowed it, grew weightless, and floated up to the moon, to live in the Guanghan Palace. Why she took it, the versions disagree: in some, the villain Pang Meng came to steal it and she swallowed it in desperation; in others, she took it alone in secret. For two thousand years, storytellers have kept rewriting her motive." },
  { id: "tales:007", zh: "精卫本是炎帝的小女儿，在东海游泳时溺亡。她的魂魄化作一只小鸟，头上有花纹，嘴是白的，脚是红的，日夜从西山衔来树枝和石子，投进东海，发誓要把它填平。一只鸟与一片海的较量，中国人讲了两千年，讲的其实是明知不可为而为之。", en: "Jingwei was once the youngest daughter of the Flame Emperor; she drowned while swimming in the Eastern Sea. Her spirit became a small bird — patterned head, white beak, red feet — that day and night carries twigs and pebbles from the western mountains to drop into the sea, vowing to fill it. A bird against an ocean: for two thousand years China has told this story about doing what you know cannot be done.", full: "炎帝神农氏有个小女儿，名叫女娃，性子倔强。一天她独自去东海边玩耍，下水游泳，再也没有回来——东海吞没了她。\n\n女娃的魂魄不肯散去，化作一只小鸟：头上有花纹，白色的嘴，红色的脚，叫声像在喊自己的名字，人们叫它精卫。它住在发鸠山上，每天衔西山的树枝和石子，一趟一趟飞到东海上空，把它们投下去。\n\n东海那么大，一只鸟衔的石子那么小。这个故事最早记在《山海经》里，两千年过去，它填进去的石子早该沉得无影无踪——可它还在飞。精卫成了中文里一个固执的意象：力量悬殊到荒谬，也要把誓言衔到底。", fullEn: "The Flame Emperor Shennong had a youngest daughter, stubborn by nature. One day she went alone to play at the Eastern Sea, swam out — and never came back. The sea had taken her.\n\nHer spirit would not scatter. It became a small bird: patterned head, white beak, red feet, with a cry that sounded like its own name — Jingwei, the Guardian Bird. From Mount Fajiu it flies daily to the western hills, picks up a twig or a pebble, carries it over the Eastern Sea, and drops it in.\n\nThe sea is so vast, and what one bird can carry so small. The tale was first written down in the Classic of Mountains and Seas; two thousand years on, every stone it dropped has long sunk out of sight — yet it is still flying. Jingwei became one of the Chinese language's most stubborn images: the odds may be absurd, but the vow is carried to the end." },
  { id: "tales:008", zh: "太行、王屋两座大山挡在愚公家门口。九十岁的他带着子孙凿石挖土，运到渤海边。邻居智叟笑他自不量力，他说：我死了还有儿子，儿子死了还有孙子，子子孙孙没有穷尽，山却不会再长高。天帝被他的执拗感动，派大力神背走了两座山——这个出自《列子》的故事，奖励的不是力气，是耐心。", en: "Two great mountains, Taihang and Wangwu, blocked the door of Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man. At ninety he led his sons and grandsons to dig them away, basket by basket, to the sea. A neighbour nicknamed the Wise Man laughed at him; Yu Gong answered: when I die my sons remain, then grandsons — my line is endless, but the mountains will grow no taller. The Emperor of Heaven, moved, sent two strongmen to carry the mountains off. The tale, from the Liezi, rewards not strength but patience." },
  { id: "tales:009", zh: "大禹的父亲鲧治水只会堵，堵了九年，水患更重。禹改用疏导：凿山开河，引水入海。他在外十三年，三次路过家门都没进去——第一次听到妻子分娩，第二次听到儿子喊他，第三次儿子已经十多岁。中国治水的神话，最终讲的是方法比蛮力重要，以及公而忘私。", en: "Yu's father Gun fought the floods by damming, and after nine years the waters were worse. Yu changed the method: instead of blocking, he dredged and channelled, cutting paths for the water to reach the sea. In thirteen years abroad he passed his own door three times and never entered — first hearing his wife in labour, then his son calling him, then when the boy was over ten. China's great flood myth is finally about method over brute force, and public duty over private life." },
  { id: "tales:010", zh: "神农氏教先民种地，也替他们试药：他亲自品尝各种草木，分辨哪些能吃、哪些治病、哪些有毒。古书说他一天之内曾中毒七十次。神农尝百草的故事，是把整个民族试错的代价，浓缩到一个人的舌头上。", en: "Shennong, the Divine Farmer, taught the early people to sow grain — and tested medicines for them, tasting plants himself to learn which nourished, which healed, and which killed. Old books say he was poisoned seventy times in a single day. The myth compresses a whole people's trial and error onto one man's tongue." },
  { id: "tales:011", zh: "相传仓颉是黄帝的史官，他从鸟兽的足迹里得到启发，造出了文字。传说文字诞生那天，天上下起粟米雨，鬼神在夜里哭泣——因为秘密从此可以被记录，谎言也无处遁形。汉字是不是一个人造的已不可考，但古人愿意把这份功劳，记在一个仰望足迹的人名下。", en: "Legend makes Cangjie, the Yellow Emperor's scribe, the inventor of writing, inspired by the tracks of birds and beasts. On the day characters were born, the story says, grain rained from heaven and ghosts wailed in the night — because secrets could now be recorded, and lies had nowhere to hide. Whether one man invented Chinese characters is unknowable; the ancients chose to credit a man who studied footprints." },
  { id: "tales:012", zh: "织女是天帝的孙女，司掌织布，与放牛的牛郎相爱成婚，婚后却荒废了织机。天帝震怒，把两人分在银河两岸，只许每年七月初七相会一次；喜鹊感念他们的深情，搭起鹊桥。七夕因此成了中国的情人节——只是这个节日的底色，是分离而不是团圆。", en: "The Weaver Girl, granddaughter of the Heavenly Emperor, wove the sky's cloth; she fell in love with the Cowherd and married him — and then, in love, let her loom stand idle. The furious Emperor separated them across the Milky Way, allowing one meeting a year, on the seventh night of the seventh month, when magpies form a bridge for them. So Qixi became China's lovers' festival — though its true colour is separation, not reunion.", full: "天上的织女是天帝的孙女，每天坐在织机前，织出云霞做天衣。她厌倦了日复一日的劳作，私自下凡，在河边遇见了靠一头老牛过活的牛郎。两人结了婚，生儿育女，过了几年男耕女织的日子。\n\n天帝得知后大怒：织女荒废了天上的织作。他派人把织女押回天上。牛郎披上老牛临死前留下的牛皮，挑着一双儿女追上天去，眼看就要追上，王母拔下金簪，在两人之间划出一道银河，波涛滚滚，再也过不去。\n\n两人隔河相望，日日以泪洗面。喜鹊们被这份深情感动，约定每年七月初七，千千万万只喜鹊飞来，用身体搭成一座桥，让牛郎织女在桥上相会一夜。这天若是下雨，传说就是他们相会时流下的眼泪。", fullEn: "The Weaver Girl was the Heavenly Emperor's granddaughter, who sat at her loom each day weaving clouds into the sky's garments. Weary of the endless work, she slipped down to earth and met the Cowherd, a poor young man whose only wealth was an old ox. They married, had two children, and lived a few quiet years, he farming, she weaving.\n\nWhen the Emperor learned she had abandoned her heavenly loom, he had her seized and carried back to the sky. The Cowherd wrapped himself in the hide of his ox — which had told him before dying to keep it for a time of need — took up the two children in baskets, and chased after her. He was almost there when the Queen Mother drew out her golden hairpin and scratched a river across the sky: the Milky Way, rolling with waves he could not cross.\n\nSo they gaze at each other across the river of stars. The magpies, moved by their devotion, made a pact: every year on the seventh night of the seventh month, thousands upon thousands of them fly up and form a bridge with their bodies, so the lovers can meet for one night. If it rains that evening, people say it is the tears they shed at meeting." },
  { id: "tales:013", zh: "白蛇修炼千年化作白娘子，与药铺伙计许仙在西湖断桥相遇相爱。金山寺和尚法海却认定人妖殊途，把她镇在雷峰塔下。这个故事越讲越变：明代小说里白蛇还带着妖气，清代以后却成了为爱抗争的象征——1924 年雷峰塔真的倒了，鲁迅还专门写文章庆祝。", en: "A white snake, after a thousand years of cultivation, became the lady Bai Suzhen and fell in love with the apothecary's assistant Xu Xian at Broken Bridge on the West Lake. The monk Fahai, insisting that human and spirit must not mix, imprisoned her beneath Leifeng Pagoda. The tale kept changing as it was retold: in the Ming-dynasty version she is still half a demon, but by the Qing she had become a symbol of love defying authority — when the real Leifeng Pagoda collapsed in 1924, Lu Xun wrote an essay to celebrate.", full: "钱塘一带有一条白蛇，修炼了一千八百年，与青蛇小青结伴，在清明时节化作主仆二人，游览西湖。断桥边下起雨来，药铺伙计许仙把伞借给她们，自己淋雨回去——一段姻缘，就从一把伞开始。白素贞与许仙成了亲，在镇江开起保安堂药铺，施药济人，名声很好。\n\n金山寺的法海却看穿了白素贞的真身，警告许仙：你的妻子是蛇妖。端午节，许仙按法海的话劝她喝下雄黄酒，白素贞现出原形，许仙当场吓死过去。白素贞为救丈夫，潜入昆仑山盗取灵芝仙草，与守山神将一番恶斗，终于把仙草抢回，救活了许仙。\n\n许仙病愈后被法海留在金山寺不许回家。白素贞与小青调动水族，水漫金山，与法海斗法，却因身怀有孕，法力不济，败下阵来。孩子出生后，法海用金钵将她收住，压在西湖边的雷峰塔下，说：除非西湖水干、雷峰塔倒，你不得出来。后来的版本不忍心，给了她一个团圆：儿子长大中了状元，来到塔前祭拜，白素贞终得重见天日。", fullEn: "Outside Hangzhou lived a white snake who had cultivated herself for eighteen hundred years. One spring day she and her companion, the green snake Xiaoqing, took human form as mistress and maid to tour the West Lake. Rain fell at Broken Bridge, and the apothecary's assistant Xu Xian lent them his umbrella and walked home in the rain himself — a marriage that begins with an umbrella. Bai Suzhen married Xu Xian, and together they opened a medicine shop, the Baohetang, whose good name spread.\n\nBut Fahai, abbot of Jinshan Temple, saw through her. He warned Xu Xian: your wife is a snake spirit. At the Dragon Boat Festival, following the monk's hint, Xu Xian pressed her to drink realgar wine; her true form broke through, and Xu Xian died of fright on the spot. To save him, Bai Suzhen stole into the Kunlun Mountains for the divine lingzhi herb, fought its guardians, and brought it back to revive her husband.\n\nOnce recovered, Xu Xian was detained by Fahai at Jinshan Temple. Bai Suzhen and Xiaoqing raised the waters against the temple — the famous Flooding of Jinshan — but she was heavy with child and her power failed. After the baby was born, Fahai trapped her under his golden alms bowl and imprisoned her beneath Leifeng Pagoda by the lake: until the West Lake runs dry and the pagoda falls, she would not come out. Later tellings could not bear it and granted a reunion: her son grew up, took first place in the imperial examinations, and came to worship at the tower — and Bai Suzhen saw the sky again." },
  { id: "tales:014", zh: "孟姜女的新婚丈夫万喜良被抓去修长城，冻死在工地上，尸骨埋进墙体。她千里寻夫，到了长城脚下放声大哭，哭崩了一段城墙，露出丈夫的遗骨。这个故事的种子在《左传》里只有短短一笔，汉代的《列女传》里她已经哭崩了一座城墙，到了唐代，人们又把那座城墙换成了秦始皇的长城——民间用一千多年，把一个寡妇的眼泪熬成了对苛政最有力的控诉。", en: "Meng Jiangnü's new husband was seized to build the Great Wall, froze to death at the works, and was buried inside the wall itself. She travelled a thousand li to find him, and at the foot of the wall wept so bitterly that a stretch of it collapsed, revealing his bones. The seed of the story is a brief passage in the Zuo Zhuan; by the Han dynasty's Biographies of Exemplary Women her weeping already brings down a city wall, and in the Tang that wall became the First Emperor's Great Wall — over a thousand years, folk telling distilled one widow's grief into the most enduring protest against tyranny." },
  { id: "tales:015", zh: "祝英台女扮男装去求学，与同窗梁山伯同吃同住三年，对方竟没看出来。临别她许下“妹妹”作媒，梁山伯赶去提亲时，祝家已把她许给马家。她在出嫁路上祭坟，坟墓裂开，她纵身跳入；随后一对蝴蝶从坟中飞出。这出东晋背景的悲剧，常被称作中国的《罗密欧与朱丽叶》，只是结局给了他们翅膀。", en: "Zhu Yingtai disguised herself as a man to attend school, and for three years shared rooms and meals with her classmate Liang Shanbo — who never guessed. Parting, she hinted he should propose to her 'sister'; by the time he came, her family had promised her to another. On her wedding procession she stopped at his grave; the tomb split open, she leapt in, and two butterflies rose from it. The Eastern Jin tragedy is often called China's Romeo and Juliet — except its ending gives the lovers wings." },
  { id: "tales:016", zh: "孤儿谢端在田边捡到一只大田螺，养在水缸里。此后他每天下地回来，家里都做好了饭、烧好了水。他躲起来偷看，才发现是田螺壳里走出的姑娘在替他操持家务。秘密被看破，姑娘留下田螺壳作粮仓，飘然离去——这个出自《搜神后记》的故事，是中国最古老的“田螺姑娘”母题，后来演化出无数报恩的版本。", en: "The orphan Xie Duan picked up an enormous river snail and kept it in his water jar. After that, every day he came home from the fields to a cooked meal and hot water. Hiding to watch, he discovered a young woman stepping out of the shell to keep house for him. With the secret seen, she could not stay; she left him the shell — a granary that never emptied — and vanished. Recorded in the Soushen Houji, this is China's oldest 'snail girl' tale, ancestor of countless stories of kindness repaid.", full: "晋安郡有个孤儿叫谢端，自小由邻居抚养长大，到了十八岁，还没娶上媳妇，只能一个人守着几亩薄田过日子。一天他在田边捡到一只大得异乎寻常的田螺，有斗那么大，便带回家，养在水缸里。\n\n从那以后，怪事接连发生：谢端每天下地回来，屋里都收拾得干干净净，灶上有热饭，壶里有热水，好像家里有个贤惠的女主人。他疑心是邻居帮忙，登门道谢，邻居却说从没来过。\n\n一天，谢端假装出门，躲在暗处偷看。只见水缸里的田螺壳缓缓打开，走出一位年轻姑娘，挽起袖子就开始生火做饭。谢端冲出来抱住田螺壳不放。姑娘红着脸说：我是天汉里的白水素女。天帝怜你自小孤苦、为人恭谨，派我来暂替你持家。本该守你十年，如今你窥破了我的行藏，我不能再留下了。\n\n她留下那只田螺壳——壳里存的米永远吃不完——便乘风而去。谢端为她立了祠，年年祭祀。", fullEn: "Xie Duan, an orphan in Jin'an, was raised by neighbours and at eighteen still had no wife, keeping a few poor fields alone. One day he found a river snail as big as a bushel basket, took it home, and kept it in his water jar.\n\nFrom then on, strange things happened: every evening he returned from the fields to a swept house, a hot meal on the stove, and warm water in the pot, as if a capable wife were keeping his home. He thanked the neighbours, suspecting their kindness — but they said they had never come.\n\nOne day he pretended to leave and hid to watch. The great shell in the jar slowly opened, and a young woman stepped out, rolled up her sleeves, and began cooking. Xie Duan rushed out and clutched the shell so she could not return to it. Blushing, she said: 'I am the White Water Maiden of the Heavenly River. The Emperor of Heaven pitied your lonely, honest life and sent me to keep your house for a time. Now that you have seen me, I cannot stay.'\n\nShe left him the shell — the rice stored in it would never run out — and departed on the wind. Xie Duan built a small shrine for her and offered sacrifices every year." },
  { id: "tales:017", zh: "三圣母爱上凡人书生，被哥哥二郎神压在华山之下。她与书生的儿子沉香长大后得知身世，拜师学艺，借母亲的宝莲灯之力，一斧劈开华山，救出母亲。这个故事里没有外敌：压山的是舅舅，劈山的是外甥——中国神话最痛的一次抗争，是在一家人之间发生的。", en: "The goddess Sanshengmu fell in love with a mortal scholar, and her own brother, the god Erlang, imprisoned her beneath Mount Hua. Her son Chenxiang grew up, learned the truth, trained under a master, and with the power of his mother's lotus lantern split the mountain open and freed her. There is no outside villain in this story: the uncle presses the mountain down, the nephew splits it — Chinese myth's most painful rebellion happens inside one family." },
  { id: "tales:018", zh: "哪吒七岁时在东海洗澡，搅得龙宫震动，与龙王三太子敖丙冲突，失手打死了他，还抽了龙筋。龙王要水淹陈塘关问罪，哪吒不肯连累父母，剔骨还父、割肉还母，自尽谢罪。师父太乙真人用莲花与莲藕为他重塑身体——中国神话里最叛逆的孩子，先用最决绝的方式还清了“父母的账”。", en: "At seven, Nezha bathed in the Eastern Sea and shook the Dragon Palace; clashing with the Dragon King's third son, Ao Bing, he killed him and pulled out his dragon tendon. When the Dragon King threatened to flood Chentang Pass in revenge, Nezha refused to implicate his parents: he returned his bones to his father and his flesh to his mother, and took his own life. His master Taiyi Zhenren rebuilt his body from lotus roots and flowers — Chinese myth's most rebellious child first settled his 'debt' to his parents in the most absolute way imaginable." },
  { id: "tales:019", zh: "孙悟空嫌天庭给的官小，先偷蟠桃、盗仙丹，又打翻太上老君的炼丹炉，与十万天兵周旋。玉帝请来如来佛祖，猴子没能翻出他的手掌心，被压在五行山下五百年。《西游记》里这段最热闹，也最清醒：一场大闹换来的不是自由，是五百年的等待——等一个去取经的人。", en: "Sun Wukong, insulted by the petty post Heaven gave him, stole the peaches of immortality, drank the elixir of the gods, and fought off a hundred thousand celestial soldiers. The Jade Emperor called in the Buddha; the monkey could not leap out of that palm, and was pinned under Five Elements Mountain for five hundred years. Journey to the West's most riotous episode is also its soberest: the great rebellion bought not freedom, but five centuries of waiting — for a pilgrim to Tang lands." },
  { id: "tales:020", zh: "黄河在晋陕之间穿过龙门山，水势湍急。相传鲤鱼逆流而上，跃过龙门的就能化身为龙，跃不过的碰得额头流血而回。后来科举兴起，“鲤鱼跃龙门”成了寒门子弟金榜题名的象征：全天下的读书人，都想做那条跃过去的鱼。", en: "Where the Yellow River forces through the Longmen mountains, the current is fierce. Legend says a carp that swims upstream and leaps the Dragon Gate turns into a dragon; those that fail fall back with bloodied foreheads. When the imperial examinations rose, 'the carp leaps the Dragon Gate' became the emblem of a poor scholar passing the exams — every student in the empire wanted to be the fish that made it over." },
  { id: "tales:021", zh: "民间传说里，玉帝选十二生肖那天，老鼠和猫本是朋友，相约一起赴会。老鼠却没叫醒猫，自己骑在牛背上，最后一刻跳到最前面，抢了第一。猫睡过了头，从此落选，还与老鼠结下世仇。这当然是后人编出来解释生肖的故事——顺便也解释了，猫为什么追老鼠追了几千年。", en: "In the folk telling, when the Jade Emperor held the race that fixed the twelve zodiac animals, the Rat and the Cat were friends and agreed to go together. The Rat never woke the Cat; it rode on the Ox's back and leapt ahead at the last moment to take first place. The Cat overslept, missed the zodiac forever, and has hated rats ever since. The story is a later invention to explain the zodiac — it also neatly explains why cats have chased rats for thousands of years." },
  { id: "tales:022", zh: "老鼠夫妇要给女儿选最强的女婿：太阳最强，云能遮日；云最强，风能吹云；风最强，墙能挡风；墙最强，老鼠能打洞。绕了一大圈，最强的还是老鼠洞里的邻居。有的版本里新郎最后是猫，喜事变丧事。这个流传极广的民间故事，画成了年画，也讲成了一个循环：强弱从来都是相对的。", en: "The mouse couple sought the strongest husband for their daughter: the Sun is strongest — but clouds cover the Sun; clouds — but wind blows them; wind — but a wall stops it; walls — but a mouse bores through them. Round the whole circle, the strongest was the neighbour in the next hole. In some versions the bridegroom turns out to be the Cat, and the wedding becomes a funeral. The widely told folk tale, also a New Year print, is a perfect loop: strength is only ever relative." },
  { id: "tales:023", zh: "叶公子高爱龙成癖：钩子、凿子、门窗、厅堂，到处刻满了龙。真龙听说后很感动，降临他家，把头探进窗户。叶公吓得魂飞魄散，掉头就跑。这个出自汉代《新序》的故事只有几十个字，却留下了一个成语：叶公好龙——爱一样东西，爱的只是它的样子。", en: "Lord Ye of the old story loved dragons to obsession: his hooks, chisels, doors, windows and halls were all carved with them. A real dragon, touched by such devotion, descended and poked its head through his window — and Lord Ye fled in terror, scared out of his wits. The tale from the Han-dynasty Xinxu is a few dozen words long, but it left a permanent idiom: 'Lord Ye loves dragons' — loving a thing only so long as it stays an image." },
  { id: "tales:024", zh: "南北朝画家张僧繇在金陵安乐寺墙上画了四条龙，都不点眼睛，说点上就会飞走。人们不信，逼他点睛：他刚点完两条，雷电大作，两条龙乘云飞去，没点的两条还留在墙上。“画龙点睛”从此成了成语——点睛之笔，是让作品活过来的那一下。", en: "The Liang-dynasty painter Zhang Sengyou painted four dragons on a temple wall in Jinling — all without eyes, warning that dotted pupils would let them fly away. Pressed by sceptics, he dotted two; thunder cracked, and those two rose on clouds and vanished, while the un-dotted pair stayed on the wall. 'Dotting the dragon's eyes' became the idiom for the final touch that brings a work to life." },
  { id: "tales:025", zh: "月亮上除了嫦娥，还有一个永远在砍树的人。吴刚学仙时犯了错，被罚到月宫砍桂树：这棵五百丈高的桂树随砍随合，斧头落下，伤口立刻长好。唐代《酉阳杂俎》记下这个故事时，吴刚已经砍了不知多少年——这是中国神话里最安静的刑罚：没有终点的重复。", en: "Beside Chang'e, the moon holds a man forever cutting a tree. Wu Gang, punished for faults committed while studying immortality, was sent to fell the cassia tree of the moon palace: five hundred zhang tall, it heals the instant the axe falls. When the Tang-dynasty miscellany Youyang Zazu recorded him, he had already been cutting for uncounted years — the quietest punishment in Chinese myth: repetition without an end." },
  { id: "tales:026", zh: "吕洞宾、铁拐李等八位仙人赴宴归来，在东海上不用船，各自亮出宝物渡海：铁拐李的葫芦、张果老的纸驴、蓝采和的花篮……“八仙过海，各显神通”成了中国人最爱用的俗语之一。这八位的名单直到明代才定型——神仙的阵容，也是民间几百年慢慢选出来的。", en: "The Eight Immortals, returning from a feast, crossed the Eastern Sea without a boat, each riding their own magic: Iron-Crutch Li's gourd, Zhang Guolao's paper donkey, Lan Caihe's flower basket. 'The Eight Immortals cross the sea, each showing their own powers' became one of China's favourite proverbs. The roster itself was only fixed in the Ming dynasty — even a pantheon's line-up was slowly voted in by centuries of folk telling." },
  { id: "tales:027", zh: "普罗米修斯同情在寒冷与黑暗中受苦的人类，从天上盗来火种送给他们。宙斯震怒，把他锁在高加索山的悬崖上，每天派鹰啄食他的肝脏，而肝脏夜里又会重新长好。盗火者的刑罚没有尽头，他的名字因此成了反抗与牺牲的代称——人类文明的第一簇火，是偷来的。", en: "Prometheus pitied humans shivering in cold and darkness, stole fire from heaven, and gave it to them. Zeus chained him to a cliff in the Caucasus, where an eagle ate his liver every day — and every night the liver grew back. The fire-thief's punishment had no end, and his name became a byword for defiance and sacrifice: the first flame of human civilisation was stolen goods." },
  { id: "tales:028", zh: "宙斯为报复盗火，把第一个女人潘多拉送给人类，并陪嫁一只大罐子，叮嘱永不可打开。好奇心终究占了上风：她掀开盖子，疾病、灾祸、忧愁一涌而出，只剩“希望”留在罐底。后人常说“潘多拉的盒子”，其实希腊原文里是陶罐——“盒子”是文艺复兴时译者伊拉斯谟改的手笔，一处误译流传了五百年。", en: "To punish mankind for Prometheus's theft, Zeus sent the first woman, Pandora, with a great jar she must never open. Curiosity won: she lifted the lid, and sickness, sorrow and every evil poured out — only Hope stayed at the bottom. We say 'Pandora's box', but in the Greek it is a storage jar, a pithos; the 'box' was Erasmus's slip in the Renaissance, a mistranslation that has now lived five hundred years." },
  { id: "tales:029", zh: "工匠代达罗斯用羽毛和蜡为自己和儿子伊卡洛斯做了翅膀，逃离克里特岛。临行前他叮嘱：飞得太低，海水会打湿羽毛；飞得太高，太阳会熔化蜡。伊卡洛斯飞上高空，得意忘形，越飞越高，蜡熔羽落，坠海而死。他的父亲飞在前面，喊他的名字时，只看见海面上的羽毛。", en: "The craftsman Daedalus made wings of feathers and wax for himself and his son Icarus, to escape from Crete. His warning was exact: fly too low and the sea spray wets the feathers; too high and the sun melts the wax. Icarus, exulting in flight, climbed and climbed — the wax melted, the feathers scattered, and he fell into the sea. His father, flying ahead, called his name and saw only feathers on the water." },
  { id: "tales:030", zh: "克里特王宫地下有一座迷宫，里面关着牛头人身的弥诺陶洛斯，雅典每年要送童男童女去喂它。王子忒修斯自愿入宫，王的女儿阿里阿德涅爱上他，送他一团线：进迷宫时把线头系在门口，杀死怪兽后循线走出。一团线救了他的命——“阿里阿德涅的线团”后来成了走出困局的代称。", en: "Beneath the palace of Crete lay a labyrinth holding the Minotaur, half man, half bull, fed each year on Athenian youths. Prince Theseus volunteered to go; the king's daughter Ariadne fell in love with him and gave him a ball of thread — tie it at the door, kill the monster, and follow the thread back out. A ball of string saved his life, and 'Ariadne's thread' is still our phrase for the clue that leads out of an impossible maze." },
  { id: "tales:031", zh: "美杜莎是蛇发女妖，看她一眼就会变成石头。珀尔修斯接受挑战，却不直视她：他借雅典娜的铜盾当镜子，看着倒影挥剑，斩下她的头。美杜莎颈中飞出的，是长着翅膀的白马珀伽索斯。最危险的东西不能直视——希腊人把这条生存经验，写进了一个怪物的死法里。", en: "Medusa was a serpent-haired monster whose gaze turned men to stone. Perseus took up the challenge without ever looking at her directly: he watched her reflection in Athena's polished bronze shield and struck off her head by the mirrored image. From her neck sprang the winged horse Pegasus. The most dangerous things cannot be faced head-on — the Greeks wrote that survival rule into a monster's death." },
  { id: "tales:032", zh: "赫拉克勒斯被天后赫拉施了疯病，亲手杀死妻儿。清醒后，神谕命他为国王欧律斯透斯服役赎罪：杀涅墨亚狮子、斩九头蛇许德拉、清扫积粪三十年的牛圈……本来只有十项，两项被判不算数，又补了两项，才凑成著名的“十二功绩”。英雄的伟业，一开始是一场赎罪。", en: "Driven mad by the goddess Hera, Heracles killed his own wife and children. When he came to himself, the oracle bound him to serve King Eurystheus in penance: kill the Nemean lion, slay the many-headed Hydra, clean stables fouled for thirty years. There were ten labours at first; two were disallowed, and two more were added — making the famous twelve. The hero's glory began as an act of atonement." },
  { id: "tales:033", zh: "奥德修斯和同伴被独眼巨人波吕斐摩斯关进山洞，每天被吃掉两个。奥德修斯灌醉巨人，问姓名时答“我叫没有人”，然后用烧红的木桩刺瞎他。巨人惨叫“没有人打我”，邻居们便散了。可逃出后奥德修斯忍不住报出真名炫耀，招来海神波塞冬十年的追杀——赢了一局的人，输在最后那句话上。", en: "Odysseus and his men were trapped in the cave of the one-eyed giant Polyphemus, who ate two of them daily. Odysseus got him drunk — having given his name as 'Nobody' — and drove a fire-hardened stake into the single eye. The giant roared 'Nobody is attacking me', and the neighbours went home. But once safely at sea, Odysseus could not resist shouting his real name in triumph — and earned Poseidon's ten-year persecution. He won the contest and lost it on one sentence." },
  { id: "tales:034", zh: "围城十年不下，希腊人留下一匹巨木马后假装撤军。特洛伊人把“战利品”拖进城，祭司拉奥孔警告说“我怕希腊人，哪怕他们带着礼物”，无人听从。当夜，藏在马腹里的士兵爬出，打开了城门。木马计最广为人知的版本出自罗马诗人维吉尔的《埃涅阿斯纪》——战胜者的故事，最后是战败者的诗人写得最动人。", en: "After ten years of failed siege, the Greeks left a giant wooden horse and pretended to sail away. The Trojans dragged the 'trophy' inside, ignoring the priest Laocoön's warning — 'I fear the Greeks, even bearing gifts.' That night the soldiers hidden in its belly climbed out and opened the gates. The fullest telling of the horse comes from Virgil's Aeneid, a Roman epic: the victors' trick was written most movingly by a poet of the wider, sadder story." },
  { id: "tales:035", zh: "阿喀琉斯是特洛伊战争中最强的希腊战士。相传他母亲忒提斯曾提着他的脚踵把他浸入冥河，浑身刀枪不入，唯独被捏住的脚踵没沾到水。后来他果然死于脚踵中箭。有趣的是，这个细节在荷马史诗里根本没有，是罗马时代的诗人补写的——最有名的弱点，是后人替英雄补上的。", en: "Achilles was the greatest Greek warrior of the Trojan War. The story goes that his mother Thetis dipped him in the River Styx, holding him by the heel: his body became invulnerable except for that one dry spot, and in time an arrow found it. Curiously, Homer never mentions any of this — the heel was added by Roman-era poets centuries later. The most famous weakness in literature was an editor's addition." },
  { id: "tales:036", zh: "女神厄科爱上了美少年纳西索斯，却受过诅咒，只能重复别人说的最后几个字，永远无法先开口。纳西索斯拒绝了她，她在山林里憔悴得只剩声音。而他俯身喝水时爱上了自己的倒影，枯守在水边，化作一朵水仙。“自恋”和“回声”这两个词，希腊人用一个故事一起解释了。", en: "The nymph Echo loved the beautiful youth Narcissus, but a curse left her able only to repeat the last words others spoke — never to speak first. Rejected, she wasted away in the hills until only her voice remained. Narcissus, bending to drink, fell in love with his own reflection, pined beside the pool, and became a narcissus flower. The Greeks explained both 'narcissism' and 'echo' in a single story." },
  { id: "tales:037", zh: "不和女神厄里斯没被邀请参加婚宴，一怒之下往宴席扔了一只金苹果，上面写着“献给最美者”。赫拉、雅典娜、阿佛洛狄忒三位女神争执不下，让特洛伊王子帕里斯裁决；阿佛洛狄忒许他世上最美的女人，他便把苹果判给了她。最美的女人是已婚的海伦——一场十年战争的导火索，是一只没写收件人的苹果。", en: "Eris, goddess of discord, was not invited to a wedding feast, so she tossed in a golden apple inscribed 'for the fairest'. Hera, Athena and Aphrodite all claimed it, and the Trojan prince Paris was made judge; Aphrodite promised him the world's most beautiful woman, and he awarded her the apple. The most beautiful woman, Helen, was already married — the fuse of a ten-year war was an apple addressed to no one." },
  { id: "tales:038", zh: "塞浦路斯雕刻家皮格马利翁厌恶现实中的女性，转而用象牙雕出一尊理想的女子，却爱上了自己的作品，日日对她说话、送她礼物。爱神被他的痴情感动，让雕像有了体温。在奥维德的原著里，这尊雕像根本没有名字——“伽拉忒亚”是后世添的。皮格马利翁效应后来被心理学借走：期待真的能改变一个人。", en: "Pygmalion, a Cypriot sculptor disgusted with real women, carved his ideal in ivory — then fell in love with his own work, speaking to it and bringing it gifts daily. The goddess of love, moved, warmed the statue into life. In Ovid's original the statue has no name at all; 'Galatea' was added by later ages. Psychology later borrowed the tale's name: the Pygmalion effect — expectation really can change a person." },
  { id: "tales:039", zh: "凡人少女普绪刻美貌惊人，爱神厄洛斯却奉命来惩罚她，反而爱上了她。他每夜在黑暗中来访，只提一个条件：不许看他的脸。普绪刻受姐妹挑唆，夜里举灯偷看，灯油滴在他肩上，爱神惊飞。她历尽万难寻回他，终成神侣。这个童话般的故事出自公元 2 世纪罗马小说《金驴记》，是“美女与野兽”最古老的祖先之一。", en: "The mortal girl Psyche was so beautiful that Eros, sent to punish her, fell in love with her instead. He visited only in darkness, on one condition: she must never look at his face. Egged on by her sisters, she lit a lamp one night; a drop of oil woke him, and he fled. After long trials she won him back, and they were wed among the gods. This fairy-tale-like story survives in Apuleius's 2nd-century Roman novel The Golden Ass — one of the oldest ancestors of Beauty and the Beast." },
  { id: "tales:040", zh: "吕底亚织女阿拉克涅手艺冠绝人间，狂言要与雅典娜比试。织机上她不仅织得无可挑剔，还把诸神的荒唐事一桩桩织进图案。雅典娜找不出针法上的错，却容不下这份揭短，把织品撕碎；阿拉克涅羞愤自尽，又被变成蜘蛛，世世代代织网。蜘蛛的学名 Arachnida，就来自她的名字。", en: "Arachne, a Lydian weaver of unmatched skill, boasted she would challenge Athena herself. At the loom her work was flawless — and she wove into it, scene by scene, the scandals of the gods. Athena could find no fault in the stitching, but she could not forgive the exposure; she tore the tapestry, and Arachne hanged herself in shame — then was turned into a spider, to weave forever. The scientific name for spiders, Arachnida, still carries hers." },
  { id: "tales:041", zh: "代达罗斯是古希腊最有名的工匠：克里特王请他设计一座关牛头怪的迷宫，复杂到连他自己走进去都几乎出不来。迷宫建成后，国王怕泄密，把代达罗斯父子也软禁在岛上。于是他抬头看天，开始收集羽毛——造迷宫的人，最后靠一双蜡翅膀逃出了自己参与的世界。伊卡洛斯的故事，要从父亲这里讲起。", en: "Daedalus was the most famous craftsman of Greek myth: the King of Crete commissioned him to design a labyrinth for the Minotaur, so intricate that even its maker could barely find the way out. Once it was built, the king, fearing the secret would spread, kept Daedalus and his son prisoner on the island. So the craftsman looked up at the sky and began gathering feathers — the man who built the maze escaped the world he helped create on wings of wax. Icarus's story begins with his father." },
  { id: "tales:042", zh: "狮身人面的斯芬克斯蹲在忒拜城外，向过路人出同一道谜语：什么动物早上四条腿，中午两条腿，晚上三条腿？答不出的人都被它吃掉。俄狄浦斯答出：是人——幼时爬行，壮年直立，老年拄杖。斯芬克斯羞愤坠崖。可解开了“人”之谜的他，偏偏不知道自己是谁：那才是这个故事真正的谜底。", en: "The Sphinx crouched outside Thebes with one riddle for every traveller: what creature walks on four legs in the morning, two at noon, and three in the evening? Those who failed were eaten. Oedipus answered: man — crawling in infancy, upright in his prime, leaning on a staff in old age. The Sphinx threw herself from the rock in shame. Yet the man who solved the riddle of 'man' did not know who he himself was — and that is the story's real riddle." },
  { id: "tales:043", zh: "众神之父奥丁为了喝一口密弥尔智慧之泉的水，把一只眼睛抵押给了泉眼，永远留在水里。从此他一只眼看世界，另一只眼看真理。北欧神话里智慧从不免费：最高的神，也要拿身体的一部分去换。", en: "Odin, father of the gods, pledged one of his eyes for a single drink from Mimir's well of wisdom; the eye stays in the water still. Ever after, one eye sees the world and the other sees truth. In Norse myth wisdom is never free — even the highest god pays for it with a piece of himself." },
  { id: "tales:044", zh: "托尔的锤子妙尔尼尔是矮人兄弟锻造的，只是锤柄比设计短了一截——锻造时洛基变成牛蝇，狠狠叮了拉风箱的矮人一口，风箱停了一瞬。短柄单手可握，反倒成了它的标志。这把锤子能召雷、碎山，扔出去还会自己飞回主人手里。", en: "Thor's hammer Mjölnir was forged by dwarf brothers, but its handle came out shorter than designed: mid-forging, Loki turned into a horsefly and bit the dwarf working the bellows, and the blast faltered for one moment. The short, one-handed grip became its signature. The hammer calls lightning, shatters mountains, and flies back to its master's hand when thrown." },
  { id: "tales:045", zh: "洛基是北欧神话里最难归类的一位：他是奥丁的结拜兄弟，诸神的麻烦大多是他惹的，也大多靠他摆平。他变成过母马，生下奥丁的八足神驹；变成苍蝇、老妇、鲑鱼，无所不变。诸神黄昏时，他将站在诸神的对面——这个亦正亦邪的角色，是北欧神话的变数本身。", en: "Loki is the hardest figure in Norse myth to classify: Odin's blood-brother, author of most of the gods' troubles and solver of most of them too. He has been a mare — and mothered Odin's eight-legged horse Sleipnir — a fly, an old woman, a salmon. At Ragnarök he will stand against the gods. Neither hero nor villain, he is the wild card the whole mythology deals around." },
  { id: "tales:046", zh: "诸神黄昏是北欧神话的终局：巨狼吞日，巨蛇出海，火焰巨人焚天，奥丁、托尔等主神大多战死。但预言说得很清楚——诸神早就知道结局，也知道打不过，还是整装出战。世界沉入海底后又会重新升起，幸存的神与一对男女将开始新的世界。知道结局而战，是北欧神话最悲壮的底色。", en: "Ragnarök is the end written into Norse myth: the wolf swallows the sun, the world-serpent rises from the sea, fire giants burn the sky, and Odin, Thor and most of the great gods die in battle. The prophecy is explicit — the gods know the outcome in advance, know they cannot win, and march out anyway. Afterwards the world rises green from the sea, and surviving gods and one human couple begin it again. To fight knowing the ending is the Norse world's most tragic colour." },
  { id: "tales:047", zh: "世界树尤克特拉希尔是一棵巨大的梣树，树冠撑起天空，树根扎进三个世界：神、人与冥界。树顶栖着一只鹰，树根处盘着巨龙，一只松鼠在两者之间上下奔跑，传话挑唆。诸神每天在树下议事，命运三女神则为它浇水续命——整个宇宙，在北欧人眼里是一棵需要照顾的树。", en: "Yggdrasil, the World Tree, is an immense ash whose crown holds up the sky and whose roots reach into the worlds of gods, humans and the dead. An eagle sits at the top, a dragon gnaws at the root, and a squirrel, Ratatoskr, runs between them carrying insults. The gods hold council beneath it daily, and the Norns water its roots to keep it alive — the whole universe, in the Norse imagination, is a tree that needs tending." },
  { id: "tales:048", zh: "彩虹桥比弗罗斯特连接人间与神国阿斯加德，桥头站着守望者海姆达尔：他的耳力能听见草木生长，目力能望出百里之外，怀里抱着末日才吹响的号角。平时，彩虹是雨后最温柔的风景；在这个神话里，它却是一座需要重兵把守的军事通道——美与戒备，只隔一层神话。", en: "The rainbow bridge Bifröst joins the human world to Asgard, home of the gods, and at its head stands the watchman Heimdall — his ears can hear grass grow, his eyes see a hundred miles, and in his arms rests the horn he will blow only at the end of the world. A rainbow is usually the gentlest sight after rain; in this mythology it is a military road under heavy guard. Beauty and vigilance are one myth apart." },
  { id: "tales:049", zh: "巨狼芬里尔是洛基之子，诸神怕它长大为祸，用尽锁链都被挣断。最后矮人用不存在的东西——猫的脚步声、鱼的呼吸、山的根之类——锻出一条柔软如丝带的魔链。芬里尔要求一位神把手放进它嘴里作保，战神提尔照做了；链子果然挣不断，提尔也永远失去了那只手。预言说，诸神黄昏时它终将挣脱。", en: "Fenrir, the monstrous wolf and son of Loki, was feared by the gods as he grew; every chain they tried, he snapped. At last the dwarves forged Gleipnir, a ribbon soft as silk, from things that do not exist — a cat's footfall, a fish's breath, a mountain's roots. Fenrir demanded a god's hand in his mouth as pledge; Tyr, god of war, gave his. The chain held, and Tyr lost the hand forever. Prophecy says that at Ragnarök the wolf will break free at last." },
  { id: "tales:050", zh: "奥丁肩头停着两只渡鸦：胡金是“思想”，穆宁是“记忆”。每天清晨它们飞遍世界，傍晚回来把见闻说给他听。奥丁在诗里坦白：他担心胡金不归，更担心穆宁——思想丢了还能再想，记忆丢了，人就不再是自己。北欧最有权势的神，最怕的是遗忘。", en: "Two ravens ride on Odin's shoulders: Huginn, Thought, and Muninn, Memory. Each dawn they fly over the whole world and return at evening to report what they have seen. In the old poem Odin admits he fears for Huginn — but fears more for Muninn. Thoughts can be thought again; memory, once lost, takes the self with it. The most powerful god of the North is afraid, above all, of forgetting." },
  { id: "tales:051", zh: "战死的勇士会被女武神瓦尔基里选入英灵殿：白天在旷野上厮杀练兵，死了当场复活，晚上大碗喝酒、大块吃肉，伤口自动愈合。这不是享福的天堂，而是一座军营——他们日日操练，是为了在诸神黄昏那天，与奥丁并肩打最后一仗。", en: "Warriors who die bravely in battle are chosen by the Valkyries for Valhalla: by day they fight on the plain and the slain rise again by evening; by night they feast on endless pork and mead, wounds healed. It is not a paradise of rest but a barracks — they drill daily for one purpose: to stand beside Odin in the last battle at Ragnarök." },
  { id: "tales:052", zh: "光明之神巴德尔梦见自己的死期。母亲弗丽嘉让世间万物立誓不伤他，唯独漏了一株不起眼的槲寄生。诸神以投掷武器打他取乐，百发不中；洛基却把槲寄生交给巴德尔的盲弟霍德尔，扶着他的手掷出——巴德尔应声而倒。北欧神话的末日，就是从这一株被漏掉的小植物开始倒计时的。", en: "Baldr, god of light, dreamed of his own death. His mother Frigg exacted an oath from all things not to harm him — all but one insignificant plant, the mistletoe. The gods made a game of hurling weapons at him, harmlessly, until Loki put a mistletoe dart in the hand of Baldr's blind brother Höðr and guided his aim. Baldr fell. The countdown to the Norse apocalypse starts with one small plant that was overlooked." },
  { id: "tales:053", zh: "吉尔伽美什是乌鲁克的王，为逃避死亡远行求索，在世界尽头找到经历大洪水而永生的乌特纳皮什提姆。洪水的故事比《圣经》里的诺亚方舟早得多。这部楔形文字史诗写于三千多年前，是人类现存最古老的伟大叙事之一——它问的问题，和今天一样：人能不能不死？", en: "Gilgamesh, king of Uruk, journeyed to the end of the world fleeing death, and found Utnapishtim, who survived a great flood and was granted eternal life. That flood story is far older than Noah's ark. This cuneiform epic, composed over three thousand years ago, is among the oldest great narratives humanity still possesses — and its question is the modern one: can a human being not die?" },
  { id: "tales:054", zh: "埃及冥神奥西里斯被弟弟塞特谋杀，尸身被切成碎块撒遍埃及。他的妻子伊西斯化作飞鸟，走遍各地把碎片一一寻回，拼合包裹成第一具木乃伊，用魔法让他短暂复活，并生下儿子荷鲁斯。奥西里斯从此为冥界之王。这个神话解释了木乃伊的由来：死亡不是终点，是需要被精心保管的开始。", en: "Osiris was murdered by his brother Set, who cut his body into pieces and scattered them across Egypt. His wife Isis flew over the land as a bird, gathered every fragment, bound them together into the first mummy, and by magic briefly revived him; their son was Horus. Osiris became king of the dead. The myth explains where mummies come from: death is not an end, but a beginning that must be carefully preserved." },
  { id: "tales:055", zh: "古埃及人相信，太阳神拉每天乘船航行：白天划过天空，给人间光；夜里船驶入冥界，他要与巨蛇阿佩普搏斗，赢了，第二天太阳才能重新升起。日出因此不是天象，是每晚一场胜负未卜的战斗的结果——埃及人每天清晨，都在庆祝神昨晚又赢了。", en: "The ancient Egyptians believed the sun god Ra travelled by boat: by day he sailed across the sky giving light; by night his barque entered the underworld, where he fought the giant serpent Apophis — and only if he won would the sun rise again. Sunrise was therefore not a weather event but the outcome of a nightly battle whose result was never guaranteed. Every Egyptian dawn was a celebration that the god had won again." },
  { id: "tales:056", zh: "罗摩是阿逾陀的王子，因宫廷之争被流放森林十四年。魔王罗波那劫走了他的妻子悉多，罗摩在神猴哈奴曼和猴军的帮助下，率大军跨海攻入楞伽岛，决战救回妻子。这部《罗摩衍那》与《摩诃婆罗多》并称印度两大史诗；罗摩的流放与归来，至今是印度人衡量忠诚与正义的标尺。", en: "Rama, prince of Ayodhya, was exiled to the forest for fourteen years by a palace intrigue. When the demon king Ravana abducted his wife Sita, Rama — aided by the divine monkey Hanuman and his army — crossed the sea to Lanka and won her back in a great war. The Ramayana stands with the Mahabharata as India's two great epics; Rama's exile and return are still a measure of loyalty and righteousness for millions." },
  { id: "tales:057", zh: "太阳女神天照的弟弟素戔呜尊在天上大闹，天照一气之下躲进天岩户，把自己关在山洞里——世界顿时陷入黑暗。众神想出一计：在洞外跳起滑稽的舞蹈、哄堂大笑。天照好奇，探出头来，埋伏的神一把将她拉出，光明重返人间。日本最古老的神话里，拯救世界的不是武力，是一场让神发笑的表演。", en: "When her brother Susanoo rampaged through heaven, the sun goddess Amaterasu shut herself in fury inside the Heavenly Rock Cave — and the world went dark. The gods devised a trick: outside the cave they staged a ridiculous dance and roared with laughter. Curious, Amaterasu peeked out, and a waiting god pulled her into the open; light returned to the world. In Japan's oldest mythology, the world is saved not by force but by a performance that made a goddess laugh." },
  { id: "tales:058", zh: "老婆婆在河边洗衣，漂来一只大桃子，切开一看，里面坐着一个小男孩——桃太郎。他带着老婆婆做的黍米团子出发去鬼岛退治恶鬼，路上用团子收服了狗、猴子和雉鸡做伙伴。这个江户时代定型的故事，是日本国民级的童话：勇气之外，它还讲了一件事——伙伴是拿点心换来的，要舍得分享。", en: "An old woman washing clothes by the river found a giant peach floating down; cutting it open, she found a baby boy inside — Momotaro, the Peach Boy. Armed with the old woman's millet dumplings, he set out for Demon Island, and along the way won three companions — a dog, a monkey and a pheasant — by sharing the dumplings. Fixed in the Edo period, the tale is Japan's national fairy story: beyond courage, it teaches that companions are won by sharing what you have." },
  { id: "tales:059", zh: "安纳西是西非阿肯人传说里的蜘蛛，既是骗子也是英雄。他向天神买故事，代价是活捉三样没人敢碰的东西：毒蜂群、大蟒蛇和豹子——全靠小聪明一一办成。从此世间的故事都叫“安纳西的故事”。这些故事后来随被贩卖的非洲人渡过大西洋，在加勒比海扎了根。", en: "Anansi the spider, of the Akan people of West Africa, is both trickster and hero. He offered to buy the Sky God's stories, and the price was three captures no one dared attempt — a swarm of hornets, a great python, and a leopard — all taken by cleverness. Ever since, tales are called 'Anansi stories'. Carried across the Atlantic by enslaved Africans, they took root in the Caribbean." },
  { id: "tales:060", zh: "羽蛇神库库尔坎——在阿兹特克人那里叫克察尔科亚特尔——是中美洲最受敬奉的神之一，掌管风、知识与晨星。神话说，人类曾一度灭绝，他潜入冥界取回先民的骸骨，用自己的血浇灌，造出了新一代人类。在玛雅的奇琴伊察金字塔，春分时阳光会在台阶上打出一条蛇影，至今人们仍在那天聚集观看。", en: "The Feathered Serpent — Kukulkan to the Maya, Quetzalcoatl to the Aztecs — was among Mesoamerica's most revered gods, lord of wind, knowledge and the morning star. Myth says that when humanity had perished, he descended into the underworld, retrieved the bones of the dead, and sprinkled them with his own blood to make a new humankind. At Chichen Itza, the equinox sun still casts a serpent of light down the pyramid's stairway, and crowds still gather to watch." },
  { id: "tales:061", zh: "佩蕾是夏威夷的火山女神。传说她乘独木舟从远方来到夏威夷群岛，一岛一岛地挖掘火坑，前几个岛的火都被海水浇灭，最后在基拉韦厄火山安了家，住在哈雷茂茂火山口。夏威夷人至今敬她为创造与毁灭同体的大地之母——那里的火山不是灾难，是神还住在家里。", en: "Pele is Hawaii's goddess of volcanoes. The story says she came by canoe from a distant homeland and dug fire-pits island by island; the sea quenched the first ones, until she settled at Kilauea, in the crater of Halemaʻumaʻu. Hawaiians honour her still as the earth-mother in whom creation and destruction are one — there, a volcano is not a disaster but a god still living at home." },
  { id: "tales:062", zh: "毛利神话里的半神毛伊是个爱闯祸的机灵鬼。他用祖母的颚骨做成鱼钩，乘独木舟出海，从海底钓起一条巨鱼——那条鱼就是新西兰的北岛，毛利语里北岛的名字直译就是“毛伊的鱼”。南岛则是他的独木舟。一个国家的地图，在神话里是一次钓鱼的战利品。", en: "Māui, the demigod of Māori myth, was a clever troublemaker. With a fishhook made from his grandmother's jawbone, he paddled out and hauled up a colossal fish from the ocean floor — that fish is New Zealand's North Island, whose Māori name literally means 'the fish of Māui'. The South Island is his canoe. In myth, a whole country's map is the catch of one fishing trip." },
  { id: "tales:063", zh: "塞德娜是因纽特人敬奉的海洋女神。流传最广的版本里，她不愿嫁人，被父亲强行带走，又在风浪中被父亲推下船；她扒住船舷，手指被一节节砍断，落入海中变成海豹、鲸鱼和海象，她自己则沉入海底，成为所有海兽的主人。猎人因此对她格外敬畏——在因纽特人那里，海洋的丰饶握在一双受伤的手里。", en: "Sedna is the sea goddess of the Inuit. In the most widely told version she refused every suitor; her father carried her off, and in a storm at sea cast her from the boat. Clinging to the gunwale, she lost her fingers joint by joint; as they fell into the water they became the seals, whales and walruses, and she sank to the sea floor as mistress of all sea beasts. Hunters therefore treat her with special reverence — in the Inuit world, the ocean's bounty rests in a pair of wounded hands." },
  { id: "tales:064", zh: "苏美尔女神伊南娜是天之女王，却执意要下到姐姐统治的冥界去。她穿过七道门，每过一道门就被摘去一件衣饰与宝物，最后赤身站在冥界女王面前，被判死刑，尸体挂在钉子上。三天后，她被救活重返大地，但冥界的规矩是：出去的人，必须留一个替身。这个写于近四千年前的故事，是人类最早记录下来的“下冥界”母题之一。", en: "Inanna, Sumerian queen of heaven, insisted on descending into the underworld ruled by her sister. She passed seven gates, and at each one a piece of her regalia was taken, until she stood naked before the queen of the dead, was judged, killed, and hung on a hook. Three days later she was revived and returned to the earth — but the underworld's rule stood: whoever leaves must send a substitute. Written down nearly four thousand years ago, it is among humanity's earliest recorded descents to the land of the dead." },
  { id: "tales:065", zh: "灰姑娘最早的文字记载不在欧洲，在中国：唐代段成式的《酉阳杂俎》里，洞主之女叶限受继母虐待，靠一条金色鱼骨的庇佑穿上华服去参加洞节，仓皇间丢了一只金鞋。岛王凭鞋寻人，娶她为妻。这比法国佩罗的《灰姑娘》早了八百多年——灰姑娘的故乡，可能是岭南的山洞之间。", en: "The earliest written Cinderella is not European but Chinese. In the Tang-dynasty miscellany Youyang Zazu, Ye Xian, a cave-chieftain's daughter abused by her stepmother, is aided by the bone of a golden fish: dressed in finery she attends a festival, flees, and loses one golden shoe. The island king searches by the shoe and marries her. This predates Perrault's French Cinderella by more than eight hundred years — the tale's first home may be the caves of the far south.", full: "唐时岭南一带，吴洞主的女儿叶限自幼丧母，继母对她百般刻薄，让她上高山砍柴、到深潭打水。一天，叶限在水边捉到一条小鱼，金目赤鳍，她偷偷养在盆里，鱼一天天长大，只好放进门前的池塘。继母知道后，骗走叶限，把鱼钓出来杀掉吃了。\n\n叶限哭着回来，一位披发人从天而降，告诉她：鱼骨还埋在粪堆里，把它收好，想要什么就向它祷告。果然，从此叶限要衣得衣、要食得食。\n\n洞节那天，继母带着亲生女儿去赶集，留叶限看家。叶限靠鱼骨变出一身翠羽衣和一双金鞋，悄悄去了集市，却被继母和妹妹认出，慌忙逃走，跑掉了一只金鞋。金鞋辗转卖到陀汗国，国王让全国女子试穿，无人合脚，最后寻到叶限——鞋穿在她脚上正好。国王带着叶限和鱼骨回到王宫，娶她为妻。至于那位继母和她的女儿，故事说，她们被飞石击中而死，葬在“懊女冢”里。", fullEn: "In Tang-dynasty Lingnan, Ye Xian, daughter of the cave-chieftain Wu, lost her mother young and was cruelly treated by her stepmother, sent to cut firewood on high hills and fetch water from deep pools. One day she caught a small fish with golden eyes and red fins and raised it in secret; as it outgrew its basin she moved it to the pond before the house. The stepmother found out, lured the fish out, killed and ate it.\n\nWeeping, Ye Xian was visited by a figure with unbound hair who descended from the sky: the fish's bones lay buried under the dung heap — keep them, he said, and pray to them for whatever you need. It was true: from then on, clothes and food came at her asking.\n\nOn the day of the cave festival, the stepmother took her own daughter and left Ye Xian to guard the house. By the bone's power Ye Xian dressed in a kingfisher-feather gown and golden shoes and slipped into the festival — but her stepmother half-recognised her, and fleeing, she lost one golden shoe. The shoe was sold from hand to hand until it reached the king of Tuohan, who ordered every woman in his realm to try it. None fit — until Ye Xian was found, and the shoe was exactly hers. She came before the king in her feather gown, carrying the fish bone, and he made her his queen. The stepmother and her daughter, the story says, were struck dead by flying stones, and buried in the Tomb of the Regretful Women." },
  { id: "tales:066", zh: "安徒生的《海的女儿》原版结局，并没有婚礼：小人鱼用声音换来双腿，王子却娶了别人。黎明时分，她只要用刀刺入王子的心，就能变回人鱼——她选择把刀扔进大海，自己化作泡沫。但安徒生给了她第二重结局：她成了“空气的女儿”，用三百年的善行，为自己挣一个不灭的灵魂。", en: "Andersen's The Little Mermaid does not end with a wedding. The mermaid traded her voice for legs, but the prince married another. At dawn she could have become a mermaid again by driving a knife into his heart; instead she threw the knife into the sea and dissolved into foam. But Andersen granted her a second ending: she became a daughter of the air, who by three hundred years of good deeds might earn an immortal soul.", full: "海底最深处住着海王一家，他最小的女儿最爱听祖母讲海面上的世界：船只、城市，和人类的灵魂——人鱼没有不灭的灵魂，死后只会化作海上的泡沫。十五岁那年，她第一次浮出水面，遇上风暴中落水的王子，把他救到岸边，自己躲了起来。\n\n为了再见到王子、也为了一个不灭的灵魂，她去找海巫婆。巫婆给了她一瓶药：喝下去，鱼尾会变成双腿，但每走一步都像踩在刀尖上；代价是她最动听的声音——巫婆割下了她的舌头。巫婆还说：如果王子娶了别人，在他婚礼后的第一个黎明，她的心就会碎裂，化作海上的泡沫。\n\n王子很宠爱这个不会说话的姑娘，却把救命恩人认成了邻国的公主，最后与公主成婚。婚礼之夜，小人鱼的姐姐们浮出海面，长发全都剪去——她们用头发向巫婆换来一把匕首：天亮前把匕首刺进王子的心，用他的血洒在脚上，她就能变回人鱼，再活三百年。\n\n小人鱼掀开帐子，看着熟睡的王子，把匕首扔进了大海。太阳升起时，她的身体化作泡沫——但她没有消失。她感到自己升入空中，成了空气的女儿。空气的精灵告诉她：用三百年的善行，她可以为自己挣得一个不灭的灵魂。她吻了吻新娘的额头，向王子微笑，飞向云端。", fullEn: "In the deepest sea lived the Sea King and his family. His youngest daughter loved her grandmother's tales of the world above — ships, cities, and the immortal souls of humans; mermaids have no such soul, and at death become mere sea foam. On her fifteenth birthday she rose to the surface for the first time and saved a drowning prince from a shipwreck, leaving him on the shore before anyone saw her.\n\nTo see the prince again — and to win an immortal soul — she went to the sea witch. The potion would turn her tail into legs, but every step would feel like walking on knives; the price was her beautiful voice, and the witch cut out her tongue. And if the prince married another, the witch warned, at dawn after his wedding night her heart would break and she would become foam.\n\nThe prince cherished the silent girl, but believed a neighbouring princess had saved him, and married her. On the wedding night the mermaid's sisters rose from the sea with their long hair shorn away — traded to the witch for a knife. Before sunrise she must drive it into the prince's heart and let his blood fall on her feet; then she would be a mermaid again, and live her three hundred years.\n\nThe little mermaid drew the curtain, looked at the sleeping prince — and threw the knife into the sea. As the sun rose, her body dissolved into foam. But she did not vanish: she felt herself rising into the air, a daughter of the air. The spirits told her that by three hundred years of good deeds she might earn an immortal soul of her own. She kissed the bride's forehead, smiled at the prince, and floated up toward the clouds." },
  { id: "tales:067", zh: "小红帽最早的书面版本出自 1697 年法国佩罗的笔下，结局冷峻得多：狼吃掉外婆，又吃掉小红帽，没有猎人赶来剖开狼肚——故事到此为止，后面只缀着一首打油诗，告诫年轻姑娘提防“温柔的狼”。剖狼救人的圆满结局，是格林兄弟一百多年后才添上的安慰。", en: "The earliest written Little Red Riding Hood, Perrault's of 1697, ends far more coldly: the wolf eats the grandmother, then eats the girl, and no huntsman comes to cut anyone out — the story simply stops, followed by a rhymed moral warning young girls about 'gentle wolves'. The rescuing huntsman and the happy ending were comfort added by the Brothers Grimm more than a century later." },
  { id: "tales:068", zh: "格林兄弟的《白雪公主》初版于 1812 年：那个嫉妒她美貌、一心要她命的王后，原本是她的亲生母亲。到了后来的版本，格林兄弟把母亲改成了继母，故事才不那么骇人。连醒来的方式也改过——初版里她是被仆人不耐烦地一拍后背，吐出了喉咙里的毒苹果。童话的残酷，是被一代代编辑慢慢磨钝的。", en: "In the Grimms' first edition of Snow White, in 1812, the queen who envied her beauty and wanted her dead was her own mother. In later editions the brothers quietly changed her into a stepmother, and the story grew less shocking. Even the waking was revised — in the first version a servant's impatient thump on the back dislodged the poisoned apple from her throat. The cruelty of fairy tales was blunted slowly, edition by edition." },
  { id: "tales:069", zh: "佩罗 1697 年的《睡美人》其实有整整后半部，现代改编大多删掉了：公主醒来、结婚生子之后，王子那位食人魔出身的母亲企图把孙辈和儿媳一一烹食。也就是说，童话里“从此幸福快乐”之后，还藏着同样长的一段惊险。睡美人睡了一百年，醒来要过的关，还不止一位巫婆。", en: "Perrault's Sleeping Beauty of 1697 has an entire second half that modern retellings usually cut: after the princess wakes, marries and has children, the prince's mother — of ogre descent — plots to have her grandchildren, and then the princess herself, cooked and eaten. After 'happily ever after' there hides an equally long adventure. The princess slept a hundred years; the witch was not the last danger she faced." },
  { id: "tales:070", zh: "青蛙王子的变身，靠的不是吻。在格林兄弟的版本里，公主嫌青蛙纠缠，抓起它狠狠摔向墙壁——青蛙落地，成了英俊的王子。吻是后来改编者的温柔加工。故事还有个常被删掉的尾巴：王子的忠仆“铁汉斯”因主人变青蛙而心痛，给心套上三道铁箍；主人复原那天，铁箍在马车里一一迸裂。", en: "The Frog Prince was not transformed by a kiss. In the Grimms' version, the princess, disgusted by the frog's demands, seizes it and hurls it against the wall — and down falls a handsome prince. The kiss is a later reteller's softening. The tale also has a tail usually cut: the prince's servant, Faithful Henry, had bound his heart with three iron bands to keep it from breaking with grief; on the day his master is restored, the bands snap one by one in the carriage.", full: "从前有位小公主，最喜欢在宫殿旁的井边玩金球。一天金球掉进深井，她哭了起来。一只青蛙探出头说：我可以替你捞球，但你要答应让我做你的伙伴，同桌吃饭、同杯喝水、睡你的小床。公主满口答应，心想：一只青蛙，怎么可能爬进王宫？\n\n球捞上来了，公主拿起球跑回宫，头也不回。第二天用餐时，门外传来吧嗒吧嗒的脚步声，青蛙在门外喊：公主，你答应过我的。国王听完原委，严肃地说：答应过的事，就要做到。公主只好让青蛙爬上餐桌，用她的金盘子吃饭。\n\n饭后，青蛙说：现在抱我去你的房间，我要睡你的丝绸小床。公主嫌恶至极，一把抓起青蛙，狠狠摔在墙上：这下你该安静了，你这讨厌的青蛙！\n\n青蛙落地，却站起一位眼睛明亮的王子。他说，他被巫婆变成青蛙，只有这样才能解咒。第二天，王子的马车来接他们回国，车后站着忠仆亨利。路上只听咔嚓咔嚓几声响，王子问：是车坏了吗？亨利答：不是，是我心上的铁箍断了——您变青蛙时我怕心痛碎，给它套了三道铁箍；如今您回来了，我的心高兴得把它们一一撑断了。", fullEn: "Once there was a young princess whose favourite toy was a golden ball, played with beside a deep well near the palace. One day the ball fell in, and she wept. A frog poked its head out: I will fetch your ball, if you promise I may be your companion — eat at your table, drink from your cup, and sleep in your little bed. The princess promised everything, thinking: as if a frog could ever climb into a palace.\n\nThe ball was fetched; the princess snatched it and ran home without a backward glance. Next day at dinner came a splashing step on the stairs, and the frog's voice at the door: Princess, you promised. The king, hearing the story, said gravely: what you have promised, you must keep. So the frog climbed onto the table and ate from her golden plate.\n\nThen the frog said: now carry me to your room; I will sleep in your little silk bed. In disgust the princess seized it and hurled it against the wall: Now you'll be quiet, you hateful frog!\n\nThe frog fell — and stood up a prince with kind, bright eyes. A witch had turned him into a frog, he said, and only this could break the spell. Next morning his carriage came to carry them to his kingdom, with his servant Faithful Henry standing behind. On the road there came a crack, and another. Is the carriage breaking? asked the prince. No, my lord, said Henry — it is the iron bands around my heart. When you were a frog I bound my heart with three bands lest it break with grief; now that you are restored, my heart is bursting them with joy." },
  { id: "tales:071", zh: "《汉塞尔与格莱特》里，真正救命的是妹妹：哥哥用鹅卵石和面包屑两次做记号，到了女巫的糖果屋前，是格莱特假装不会生火，把女巫推进了灶炉。格林兄弟记录这个故事时，德国民间还留着饥荒的记忆——把孩子遗弃在森林里，在最坏的年景里，是真实发生过的绝望。糖果屋的甜，盖不住故事底下的饿。", en: "In Hansel and Gretel, it is the little sister who saves them: Hansel twice marks the path, with pebbles and then breadcrumbs, but at the witch's gingerbread house it is Gretel who pretends not to understand the oven and pushes the witch into it. When the Grimms recorded the tale, German folk memory still held real famines — abandoning children in the forest was a despair that had truly happened in the worst years. The sweetness of the candy house never quite covers the hunger underneath." },
  { id: "tales:072", zh: "杰克用一头奶牛换了几粒魔豆，豆茎一夜长到天上，他爬上云端，从巨人家里偷走下金蛋的母鸡和金竖琴，还砍断豆茎摔死了追来的巨人。这个英国故事的道德一直有点尴尬：巨人在自己家里，杰克才是闯入者。早期的版本索性给杰克安了个“替父报仇”的理由——故事的对错，是讲故事的人慢慢修出来的。", en: "Jack traded the family cow for a handful of magic beans; the stalk reached the sky overnight, and up in the clouds he stole the giant's hen that laid golden eggs and his golden harp, then chopped the stalk and let the pursuing giant fall to his death. The tale's morals have always been awkward: the giant was in his own home, and Jack was the intruder. Early versions simply gave Jack a motive — avenging his father. Right and wrong in a story get repaired by its tellers over time." },
  { id: "tales:073", zh: "拇指姑娘是安徒生原创的童话，不是民间故事。她从一朵郁金香里生出来，只有拇指大，先后被癞蛤蟆、鱼、田鼠和鼹鼠安排婚事——每个都觉得自己是她的良配。最后她救过的燕子带她飞往南方，落在花丛里，那里的花天使与她一般大小。这个故事温柔地讲了一件事：所有人替你选的“合适”，都不如自己飞去找的地方。", en: "Thumbelina is Andersen's own invention, not a folk tale. Born from a tulip and no bigger than a thumb, she is promised in marriage one after another by a toad, a fish, a field mouse and a mole — each certain he is the right match. At last the swallow she once saved carries her south to a land of flowers, where a flower-angel of her own size waits. The gentle tale says one thing: the 'suitable' matches everyone picks for you are worth less than the place you fly to find yourself." },
  { id: "tales:074", zh: "丑小鸭被鸭群啄、被鸡群笑，逃到野外挨过寒冬，第二年春天在湖面上看见自己的倒影，才发现自己是天鹅。这个故事常被看作安徒生本人的写照：他出身贫寒，长相笨拙，年轻时在哥本哈根四处碰壁。童话的安慰并不廉价——它没有说忍耐必然有回报，只说：你先得是天鹅。", en: "The ugly duckling is pecked by the ducks and mocked by the hens, flees, and barely survives the winter — then in spring sees his reflection and finds he is a swan. The tale is widely read as Andersen's self-portrait: poor-born, awkward, and rebuffed all over Copenhagen in his youth. Its comfort is not cheap: it does not promise that endurance is always rewarded — only that you must first be a swan." },
  { id: "tales:075", zh: "大年夜，卖火柴的小女孩不敢回家——一根火柴也没卖出去。她缩在墙角，一根根擦亮火柴取暖，在火光里看见烤鹅、圣诞树和去世的祖母，最后含着微笑冻死在街角。安徒生的母亲幼年时曾被送出门行乞，这个故事里有他自家的记忆。他写的不是童话的恶意，是成人世界的冷：满街灯火，没有一盏为她而亮。", en: "On New Year's Eve, the little match girl dares not go home — she has sold nothing. Huddled in a corner, she lights her matches one by one for warmth, seeing in their flames a roast goose, a Christmas tree, and her dead grandmother, and is found frozen at dawn, smiling. Andersen's own mother had been sent out to beg as a child; the tale holds his family's memory. Its cruelty is not a witch's — it is the cold of the adult world: a street full of lights, and not one of them lit for her." },
  { id: "tales:076", zh: "“皇帝的新装”不是安徒生凭空编的：它有个 14 世纪的西班牙原型，那里的骗子织的是“非婚生子看不见”的布，检验的是血统。安徒生把机关改成“愚蠢或不称职的人看不见”，矛头从出身转向了虚荣与官位。最后戳破谎言的，两个版本都选了孩子——有些话，只有不怕丢脸的人说得出口。", en: "The Emperor's New Clothes was not invented from nothing: it has a 14th-century Spanish ancestor in which the swindlers' cloth is invisible to the illegitimately born — a test of bloodline. Andersen changed the mechanism to 'invisible to the stupid and the unfit for office', turning the satire from birth to vanity and rank. In both versions, the one who speaks the truth is a child — some sentences can only be said by someone with no face to lose." },
  { id: "tales:077", zh: "阿拉丁的故事其实不在《一千零一夜》的阿拉伯古抄本里。它是 18 世纪初，叙利亚讲述者汉纳·迪亚卜在巴黎讲给法国译者加朗听的，加朗把它写进了法译本。更奇妙的是，这个最有名的“阿拉伯故事”，开场写的是中国：阿拉丁是个住在中国的懒少年——东方在东方人的故事里，也是异域。", en: "Aladdin is not actually in the old Arabic manuscripts of the Thousand and One Nights. In the early 1700s the Syrian storyteller Hanna Diyab told it in Paris to the French translator Antoine Galland, who wrote it into his translation. Stranger still, the most famous 'Arabian' tale opens in China: Aladdin is an idle boy living in a Chinese city — in a storyteller's Orient, the East itself could be the exotic elsewhere.", full: "中国的一座城市里，住着裁缝的儿子阿拉丁，父亲死后他整日游手好闲。一天，一个自称是他叔叔的北非魔法师找上门来，带他到城外山中，念咒打开一座地宫，让阿拉丁下去取一盏旧油灯——地宫里满是金银珠宝，魔法师却只要那盏灯。阿拉丁起了疑心，不肯先交灯，魔法师一怒之下封死了洞口。\n\n绝境中，阿拉丁无意间擦了魔法师给他的戒指，跳出一个巨神，救他回到地面。回家后母亲擦拭那盏捡回的油灯，灯里竟住着更强大的神仆：要宫殿有宫殿，要珍馐有珍馐。阿拉丁娶了公主，住进凭空而起的华宫，成了全国最体面的人。\n\n几年后，魔法师卷土重来，扮作小贩在宫外吆喝“新灯换旧灯”。公主不知道油灯的秘密，拿神灯换了新灯。魔法师一声令下，连宫殿带公主一起搬到了北非。阿拉丁靠戒指神仆追踪而至，趁魔法师醉酒将其杀死，夺回神灯。从此他学得谨慎，把神灯深藏——宝物招来的祸，往往比宝物本身更长久。", fullEn: "In a city of China lived Aladdin, a tailor's idle son. One day a North African magician, claiming to be his uncle, led him to a cave outside the city, opened it with spells, and sent him down for a single old lamp — though the cave glittered with treasure, the magician wanted only the lamp. Suspicious, Aladdin refused to hand it over first, and the furious magician sealed him inside.\n\nIn the dark, Aladdin chanced to rub the ring the magician had lent him; a great jinni burst out and carried him home. Then his mother, polishing the old lamp, released a mightier servant still: palaces, feasts, whatever was wished. Aladdin married the princess and lived in a palace raised overnight, the grandest man in the kingdom.\n\nYears later the magician returned, crying 'new lamps for old' beneath the palace windows. The princess, who knew nothing of the lamp's secret, traded it away. At the magician's command the palace — and the princess with it — vanished to North Africa. By the ring's jinni Aladdin followed, killed the magician in his drunken sleep, and took back the lamp. He kept it hidden ever after: the troubles a treasure brings usually outlast the treasure." },
  { id: "tales:078", zh: "《阿里巴巴和四十大盗》同样出自迪亚卜讲给加朗的故事，阿拉伯古抄本里也没有。但这个故事真正的主角不是阿里巴巴：是他家的女仆马尔贾娜——强盗在门上做记号，是她发现并抹去；强盗藏进油瓮，是她浇下滚油；最后舞宴上行刺，也是她反手了结。阿里巴巴的运气，全靠一个聪明人的警醒撑着。", en: "Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves also comes from Diyab's tales to Galland, absent from the old Arabic manuscripts. But the story's true hero is not Ali Baba — it is his servant Morgiana. She finds and erases the mark the thieves chalked on the door; she pours boiling oil into the jars where they hide; and at the final banquet it is she who turns the assassin's dance against him. Ali Baba's luck is held up entirely by one clever person's vigilance." },
  { id: "tales:079", zh: "水手辛巴达的七次航海，讲的其实是一个巴格达富翁的回忆录：他把历险讲给一个与他同名的穷挑夫听——你以为我生来富贵？我散尽家财、七次出海，每一次都差点把命留在岛上。鲸鱼背上的岛、巨鹰衔走的钻石谷、食人巨人……这些奇遇的底下，是阿拔斯王朝海上贸易的真实世界影子。", en: "Sinbad's seven voyages are framed as a rich Baghdad merchant's memoir, told to a poor porter who shares his name: you think I was born to wealth? I squandered my inheritance and sailed seven times, nearly leaving my life on an island each time. Islands on a whale's back, a valley of diamonds reached by eagles, man-eating giants — beneath the marvels lies the real maritime world of the Abbasid age." },
  { id: "tales:080", zh: "渔夫第四次撒网，捞起一只所罗门封印的铜瓶，瓶里冒出的魔鬼却说要杀他：魔鬼被关的最初几百年，他许愿让放他出来的人享尽财富；又过几百年，誓言熬成了报复——最后他发誓，谁放他出来，就赐谁一死。渔夫用一句话把他骗回瓶里：“我不信你这么大个子，能装进这只小瓶。”魔鬼为了证明，化作青烟钻了回去。弱者的武器，有时只是一句“我不信”。", en: "On his fourth cast, the fisherman hauled up a copper bottle sealed by Solomon — and the jinni who poured out announced he would kill him. In his first centuries shut in the bottle, the jinni had vowed endless riches to whoever freed him; as the centuries soured, the vow turned to vengeance — at last he swore to kill his rescuer. The fisherman talked him back into the bottle with one sentence: 'I don't believe a great creature like you could ever fit in that little flask.' To prove it, the jinni turned to smoke and slipped inside. The weapon of the weak is sometimes just the words 'I don't believe you'." },
];


